    {
     "seat": "fable5",
     "phase": "assess",
     "d": "OK",
     "date": "2026-08-29",
     "grade": "CONFIRMED",
     "ev": "re-verified at HEAD by the main loop against machine_pmax.c, mips_cpu_types.h, cpu_dyntrans.c and float_emul.c; reading only",
     "outcome": "WRONG-RECORD-CORRECTED",
     "note": "*** THE WITNESS FIELD SAID 'pmax OPEN'.  IT IS NOT OPEN, AND THIS ROW'S OWN PANEL CLOSED IT\nTWO WEEKS AGO. ***  Corrected in place 2026-08-29; the old text is quoted in the field.\n\n**Four seats settled pmax on 2026-08-15** -- fable5, opus5, grok and codex, the last two\nfinding the EXC3K scope limit independently.  The field kept saying OPEN anyway, which is the\ngap-closes-itself shape: the row went on carrying a question its panel had answered.\n\n**RE-VERIFIED AT HEAD BY READING, three ways plus a corroboration:**\n  1. `machine_pmax.c` defaults `cpu_name` to **R3000A**, else R3000, else R2000.\n  2. `mips_cpu_types.h` maps R2000 / R2000A / R3000 / R3000A **all to EXC3K**.\n  3. `cpu_dyntrans.c` carries, verbatim, `/*  Update the count register (on everything\n     except EXC3K):  */` followed by `if (cpu->cd.mips.cpu_type.exc_model != EXC3K) {`.\n     **So on pmax COUNT is never updated -- and a register that does not advance can never\n     reach COMPARE.**  The wrap cannot fire.\n  4. `float_emul.c` independently records EXC3K fall-through behaviour \"measured on pmax\",\n     which is a second, unrelated site agreeing about what EXC3K does on this machine.\n\n**WHAT CHANGES:** the row is LATENT rather than partly-unknown.  Live only on\nalgor/evbmips/vocore; dead on pmax by construction and unused on arc.  That is a RANKING\nfact, and it moves the row DOWN relative to anything reachable on a rig this project boots.\n\n**WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE:** the defect is real and unambiguous -- eleven seats said so -- and a\nwitness still needs a build.  *This corrects the reachability record only; it does not close\nthe row and does not touch the code.*"
    }
   ],
   "witness": "*** CORRECTED 2026-08-29 (L4).  THIS FIELD SAID: \"none yet -- the code defect is verified by reading and is unambiguous, but which machines can reach it is only partly settled (arc confirmed dormant; \" -- and the 'pmax OPEN' half is REFUTED, by this row's OWN PANEL and by three independent readings of the code. ***  FOUR seats settled it on 2026-08-15: fable5 (\"Delivery is DEAD on pmax (EXC3K) and unused on arc; live only on algor/evbmips/vocore\"), opus5 (\"pmax is NOT a live path (EXC3K gate)\"), grok and codex (both found the EXC3K scope limit independently).  RE-VERIFIED AT HEAD: (1) machine_pmax.c defaults the cpu_name to R3000A, else R3000, else R2000; (2) mips_cpu_types.h maps R2000/R2000A/R3000/R3000A ALL to EXC3K; (3) cpu_dyntrans.c carries `/* Update the count register (on everything except EXC3K): */` followed by `if (... exc_model != EXC3K) {` -- so on pmax COUNT IS NEVER UPDATED, and a register that does not advance cannot reach COMPARE.  A fourth, independent corroboration sits in float_emul.c, which records EXC3K fall-through behaviour \"measured on pmax\".  So the reachability question is CLOSED, not open: none of the owner's MIPS rigs reaches this -- it is LATENT, live only on algor/evbmips/vocore.  The rest of the old field stands: the code defect is verified by reading and is unambiguous, and a witness would still need a build.",
   "state_note": "*** EVIDENCE 2026-08-27: THE TITLE'S PREMISE IS MIS-STATED AND THE UNDERLYING DEFECT IS REAL AND WORSE THAN EITHER THE TITLE OR THE SEAT DESCRIBED. It is a MISSING `else`. ***\n\nThe title blames *wrap-around*. A farmed seat checked that premise -- as the brief asked -- and refuted it: ordinary periodic wrap is handled. **But it confirmed the mechanism, and reading the code shows a sharper defect than the one it named.**\n\n`src/cpus/cpu_mips_coproc.c:796-819`:\n\n```c\nint32_t compare_diff = tmp - cp->reg[COP0_COMPARE];\ndouble hz;\n\nif (compare_diff < 0)\n        hz = tmp - cp->reg[COP0_COUNT];        /* (A) */\n\nif (compare_diff == 0)                         /* SEPARATE statement */\n        hz = 0;                                /* (B) */\nelse\n        hz = (double)emulated_hz / (double)compare_diff;   /* (C) */\n```\n\n**(A) IS AN UNCONDITIONALLY DEAD STORE.** The `if/else` at (B)/(C) is a separate statement, and both its arms assign `hz` -- so whatever (A) wrote is always overwritten. When `compare_diff < 0`, (C) runs and yields a **NEGATIVE** `hz`; `if (hz > 0)` at `:812` is then false and **the timer is neither created nor updated**.\n\n**The three cases, exhaustively:**\n  * `compare_diff > 0` -- (C) gives a positive rate; timer set. **The only working path.**\n  * `compare_diff == 0` -- (B) gives 0; `hz > 0` false; nothing set.\n  * `compare_diff < 0` -- (A) computed the intended value and (C) throws it away for a negative one; `hz > 0` false; nothing set.\n\n**Consequence, which is what the title was reaching for:** if the timer was never created it stays NULL, so **no timer interrupt is ever delivered again**; if it already existed it keeps its stale rate. Either way a guest write that ought to reprogram the timer silently does nothing.\n\n*** THE DEAD STORE IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING, because it shows INTENT. *** The author wrote handling for `compare_diff < 0` and it has never executed. This is not a design choice being second-guessed -- it is a chain of `if`s that lost its `else`, and (A) is the evidence.\n\n**REACHABILITY -- PARTLY SETTLED, and the unsettled half is recorded as unsettled rather than asserted.** The gate is `emulated_hz > 0` at `:797`; **21 assignments to `emulated_hz` exist across the tree.**\n  * **arc: DORMANT, verified here** -- `machine_arc.c` contains ZERO `emulated_hz` assignments, so the gate cannot pass.\n  * **pmax: OPEN.** It DOES set `emulated_hz` (`machine_pmax.c:96`, `:160`), so the `:797` gate passes. The seat argued pmax is nonetheless dormant because its default R3000 is `EXC3K`, citing `cpu_dyntrans.c` -- **a file not read here, so this is recorded as the seat's claim, not as verified.** Settle it before ranking this row.\n  * the seat named `algor` (RM5200/EXC4K, `machine_algor.c:49`) as directly reachable. Also unverified here.\n\n**Observable for a detector:** guest-visible timer interrupt delivery -- `Cause.IP7`. Broken: no assertion at all after a write with `compare_diff <= 0`, or delivery stuck at the stale cadence. Fixed: the newly programmed cadence.\n\n**Related:** see `dfreq`, whose closure text also concerns the MIPS compare write converting a distance-to-deadline into a frequency. **Check for overlap before opening a round on either** -- a queue id covering two defects is how one gets buried when the other closes.\n\n",
   "hold_question": "n1 MIPS Compare wrap-around. The reachability question this row was carrying is CLOSED as of 2026-08-29 (see the corrected witness field): pmax CANNOT reach it, because COUNT is not updated on EXC3K at all. QUESTION for the seat: given the defect is REAL, unambiguous by reading, and LATENT on every rig this project owns -- live only on algor/evbmips/vocore -- is this documented-only, or does a latent-but-unambiguous timer hang earn a fix on a machine nobody here boots? Note the fix is small and the failure mode is a guest whose timer never fires again, which is a hang rather than a wrong value."
  },
