   "id": "b120r",
   "title": "b120r  SCSI: the reported length can exceed what the guest asked for",
   "state": "held",
   "commits": "",
   "hold": "FILE - offer as a rider in b120u's first panel",
   "closure": {
    "by": "fable5",
    "badge": "",
    "note": "Split from b120. The reported length is set to the rounded-up value rather than the length the guest asked for, and one assignment is a dead store overwritten by the next line, so the descriptor advertises 44 bytes where 36 exist. Host-safe; guest-visible."
   },
   "entries": [
    {
     "phase": "assess",
     "seat": "opus5",
     "date": "2026-08-17",
     "verdict": "REAL",
     "evidence": "CONFIRMED",
     "note": "*** REFUTED THE AUTHOR'S OWN DUPLICATE SUSPICION, with the code. *** The shipped work (#281/#283/#286, #263) is DATA_OUT transfer counts in HBA registers; this is a DIFFERENT LAYER AND THE OPPOSITE DIRECTION -- length fields embedded in the DATA_IN payload, which no HBA clamps. MODE SENSE: `retlen += 100` (:589) sizes the buffer (:596), then :599 restores data_in_len but NOT retlen, so :607 `data_in[0] = retlen` reports asked+100. VERIFIED INDEPENDENTLY BY THE MAIN LOOP at those exact lines. A prior capture (_scratchpad/s1_asc.txt) shows a guest asking 32 and receiving 32 while the reported MODE DATA LENGTH is 132; allocation length 156 wraps the field to 0. Siblings: :371-374 and :957-960 force allocation length UP; :386-387 hardcodes INQUIRY additional-length via an unindented line overwriting the one above; MODE SENSE 10 writes the 6-byte header layout into a 10-byte-header response. HONEST UNKNOWN: the lie is certain, guest HARM is not -- pmax/arc tolerate it today, so one instrumented boot before committing to a fix.  [panel_20260817_174701]"
