     "note": "*** NAMED A MEASUREMENT NOBODY HAD RUN AND TOLD ME TO RUN IT BEFORE WRITING THE CHANGELOG SENTENCE: an fflush-ONLY (no fsync) arm. Its words: 'If that arm persists after _exit, the kill-and-check row is not an fsync test.' I RAN IT AND IT WAS RIGHT. *** MEASURED, one 512-byte write: fflush only -> 512 bytes DURABLE to a separate reader; fsync only (what ships today) -> 0. So _exit() simulates PROCESS death, not host death -- the page cache belongs to the KERNEL and outlives the process -- and a kill-and-check test therefore measures the missing FFLUSH, never the missing FSYNC. This SPLITS THE DEFECT: (a) missing fflush is byte-testable and bounded; (b) overlays never fsynced at all is NOT byte-testable by ANY process-kill test, needs the --wrap rows, and is unbounded on real power loss. It also placed the detector correctly (beside diff_diskimage_io.c, NOT on the #411 write-fail fixtures) and filed six things explicitly NOT to do this round.  [panel_20260817_215052]"
