     "note": "*** VERDICT: REAL, BUT DO NOT CLOSE AS WORDED. `4d0981d` completely fixes SYNCHRONIZE CACHE(10) / 0x35; SYNCHRONIZE CACHE(16) / 0x91 still returns guest-visible GOOD without flushing anything. *** All three phases answered separately, which is what this row's three held stages needed: ASSESS real, RESEARCH go-with-changes, REVIEW no-go for closure. VERIFIED BEFORE RECORDING: `diskimage_scsicmd.c` defines and handles only `SCSICMD_SYNCHRONIZE_CACHE` (0x35) -- there is no 0x91 case -- and `dev_asc.c:1284-1287` reads `if (d->xferp != NULL && d->xferp->status != NULL) write(status[0]); else write(0x00);`, so ABSENT STATUS BECOMES GOOD. A valid 16-byte 0x91 CDB passes the group-length check, falls to the unimplemented default, and the guest is told GOOD while stdio-buffered overlay and bitmap data can still be unflushed. IT ALSO CORRECTED THE ORIGINAL FILING'S PREMISE: the filing assumed the BASE file was already flushed (#134). It was not -- before `4d0981d` the handler called `fsync()` with no `fflush()`, so even the flat base's stdio buffer was unflushed. **The shipped commit is therefore BETTER than its own filing claimed**, repairing both defects. AND IT CLOSED THE 0x35 QUESTION PROPERLY rather than asserting it: no ordinary 0x35 path returns GOOD with dirty data pending -- a missing data handle fails; a missing bitmap handle is unreachable because `diskimage_add_overlay()` appends only after opening BOTH files; a read-only flat image cannot hold guest-written dirty data. DISPOSITION IT PROPOSES, and it is the honest one: either route 0x91 through the same helper, or explicitly NARROW `ovsync` to SYNCHRONIZE CACHE(10) and TRANSFER the 0x91 instance to `b120` -- after which `ovsync` may close. It also checked and found **no dedicated SC(16) row exists**, and that the pass-2 statement claiming the 0x91 sibling was filed is NOT explicit in `b120`'s record. That is a wrong record to fix, not a filing to assume. Coverage gap named too: the committed differential exercises only 0x35, and the later coverage commit gates ordering without adding a 0x91 case."
