    {
     "seat": "opus5",
     "phase": "assess",
     "d": "✓",
     "date": "2026-08-26",
     "ev": "Agent(model=opus) held-stage seat, batch A (harness rows). Read-only; GXEMUL-SEC only.",
     "note": "*** VERDICT: KEEP OPEN -- but the premise is REFUTED for most of the battery, and the highest-value action was ONE CHARACTER, now shipped. *** 'Nothing outside it notices' is FALSE for every block that is the sole invoker of a python detector: section R hard-fails, verified (every detector has exactly one invoker across all gates, so deleting the block leaves the detector run by nothing). It is TRUE for log-grading blocks, source-grep blocks, per-row name lists, and the whole of gate_ab / gate_build / gate_upstream -- 38 check sites with zero python detectors. *** THE MEASURED SLACK, AND IT IS FIXED IN THE SAME COMMIT AS THIS ENTRY: section U's ratchet floor was 16 and the real value is 18. *** The count went 16 -> 17 -> 18 across recent commits while the floor never moved, despite the block's own comment saying 'raise the floor when it rises'. **Two whole gate blocks carrying score-claim pins could have been deleted with the check still printing `good`.** Its argument for the fix is the one adopted: **a hand-maintained ratchet is itself an unratcheted number**, so it is now an EQUALITY pin on the GATE_MANIFEST idiom, negative-controlled in both directions (17 -> GREW, 19 -> DROPPED, 18 -> ok). It also measured that `_checks` has NO consumer outside lib.sh -- any nonzero count is a PASS -- and proposed a per-gate runtime floor in `gate_end()` placed AFTER the fails/degraded tests so a legitimately degraded run reports SKIP and is never falsely reddened, with a rig-free `selftest_checkfloor.sh` asserting three outcomes. It recorded honest UNKNOWN on the floor VALUES (they need one clean battery run) and stated plainly what such a floor CANNOT see: a block hollowed while keeping its call count, a check made vacuous in place, or a pin's literal quoted. **It cited this project's own measured instance of the first** -- a seat deleting whole SELFCHECK blocks from five detectors with the gate staying PASS at the same check count -- and correctly flagged it as evidence a count floor is NECESSARY BUT NOT SUFFICIENT rather than as support. BONUS L4 IT FOUND: check_probe_wiring.py's own docstring -- the file whose thesis is 'a fact-shaped string with nothing behind it' -- carries two stale citations (gate_hygiene.sh:331 for a note actually at :381; gate_sh_rounding.sh:185 for one at :690)."
    }
   ],
   "hold_question": "If a whole gate BLOCK is deleted, nothing outside it notices. QUESTION: does a floor check exist today that would catch it, and if not, what is the cheapest one that WOULD? Concretely: is there a pinned count of gate blocks or of `check` calls per gate anywhere, comparable to the way `check_probe_wiring.py` pins detectors-to-gates? Note `precommit_check.sh` section U already compares score claims against gate pins, so some of the machinery exists. If the honest answer is that this needs a new check, say what it should count and what its NEGATIVE CONTROL would be -- a check that cannot be shown to fail is worth nothing here."
  },
