     "note": "*** VERDICT: `armbdt` IS REAL -- and it REFUTED MY BRIEF'S CITATION PREMISE, which I had planted from the carrier. VERIFIED AGAINST THE LOCAL MANUAL LINE BY LINE BEFORE RECORDING. *** My brief said the controlling text is LDR's own Alignment note at p. A4-44. **That governs LDR's rotate-the-aligned-word rule and does NOT govern block transfers.** The controlling text is LDM(1) p. A4-37 and STM(1) p. A4-190. Read from `_scratchpad/ddi0100i.txt:7954`: *'For CP15_reg1_Ubit == 0, the Load Multiple instructions ignore the least significant two bits of the address... For CP15_reg1_Ubit == 1, all non-word aligned accesses cause an alignment fault.'* STM says the same at `:14115`. **This is a citation read from the local manual, which is the standard the carrier demands after #357/#362/#365 shipped ARM page numbers that came from panel output.** And the seat's own argument for why LDR cannot govern is the good one: if A4-44 applied, the fast path -- which aligns WITHOUT rotating -- would not be correct either. THE FINDING: with `U=0` and alignment checking off, the masked fast/warm path is RIGHT and the raw slow/cold path is WRONG (`memory_rw` uses the raw byte offset and copies four bytes from there, so a load can span two architectural words and an STM can overwrite part of the next). The generated optimised path confirms the intended shape independently: select the page from the raw address, mask the access offset with `addr &= 0xffc`, update the base register from the UNMASKED value. *** THE FIX SHAPE MATTERS AND IS NARROWER THAN IT LOOKS: mask ONLY the addresses passed to the two slow `memory_rw` calls. Masking `addr` at function entry would CORRUPT WRITEBACK, because Addressing Mode 4 computes writeback from the raw `Rn` and the code writes its running raw `addr` back. *** AND IT QUALIFIED THE RECORD RATHER THAN RATIFYING IT: `ARM_CONTROL_ALIGN` is initialised ENABLED but never enforced by any memory access, so the record must NOT say unqualifiedly that the fast path is 'the architectural answer' -- it is the answer for the emulator's implemented A=0/U=0 behaviour, and at the advertised A=1 state **both paths are wrong** because an alignment exception is owed. RANKING: above `b120r`, and it noted `fable_queue.md` ALREADY ranks it first among the five triage rows -- so my brief's framing that it sat behind boot-witnessed items was also wrong. It declined to generalise: this does not put it above every boot-witnessed durability-loss or host-death defect, which need individual harm comparisons. HONEST LIMIT RECORDED: it could not certify the rung-3 probe in a read-only session -- the ledger says that probe is private and uncommitted and no artifact exists under `regress/`. So its endorsement of the WITNESS is READ-from-record, while the manual-and-source finding stands on its own."
