     "note": "HARNESS-INTEGRITY CLUSTER TRIAGE: eight rows about the instruments themselves, each filed by ONE seat and never reviewed. Brief generated mechanically from the ledger.\n\n*** THE PANEL VOTED 5-0 TO DROP `constblind` AND THE VOTE WAS WRONG -- CHECKED, NOT TAKEN. ***  agy, grok, glm, deepseek and minimax all said drop it as 'already audited and fixed'; minimax even named the closing commit (cdc4849). Every one of them was reading the SAME SENTENCE -- the row's own hold, 'AUDITED: diff_timer was the only one exposed; now fixed'. That audit covered SEVEN differentials. THERE ARE THIRTEEN NOW. Six arrived after it, and re-running the audit's own test against today's tree found a LIVE INSTANCE: diff_rtc_range.c, written HOURS EARLIER IN THIS SESSION BY ME, reads TIMER_MAX_FREQUENCY on the expected side of three rows.\n\nMEASURED, and it is the constblind shape in one screen: changing TIMER_MAX_FREQUENCY to 1073741823.0 left all three clamp rows GREEN -- they printed the new value and passed -- while only an absolute-consequence row noticed. Fixed in the same commit by asserting `TIMER_MAX_FREQUENCY == (double) INT_MAX`, derived from OUTSIDE the header, exactly as diff_timer.c:276-280 already does. Gate 2 PASS at 259 checks.\n\n*** THE LESSON IS BIGGER THAN THE ROW: A UNANIMOUS VERDICT DERIVED FROM ONE STALE RECORD IS ONE STALE RECORD, NOT FIVE CONFIRMATIONS. ***  Five seats agreeing looks like strong evidence and was worth exactly as much as the sentence they all read. The packet-fed seats COULD NOT have checked it; the two file-reading seats COULD have and did not. This is the clearest case yet for the standing rule that anything checkable in under two minutes gets CHECKED, not judged -- and the check here was one grep.\n\nWHAT TO DO FIRST -- four-way split, recorded not voted: gateflr (agy, minimax), pcheck (grok), optrow (glm, deepseek: 'the only measured false pass with a known one-flag fix').\n\nSeats: 6 of 7 scriptable (agy 10.3 KB, grok 10.0 KB, kimi 