     "note": "*** VERDICT: REJECT THE STANDING RULE; CLOSE AS SUPERSEDED BY IMPLEMENTATION -- and it explicitly warns AGAINST closing it as a clause-(b) duplicate, which would be a wrong record. *** Three separate grounds, each measured. (1) **THE WARRANT IS A STALE NUMBER.** The closure reasons from '13 of 18 mutants'. `absorb_census.py` defines **22** mutants today (m01..m22) and its own docstring says 'five of twenty-two survived'. The 13/18 survives only as prose in three files -- a frozen number whose denominator moved, **the identical defect `census_exits.py` was written to abolish and the identical defect `exitsweep` was reopened for.** (2) **IT IS NOT IMPLIED BY CLAUSE (b).** Clause (b)'s scope is THIS round's own defect at every fixed site; the census proposal's scope is every PRE-EXISTING row in the touched gate. Different denominators, different questions. (3) **THE TRIGGER IS UNBOUNDED BY THE DIFF, which is the #392 shape.** The capped ask-again works because its cost is bounded by the diff. 'A round that touches a gate runs that gate's census' sets scope by THE GATE: a one-line edit to gate_offline.sh would owe 12 selfmutant lanes, one linking ~221 objects. **No cap stated after the fact repairs a trigger chosen that way.** *** AND THE FUNCTION ALREADY SHIPS, CONTINUOUSLY, WITH BETTER PROPERTIES: *** gate_offline.sh runs 12 `selfmutant_one` invocations every battery, with SM_COVERED (12 stems), SM_EXEMPT carrying DATED named exemptions, an anti-paper row asserting every COVERED stem has a real invocation, and a row failing any past-deadline exemption. **The manifest caught the only hole the seat could find** (fbpending_bound has fragments but no invocation -- exactly what its dated exemption records). RESIDUAL WORTH ADOPTING INSTEAD, bounded in one sentence: extend the SM_COVERED/SM_EXEMPT idiom to `*_probe.py`. **40 probe detectors exist and only FOUR carry a named failability control.** Section R verifies a probe is RUN by a gate; nothing verifies it 