became a limit on guest work", "hold": null}, {"id": "r420", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "#420  Gate 5: the same defect. It failed first, and it is the load witness.", "hold": null}, {"id": "r421", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "r421  The parser got a direct test that uses the shipped code", "hold": null}, {"id": "deadman", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "dead-man switch  nightly_check.sh \u2014 no verdict file is a FAIL", "hold": null}, {"id": "b82", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "#82  MIPS Count/Compare timer: a negative difference, then a division", "hold": "retired: the claim cannot execute; n1 is the real defect"}, {"id": "b75", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "#75  ARM LDRD/STRD to a device loses the second word", "hold": "retired: the claim is refuted by the emitter"}, {"id": "pkbrief", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "pkbrief  Panel process: packet seats need a packet-only brief (panel discovery)", "hold": null}, {"id": "fbdiv", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "fbdiv  footbridge: a guest-written zero timer load divides by zero (SIGFPE, host core dump)", "hold": ""}, {"id": "capgap", "state": "dropped", "sev": null, "title": "capgap  The timer domain admits 32x more than the catch-up cap can ever serve", "hold": ""}, {"id": "capN", "state": "dropped", "sev": null, "title": "capN  The catch-up budget is per timer, but its derivation assumed a single timer", "hold": ""}, {"id": "cflood", "state": "dropped", "sev": null, "title": "cflood  The out-of-domain complaint fires once per CHANGE, so alternating rates flood stderr", "hold": ""}, {"id": "sigunsafe", "state": "dropped", "sev": null, "title": "sigunsafe  Timer callbacks do async-signal-unsafe work, including exit(1), inside SIGALRM", "hold": ""}, {"id": "rtcdet", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "rtcdet  #429 has no detector: reverting the RTC range check leaves gate 2 fully green", "hold": ""}, {"id": "constblind", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "constblind  Every differential that reads a #define for its expected value is blind to that #define", "hold": "STALE HOLD CORRECTED 2026-08-20. It read 'AUDITED: diff_timer was the only one exposed; now fixed' -- that audit covered SEVEN differentials and there are THIRTEEN. Five seats voted DROP on that sentence; re-running the audit found a live instance (diff_rtc_range.c), fixed the same day. The row wants a MECHANICAL check, because the count keeps moving."}, {"id": "mrwgrind", "state": "dropped", "sev": null, "title": "mrwgrind  memory_rw: a length near SIZE_MAX grinds instead of being declined", "hold": ""}, {"id": "mrwstore2", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "mrwstore2  A straddling store whose tail faults makes an MMIO head see the write twice", "hold": "*** THE OLD BLOCKING REASON IS REFUTED AND WAS AN L4 -- corrected 2026-08-26. *** It read 'the fix may itself DIVERGE from real hardware'. **A fix cannot diverge, because the behaviour is PERMITTED and never REQUIRED.** Four PowerPC manuals are now local in `_scratchpad/` (OEA Book III v2.01, VEA Book II v2.01, MPCFPE32B Rev 2 -- the 32-bit PEM covering the PPC750 this row's witness uses -- and the 2005 32/64-bit PEM), fetched and extracted with the same pdftotext used for ddi0100i.txt. PEM 3.2.2 / Book II 2.1: a misaligned store is not atomic, so it falls in the 'any other load or store instruction MAY be partially executed and then aborted... and then re-executed' bucket; 6.1.4 says 'some bytes just before the boundary may have been updated'. **So an emulator that never partially executes is conformant under every one of them -- the INTERSECTION of both readings, not a divergence from either.** *** AND THE MANUALS DISAGREE ON THE CASE THIS ROW IS ACTUALLY ABOUT, recorded as an honest UNKNOWN rather than settled by the stronger document: *** Book III v2.01 4.2.4.1 says that for an access whose first byte is Caching-Inhibited AND Guarded -- exactly the MMIO-head shape here -- a DSI means 'the portion of the storage operand that is in Caching Inhibited and Guarded storage is NOT accessed', i.e. the device write must not happen. The PEM contains no such rule and its 6.1.4 explicitly permits the head bytes to have been updated. Whether a real PPC750 suppresses the device write is NOT established. THE REASONS THAT SURVIVE and on which this row stays filed: REACHABILITY (zero straddling stores measured across three real boots) and SEVERITY (a duplicated device write -- guest-visible, no host kill, no memory-safety consequence; both PowerPC and ARM put the burden on software). *** SCOPE CORRECTION THAT MATTERS MORE THAN THE BLOCKER: 'memory_rw is the choke point' is FALSE FOR THE CLASS. *** stmw/stswi/lmw/lswi and ARM's bdt_store each loop and return on the first failing element, so they have the identical partial-completion-across-a-fault property at the INSTRUCTION level, entirely outside memory_rw -- and PEM 6.1.4's first bullet is about exactly those. **A memory_rw fix would be a partial fix that reads as a complete one**, which is the argument for filing this properly rather than shipping a round a future reader mistakes for closure. TWO SIDE-EFFECT CAUTIONS FROM A PRIOR SEAT MEASURED ABSENT IN THIS TREE: FLAG_NOEXCEPTIONS returns before every SPR write and before ppc_exception(), and there is NO R/C writeback on PPC at all -- so the recorded warning about PPC603 software reload describes hazards that do not exist here. FILED SEPARATELY, all from the same reading: `X(stvx)` does a raw 16-byte memory_rw with no `EA & ~0xF` mask (a second straddling producer, and a divergence in its own right); diff_memory_rw.c has a coverage hole exactly at this row's shape (device head + FAULTING tail); and memory_ppc.c:302-305 is an unlatched guest-drivable fatal(), one per translation fault -- the floodclass shape #464 just closed six of."}, {"id": "optrow", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "optrow  Any row measuring a RESOURCE is only as good as the optimisation it pins", "hold": "audit the other resource-measuring rows"}, {"id": "m8820x", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "m8820x  CMMU: any unhandled offset in a 4 KB window exits the host -- ON A BOOTING RIG", "hold": ""}, {"id": "exitsweep", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "exitsweep  terminating sites across src/devices/ -- MEASURED 360 on HEAD 733a4b6 (90 ACCESS, 101 HELPER, 169 INIT; 177 direct + 183 macro). The earlier 170 was a DATED SNAPSHOT and a HETEROGENEOUS list, not a work queue", "hold": "inventory done; fix per-device, luna88k first\n\n*** CLOSURE REVERTED 2026-08-22: the fable5 batch answered this row's held stage, and the main loop then closed it on 'has commits and was held' WITHOUT checking every other stage. check_stage_panels caught it: this row's review stage is 8/9 missing OPUS5, not fable5. The owner's rule is fire the seat, not degrade -- opus5 is an Agent call and can be run, so it is being run rather than waived."}, {"id": "fbmode", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "fbmode  footbridge ignores FREERUN vs PERIODIC, and three more datasheet-backed defects", "hold": "Scope widened 2026-08-16 by the R6 pass-2 codex seat (read late): beyond the four listed defects it should cover external-event handling, reserved CONTROL-bit masking, and the retained/down-counter model. Still after the live-rig items -- nothing boots a footbridge, so this is correctness, not stability."}, {"id": "i8253zero", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "i8253zero  A programmed count of 0 means 65536, not 'disabled' -- now datasheet-backed", "hold": "settled by document; one-line fix in the hz computation"}, {"id": "fbpending", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "fbpending  footbridge pending_timer_interrupts is an uncapped int, never decremented by the tick", "hold": ""}, {"id": "fbextrate", "state": "dropped", "sev": null, "title": "fbextrate  TIMER_EXTERNAL is given the FASTEST period, which is the least safe choice", "hold": ""}, {"id": "m8probe", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "m8probe  m8820x PROBE_USER/PROBE_SUPER are dropped, so SSR answers a deterministic miss", "hold": "*** RE-FILED LATENT 2026-08-26 ON THE `gdrompiooddlen` PRECEDENT -- REACHABILITY MEASURED NEGATIVE, WITH CONTROLS IN BOTH DIRECTIONS. MUST NOT BE RANKED LIVE. *** A full OpenBSD 7.7/luna88k boot to `login:` (7,684,964,995 instructions) issues **seven distinct CMMU commands and ZERO probes**. Denominator stated: 0 of 7 distinct commands, not 0 of 0 -- and it independently replicates #433's finding thirty rounds later. **The controls are what make this evidence:** a real guest supervisor store on a cold debugger, with the planted word DISASSEMBLED BEFORE STEPPING per the hand-assembled-encoding rule -- cmd 0x24 and 0x20 fire the detector, cmd 0x35 does not while still registering as M8CMD_FIRST. So the negative is real and not a broken grep. CORROBORATION that sharpens it past 'this guest does not': OpenBSD's m88k CMMU driver issues CMMU_PROBE from exactly two places and **both are inside DDB display routines** -- a kernel-debugger feature, not an operating path, reachable only by a human at a DDB prompt. (Caveat recorded by the seat: that source is OpenBSD 2.2/mvme88k, the lineage, not the booting guest; the boot measurement is the evidence for the actual guest.) *** THE OLD COST NOTE WAS WRONG AND IS CORRECTED HERE. *** 'Needs the translation walk' implies a CAPABILITY gap. **The walk exists** -- m88k_translate_v2p() at memory_m88k.c:103 -- **and is already side-effect-free under FLAG_NOEXCEPTIONS** (:344 guards the PG_U/PG_M writeback, :374 returns before any PFSR/PFAR write), its flags already cover the probe's own selectors, and the SSR bit layout is in-tree. **And the driving probe SHAPE is already committed**: m8820x_sites_probe.py:159 ships a PROBE_SUPER row driven by one real guest instruction, ~3 s, no boot. *This is the `sh4dmacie` situation in reverse -- there the shape was missing and it was mis-read as a capability gap; here the shape was already there and nobody re-read the probe.* **WHAT IS ACTUALLY MISSING IS AN ORACLE, AND THE HOLD IS NOW CONDITIONAL ON IT:** translate_v2p exposes no BATC-vs-PATC-vs-walk distinction (needed for CMMU_SSR_BH) and none of the U/M/PROT/SO attributes SSR must carry; real probe semantics are supported only by DDB code reading those registers, which is evidence and not proof; and **there is no 88200/88204 manual in this project**. TRIGGER, on the SH4_VAL_NCLASS precedent: **if an 88200/88204 manual is obtained, this becomes a small round.** That converts an indefinite hold into a conditional one. *** AND THE CURRENT BEHAVIOUR IS THE BETTER ONE, which is the strongest reason not to rank it: *** a dropped probe leaves SSR zero with the V bit clear -- a deterministic, DEFINED 'no valid translation'. Implementing without the manual would replace a defined conservative miss with an INVENTED answer that could steer the guest's paging. **That is the same reasoning used to decline `sh4aaalias` before the SH manual arrived -- and that one turned out to be a wrong finding.** The only known consumer prints a line in a debugger display, so a cosmetically wrong DDB line for a human is the entire measured consequence. WORTH ADDING CHEAPLY: one row asserting SSR == 0 after a probe, to pin the DELIBERATE behaviour that today rests on prose in two comments and no oracle at all."}, {"id": "m8invread", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "m8invread  The register-file arm invalidates EVERY translation on a plain READ", "hold": "measurable today on the booting rig; may be hot"}, {"id": "m8online", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "m8online  Commit the cold-debugger probe: guest-instruction reachability, 3.1 s", "hold": ""}, {"id": "m8latch", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "m8latch  One latch covers four kinds, so one IDR write silences the offset diagnostic", "hold": "behaviour change; filed rather than widened mid-round"}, {"id": "m8snprintf", "state": "dropped", "sev": null, "title": "m8snprintf  The unhandled-access message is formatted on every access even when latched", "hold": ""}, {"id": "gcsections", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "gcsections  diff_m8820x only links because of --gc-sections, and would go RED not SKIP", "hold": "a linker difference would look like a regression"}, {"id": "ns16550", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "ns16550  Guest-writable divisor divides by zero (UB) -- but not in the shipped build", "hold": "REAL but optimisation-dependent; NOT the top item"}, {"id": "m8seg", "state": "dropped", "sev": null, "title": "m8seg  A SEGMENT flush maps to ALL, and the over-invalidation is guest-visible", "hold": ""}, {"id": "m8sarpurge", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "m8sarpurge  98% of the CMMU arm's write-side purges are gratuitous; the biggest is CMMU_SAR", "hold": "A second behaviour change on the write side; #434's claim is the one that was A/B measured. Same one-line shape, worth 2.1x as much."}, {"id": "rtcgate", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "rtcgate  #429's dev_rtc fix has NO offline detector -- reverting it leaves gate 2 green", "hold": ""}, {"id": "m8invground", "state": "dropped", "sev": null, "title": "m8invground  diff_m8invread's spy counts CALLBACK calls; a mutant that clears the dyntrans arrays directly is invisible", "hold": ""}, {"id": "xfamscope", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "xfamscope  gate_crossfamily: a stale no-guest-input comment, and a budget range check that should be per-rig", "hold": "Two residuals from the agy seat on the r420 panel, read 2026-08-16 after the seats-read gate found it unrecorded. Its headline finding was already fixed."}, {"id": "pcchalt", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "pcchalt  #431's deleted PPC page-crossing halt has NO detector -- reinstating it passes all 16 gates", "hold": "Same class as rtcgate. Needs a testppc unaligned page-crossing probe; moderate cost, can ride the next PPC round."}, {"id": "gate3scope", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "gate3scope  The battery's only failability control covers 7 float mutants out of 213 checks", "hold": "Not vacuous today; an overclaim that widens with every check gate 2 gains."}, {"id": "m8patc", "state": "dropped", "sev": null, "title": "m8patc  A SAPR/UAPR write does not invalidate the GUEST's PATC, only the emulator's dyntrans cache", "hold": ""}, {"id": "m8sarseq", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "m8sarseq  No detector composes a SAR write with the SCR flush that consumes it", "hold": "Detector work on a closed round (#435). The two rows a pass-1 codex answer asked for that no other seat did."}, {"id": "m8invpred", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "m8invpred  A reading seat predicted the state-dependent mutant class in pass 1, and it went unread for a day", "hold": "DROP RECOMMENDED BY UNANIMOUS PANEL VERDICT, closure HELD behind codex. The lesson has already been moved into the carrier, so nothing is lost by waiting."}, {"id": "selfmutant6", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "selfmutant6  Six of eleven differentials still have no failability control", "hold": ""}, {"id": "fablequeue", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "fablequeue  A HELD stage is indistinguishable from a forgotten one, so the queue is now gated rather than kept by hand", "hold": null}, {"id": "carrier", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "carrier  CLAUDE.md and PIPELINE.md tracked by copy, with a byte-identity gate so the copy cannot go stale", "hold": null}, {"id": "reprowitness", "state": "in work", "sev": null, "title": "reprowitness  The reproducibility rule asks 'can this MACHINE boot' when it should ask 'can this CODE PATH be executed'", "hold": null}, {"id": "m437ord", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "m437ord  The #437 ordering rule is reviewed but NOT gated -- it needs one fault-injection row", "hold": null}, {"id": "m437multi", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "m437multi  A multi-overlay disk reports CHECK CONDITION when a non-last overlay's fsync fails, though all guest data is durable", "hold": null}, {"id": "m437rtmp", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "m437rtmp  Under R: the overlay files are unlinked at open, so #437's durability claim does not mean what it sounds like", "hold": null}, {"id": "idesync", "state": "in work", "sev": null, "title": "idesync  IDE FLUSH CACHE has no durability path at all -- #437 is SCSI-only", "hold": null}, {"id": "wdcnoirq", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "wdcnoirq  wdc_command()'s default: arm asserts no interrupt, so an interrupt-driven guest waits for a completion that never comes", "hold": "held for the regress seat -- see the entry note and fable_queue.md"}, {"id": "wdcflood", "state": "in work", "sev": null, "title": "wdcflood  wdc_command()'s default: fatal() is unlatched -- 100 commands, 100 host log lines", "hold": null}, {"id": "wdcstandby", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "wdcstandby  0xE0 STANDBY IMMEDIATE is unimplemented and a real guest issues it on every powerdown", "hold": null}, {"id": "landiskdisk", "state": "in work", "sev": null, "title": "landiskdisk  the landisk rig attaches NO DISK, so dev_wdc.c has zero rig coverage in the whole battery", "hold": null}, {"id": "fbwitness", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "fbwitness  The footbridge now HAS a probe witness, dissolving the 'only a rig is missing' blocker on four datasheet-backed defects", "hold": null}, {"id": "ledgerwitness", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "ledgerwitness  The ledger has no field naming which artefact witnessed a defect, so reproducibility claims are unauditable in either direction", "hold": null}, {"id": "smhang", "state": "closed", "sev": "med", "title": "smhang  selfmutant.py has NO TIMEOUT: a hanging mutant wedges gate 2 with no verdict token at all", "hold": null}, {"id": "smnotland", "state": "in work", "sev": "med", "title": "smnotland  selfmutant.py never verifies the mutation LANDED in the tree it compiled -- the missing postcondition behind all three recorded hazards", "hold": null}, {"id": "smdatefmt", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "smdatefmt  A malformed or absent SM_EXEMPT date NEVER expires, silently", "hold": "FIXED and gated; closure held behind codex (Aug 22) like every other row tonight."}, {"id": "ieeeupgrade", "state": "in work", "sev": null, "title": "ieeeupgrade  ieee_store's 2099 exemption entrenches the weakest control on the largest differential", "hold": null}, {"id": "selfcheckman", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "selfcheckman  The SELFCHECK sentinel's hardcoded 5-list is byte-for-byte the F3 defect, one control over", "hold": ""}, {"id": "es438", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "es438  #438 the FIX round for dev_luna88k.c:816 -- store-as-is, per-CPU latch", "hold": "*** STATE CORRECTED 2026-08-22 on the flagship seat's finding: this row was marked DROPPED while recording shipped, witnessed and reviewed work. 'Dropped' reads as 'not done' to anyone who trusts states, which is false here. Closed."}, {"id": "rtcnarrow", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "rtcnarrow  The guest-rate narrowing belongs in the timer core, and the three-device premise for moving it is stale", "hold": "Premise re-derived and largely dissolved; the surviving item is the SILENT FLOOR, not the wide-domain narrowing. Needs its own reproduction before any edit."}, {"id": "rtcflood", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "rtcflood  #429's own clamp diagnostic is unlatched: a guest can drive an unbounded stderr flood", "hold": "Small and well-precedented, but it is a CODE change to a shipped correction and gets its own round with a reproduction, not a fold-in."}, {"id": "scunbacked", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "scunbacked  The SELFCHECK manifest's H is satisfied by an explanatory COMMENT, so a gutted sentinel passes it", "hold": "Not a live hole -- the G covers it -- so this is a strengthening item, and the wrong COMMENT is the more urgent half."}, {"id": "cmtattr", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "cmtattr  The ledger did not know about work that had shipped: 2 hard, 13 soft attribution failures on the first run of a new check", "hold": "Instrument shipped and the 2 hard rows fixed; the 13 soft rows are per-row judgement and want the adjudication, not a sweep."}, {"id": "floodclass", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "floodclass  Guest-drivable host-log floods are a CLASS, not the three instances filed so far -- 72 device files carry fatal() with no latch idiom at all", "hold": "Instrument shipped; the FIX is a policy question (which paths are genuinely guest-drivable) that needs a panel, not a sweep."}, {"id": "pitflip", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "pitflip  The 8253's 16-bit LSB/MSB selector is one-way, so it manufactures a count of 0 and silently loses bytes", "hold": ""}, {"id": "pitlatch", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "pitlatch  The 8253 treats the standard LATCH command as an error, and a real guest issues it in microtime()", "hold": "FIX SHIPPED (#439, 020a672); CLOSURE HELD FOR TWO REASONS, and the second is the substantive one. (1) The flagship review of the diff is held by owner allocation. (2) *** PASS 2 FOUND A REAL DEFECT THAT IS NOT YET FIXED: the latch is never CONSUMED, so after one latch the counter returns 0 indefinitely. *** Handed to the in-flight pitflip round because the consume semantics interact with the LSB/MSB flip-flop that round is fixing. Closing now would record as finished a round whose own pass 2 found it incomplete."}, {"id": "pitlsb", "state": "dropped", "sev": null, "title": "pitlsb  8253 LSB-only mode (RW=01) recomputes no frequency at all, so the timer is never created or retimed", "hold": ""}, {"id": "isaorder", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "isaorder  bus_isa copies a pointer before the device that sets it is added, so the timer-interrupt ack can never run", "hold": "READ only -- needs its own reproduction before any edit."}, {"id": "pitclobber", "state": "in work", "sev": null, "title": "pitclobber  An 8253 LATCH command clobbers the counter's programmed RW format and counting mode, which a real 8254 never does", "hold": "FIX SHIPPED (#439, 020a672); CLOSURE HELD FOR TWO REASONS, and the second is the substantive one. (1) The flagship review of the diff is held by owner allocation. (2) *** PASS 2 FOUND A REAL DEFECT THAT IS NOT YET FIXED: the latch is never CONSUMED, so after one latch the counter returns 0 indefinitely. *** Handed to the in-flight pitflip round because the consume semantics interact with the LSB/MSB flip-flop that round is fixing. Closing now would record as finished a round whose own pass 2 found it incomplete."}, {"id": "witnessunwired", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "witnessunwired  Section K passes a row whose witness artefact no gate ever executes", "hold": "Found by a ninth-seat review. The floodclass detector round would force the wiring as a side effect, so it may not need its own round."}, {"id": "warnnotfail", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "warnnotfail  Deleting a pipeline checker still yields PRECOMMIT_PASS, because the MISSING branches only warn", "hold": "One-line change per branch, but it is a POLICY decision -- a missing checker in a fresh clone is different from one deleted in a working tree."}, {"id": "m8batc", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "m8batc  The BWP/BATC arm has ZERO detector rows, and a mutant that stops updating the shadow batc[] passes BOTH m88k differentials green", "hold": ""}, {"id": "m88kexit", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "m88kexit  memory_m88k.c still calls exit(1) on the CMMU_PFSR_SUCCESS internal-error branch -- the host-kill shape #433 removed from this same device", "hold": "Same class as hlen/devexit; schedule with that sweep rather than alone."}, {"id": "smfalsegreen", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "smfalsegreen  The smnotland fix opened a FALSE GREEN: with the detector itself as subject, selfmutant returns OK while the code under test is untouched", "hold": "One line, but decide the policy first: refuse non-src subjects outright, or refuse only the harness's own directory."}, {"id": "smsetuprow", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "smsetuprow  selfmutant's three-way SETUP/OK/FAIL split is flattened back to two-way at the gate, reproducing the shape it fixes one layer up", "hold": ""}, {"id": "hpcabort", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "hpcabort  Three of eight hpcmips subtypes ABORT at construction, and no gate covers hpcmips at all", "hold": ""}, {"id": "lunafuse", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "lunafuse  dev_luna88k fuse ROM: the word-write arm stores, the byte-write arm drops the write behind an unlatched fatal()", "hold": "Its blocker today was a records line, now discharged by this row existing."}, {"id": "sh4bcr", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "sh4bcr  A guest 4-byte store to SH4_BCR2/BCR3 terminates the host process -- rung-3 witnessed, 5/5 controls green, on an unmodified -E landisk", "hold": ""}, {"id": "rtclen", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "rtclen  A one-token edit to dev_rtc.c:92 re-creates #429's own ADD-becomes-REMOVE defect and passes all 18 detector rows", "hold": "Detector round: give wr_hz a len, have the stub honour it, add a 2^32 row at len 8. Rolls up rtcbranch/rtczero/rtccore cheaply -- same file, same oracle."}, {"id": "rtcbranch", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "rtcbranch  diff_rtc_range.c:239 claims a `>=` would fail its row; MEASURED, it fails NOTHING", "hold": "Merges into [[rtclen]]'s detector round -- same file, same oracle, one fix design."}, {"id": "rtcstale18", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "rtcstale18  Three records say the RTC table has 17 rows; it has 18 -- including the file that cites this exact staleness class as its reason for naming the number", "hold": "Records-only. Rolls into [[rtclen]]'s round for free."}, {"id": "pitcount", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "pitcount  The 8253 models no decrementing count, so OpenBSD's delay() never returns -- under every design discussed, INCLUDING the one that shipped", "hold": "The row with the real guest value, and the one nobody filed. Needs a decrementing count model -- a design item, not a small fix."}, {"id": "ledgerboiler", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "ledgerboiler  The ledger records that seats were FIRED as though they AGREED -- 52 rows carry one summary stamped across five seat cells", "hold": "The CHECK ships now, forward-only. Re-harvesting 52 archived rows from the original seat files is a separate and much larger records round -- and may not be worth it: the seat files still exist under _scratchpad/panel_*/, so the archived cells are recoverable rather than lost."}, {"id": "wdcoracle", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "wdcoracle  Deleting one line from dev_wdc_tick() leaves a controller that can NEVER raise an interrupt -- and a struct-reading opcode detector stays 100% green", "hold": "Not a defect round -- a design constraint on the wdcnoirq and wdcflood detectors. Consume it when either ships; there is nothing to fix on its own."}, {"id": "stalecopies", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "stalecopies  Three unpatched pre-#337 copies of dev_wdc.c live under the project root, and a file-reading seat cited one of them as \"root\"", "hold": "Owner decision on which fix: retarget --add-dir, delete build-asan/, or amend the brief template. Deleting anything under the project root needs the owner's word."}, {"id": "pitlatch2", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "pitlatch2  Does a Counter Latch Command rewind the read flip-flop? The local datasheet does not say, and #440 had to choose", "hold": "An honest UNKNOWN, not a defect either way. Needs a second 8253/8254 source, and there is none in the tree. Do not decide it by vote."}, {"id": "sh4pcicexit", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "sh4pcicexit  sh4_pcic has ELEVEN exit(1) sites and ~116 word offsets reach its default:, plus five more value guards in DEVICE_ACCESS(sh4) itself", "hold": ""}, {"id": "sh4partialread", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "sh4partialread  #441 drops a partial READ of BCR2/BCR3 where it could be serviced exactly", "hold": ""}, {"id": "sh4bsclane", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "sh4bsclane  #441 cites PCR/RTCSR/RTCOR/RFCR as precedent for servicing, then differs from them on both byte-lane and partial-width behaviour", "hold": ""}, {"id": "sh4markspell", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "sh4markspell  Gate 6's fresh-mark census keys on the exact local name `_mark`, so a correct guard spelled `mark` is invisible to it", "hold": ""}, {"id": "fbdisabled", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "fbdisabled  A DISABLED footbridge timer keeps accruing ticks forever, and re-enabling delivers the whole backlog at once", "hold": "Its own round. The witness already exists as arm X of fbpending_drain_probe.py, so it starts with a rung-3 reproduction in hand."}, {"id": "fbvolatile", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "fbvolatile  Two footbridge counters cross the SIGALRM boundary as plain `int` while the identically-used counter in dev_mc146818.c is `volatile int`", "hold": "Needs a decision on the tree-wide convention, not just this device. Two devices already disagree."}, {"id": "fbclearread", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "fbclearread  `case TIMER_1_CLEAR:` never tests writeflag, so a guest READ of the clear register acknowledges an interrupt", "hold": "Small and self-contained, but it changes guest-visible acknowledgement behaviour, so it needs a witness showing a read currently acks and does not after."}, {"id": "fbsmlane", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "fbsmlane  The fbpending_bound self-mutant lane does not run: its pristine arm builds in selfmutant's mirrored tree but emits no PASS token", "hold": "Wiring, not design. Deadline 2026-09-20 is in gate_offline.sh's SM_EXEMPT."}, {"id": "sh4valguards", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "sh4valguards  Four guest-reachable value-guard exit(1)s remain in DEVICE_ACCESS(sh4) itself -- the switch #443 did NOT touch", "hold": "Fix SHIPPED as e841f44 and gate-green (gate_sh_rounding PASS 54). Held open, NOT closed, for two reasons that are both live: the review stage awaits the flagship seat -- and section H refuses a CLOSED row carrying a HELD stage, which is the marker being used to walk away from a gap -- and the pass-2 panel measured nine mutants scoring 33/33 against the shipped detector, filed as sh4valrows. Close when the flagship seat has adjudicated the weak-versus-vacuous call the queue entry asks it to settle."}, {"id": "sh4pcicvoid", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "sh4pcicvoid  One line of #443's fix is genuinely void: `odata = 0` in the unimplemented-CPU PCICONF0 arm cannot be distinguished from its absence", "hold": "Records-only unless the restore logic changes. No round owed."}, {"id": "bscunwired", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "bscunwired  #441's sh4_bsc_width_probe.py still runs in no gate, two rounds after shipping", "hold": "~15 lines in gate_sh_rounding.sh, copying #443's block. Wants one gate run to confirm, so it is not a zero-cost drive-by."}, {"id": "ctorabortclass", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "ctorabortclass  The same construction abort fires on NINE subtypes across FIVE machines -- the alpha instance was recorded as LATENT and UNADVERTISED, and MEASURED 2026-09-07 as both advertised and aborting", "hold": "Do hpcabort first; this is its sweep. The witness already enumerates from -H, so the marginal cost is small once the first fix design is settled."}, {"id": "asansweepblind", "state": "dropped", "sev": null, "title": "asansweepblind  Gate 9 cannot see a construction abort at all, and its awk silently re-attributes SGI's twelve subtypes to rpi", "hold": "Harness round. The awk fix is small; making the gate see aborts is a design question (a floor cannot express 'none of them died')."}, {"id": "pcicmergeA", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "pcicmergeA  The PCICONF0 latch-merge reproduction has no detector row, and could not have had one until the third fault class landed", "hold": "Three lines, but needs a probe run made AFTER the third class landed. Not a design question."}, {"id": "queuesubstr", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "queuesubstr  check_fable_queue matched ids by SUBSTRING, so the gate was green over exactly the state it exists to redden", "hold": "FIXED in this pass; the row is the record. Left open only until its commit lands."}, {"id": "probewiring", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "probewiring  Nothing makes 'no gate runs this probe' a red row or a dated debt -- 24 committed detector rows currently defend nothing between batteries", "hold": ""}, {"id": "smcovered12", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "smcovered12  A closure note says SM_COVERED lists thirteen stems; MEASURED, it lists twelve", "hold": "Records-only, one number. Fold into selfcheckman's dequeue."}, {"id": "sgiarcbiosoob", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "sgiarcbiosoob  machine_sgi.c hands arcbios_init an UNINITIALISED 40-byte buffer on four SGI subtypes that no harness had ever executed", "hold": "Fix SHIPPED as bcdbfb8 and gate-green. Held open only because the pass-2 panel measured the shipped DETECTOR vacuous (16/16 mutants passed it, and a comment-only file passed it), and the runtime value oracle that replaces it is not committed yet. Close when that lands.\n\n*** CLOSURE REVERTED 2026-08-22: the fable5 batch answered this row's held stage, and the main loop then closed it on 'has commits and was held' WITHOUT checking every other stage. check_stage_panels caught it: this row's review stage is 8/9 missing OPUS5, not fable5. The owner's rule is fire the seat, not degrade -- opus5 is an Agent call and can be run, so it is being run rather than waived.\n\n*** THIS HOLD TEXT WAS STALE, not wrong-at-the-time: the runtime value oracle LANDED as regress/sgi_eaddr_probe.py in 6b6d596, wired into gate_asan_sweep.sh and gate_offline.sh with a manifest row. Found by the 2026-08-22 flagship batch -- five days of delay produced exactly one drift of this kind across 46 rows."}, {"id": "asanstale", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "asanstale  The ASan binaries gate 9 grades against are three weeks and many rounds behind HEAD, and nothing asserts freshness", "hold": "Small check, but the remedy interacts with build_asan.sh's cost -- rebuilding two instrumented trees is not free, so the right answer may be SKIP-loudly rather than fail."}, {"id": "stdbufasan", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "stdbufasan  `stdbuf` silently breaks an ASan binary, and this ASan ignores bogus flags so the usual accept/reject discriminator cannot detect it", "hold": "Records-only. No fix owed; the value is that the next round does not rediscover it."}, {"id": "m8saprspy", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "m8saprspy  The SAPR/UAPR arm has the same callback-pointer blindness J6 just closed for the BWP arm, and the machinery to close it now exists", "hold": "One row, and the two-spy fixture already exists. Cheap."}, {"id": "m8lanecomment", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "m8lanecomment  gate_offline's m8820x lane says it 'compiles against the real dev_m8820x.c'; it now also compiles the real memory_m88k.c, and ten kills land there", "hold": "Records-only, one line, in a file the round that caused it was not permitted to edit."}, {"id": "killedreports0", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "killedreports0  A battery KILLED mid-run reported exit 0, because the invocation piped run.sh through tail", "hold": "The habit is most of the fix, but the harness CAN refuse a verdict whose log has no terminal token -- that part is checkable and is what the row is for."}, {"id": "arcbiosubsan", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "arcbiosubsan  Ten uncast buf[3]<<24 sites in arcbios.c -- UB at all ten, and the 64-bit assemblies put the pointer's high word one too small", "hold": "Fix SHIPPED as f39b898 (all ten casts + the frozen-table census + the gate rows) and gate 9 is green at 16 checks against the HEAD-built instrumented tree. Held open, not closed: both stages carry deliberate fable5 omissions, and section H refuses a CLOSED row with a HELD stage. The queue question for that seat: is the frozen-table census (S4 form) the right standing answer for idiom-class fixes, or does it over-freeze?\n\n*** CLOSURE REVERTED 2026-08-22: the fable5 batch answered this row's held stage, and the main loop then closed it on 'has commits and was held' WITHOUT checking every other stage. check_stage_panels caught it: this row's review stage is 8/9 missing OPUS5, not fable5. The owner's rule is fire the seat, not degrade -- opus5 is an Agent call and can be run, so it is being run rather than waived."}, {"id": "nulheredoc", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "nulheredoc  A shell heredoc wrote three literal NUL bytes into two tracked text files, silently turning every grep against them binary", "hold": "Fix and guard are written and negative-controlled; awaiting its commit."}, {"id": "sh4chcr", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "sh4chcr  Essentially ANY guest CHCR write with TD=1 ends the host process -- five exit(1) sites, MEASURED on all eight DMA channels", "hold": "Fix SHIPPED as a636a33 and gate-green. Held open, not closed: the review stage awaits the flagship seat, and section H refuses a CLOSED row carrying a HELD stage. The queue entry asks whether rows A1-A4 discharge the de-escalation clause that seat itself wrote.\n\n*** CLOSURE REVERTED 2026-08-22: the fable5 batch answered this row's held stage, and the main loop then closed it on 'has commits and was held' WITHOUT checking every other stage. check_stage_panels caught it: this row's review stage is 8/9 missing OPUS5, not fable5. The owner's rule is fire the seat, not degrade -- opus5 is an Agent call and can be run, so it is being run rather than waived."}, {"id": "sh4rtcsr", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "sh4rtcsr  A guest RTCSR write kills the host MILLISECONDS LATER from a host timer callback -- a DELAYED shape no repaired site has", "hold": "Fix SHIPPED as f437411 and gate-green (gate_sh_rounding 58, gate_offline 263). Held open, not closed: the review stage awaits the flagship seat, and section H refuses a CLOSED row carrying a HELD stage.\n\nIts queue entry asked whether the de-escalation clause needs a companion rule, because this round's accept row A1 was MEASURED VACUOUS -- it pinned RTCSR_CMF, a write-1-to-keep bit the device never sets, so no guest store could put it in the predicate's operand. *** THAT QUESTION HAS SINCE BEEN ANSWERED AND THE RULE ADOPTED (ACCEPT-ROW FAILABILITY, in the carrier), so what remains here is the seat signing the review stage itself rather than an open design question.\n\n*** CLOSURE REVERTED 2026-08-22: the fable5 batch answered this row's held stage, and the main loop then closed it on 'has commits and was held' WITHOUT checking every other stage. check_stage_panels caught it: this row's review stage is 8/9 missing OPUS5, not fable5. The owner's rule is fire the seat, not degrade -- opus5 is an Agent call and can be run, so it is being run rather than waived."}, {"id": "sh4valrows", "state": "dropped", "sev": null, "title": "sh4valrows  #447's detector is escapable by NINE measured mutants -- one is a one-identifier silent defect, one is a guest-reachable host death", "hold": "Its own round, and it is fully specified below -- four structural holes, the mutants that exploit each, and the fix each seat recommended. Two attempts at it died to transient API 529s, NOT to any difficulty in the work."}, {"id": "sh4dmacie", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "sh4dmacie  CHCR_IE on the one ACCEPTED resource select still ends the host -- deliberately excluded from #448, and PINNED by a detector row", "hold": "Its own round by panel ruling: an unimplemented FEATURE, not an unimplemented ENCODING. Needs an interrupt-delivery oracle, which #448's decline oracle cannot express."}, {"id": "sh4dmanop", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "sh4dmanop  sh4_dmac_transfer() never transfers anything on ANY path, so a guest polling for DMA completion waits forever", "hold": "Filed out of #448's pass 1. A capability gap rather than a crash, and it predates every SH-4 round here."}, {"id": "sh4rtcflags", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "sh4rtcflags  RTCSR's CMF and OVF are never set on any path, and RTCNT has no `case` at all -- a guest polling for a refresh compare-match waits forever", "hold": "Filed out of #449's pass 1. A capability gap, and it predates every SH-4 round here."}, {"id": "sh4latchcollide", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "sh4latchcollide  The sh4_val_first latch classes are kept apart by the ACCIDENT of disjoint bit constants, and no row can detect a wrong class", "hold": "Filed out of #449's pass 2. Not a live defect -- a fragility. Needs a design answer rather than a fix."}, {"id": "arcbioswalk64", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "arcbioswalk64  The 64-bit component walker reads peeraddr+0x34 -- a slice of AffinityMask -- where IdentifierLength is at +0x40", "hold": "Filed out of #450's pass 1. Wrong-offset defect, different semantic class from the shift UB; its own round."}, {"id": "sh4sci", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "sh4sci  Three guest-clocked command bytes end the host in sh4_sci_cmd() -- the first SH-4 site here that a single store cannot reach", "hold": "Fix and detector SHIPPED; gate 10 PASS at 60 checks, run singly; build rc=0 with zero warnings. Held open, not closed: both stages carry a deliberate fable5 omission (the owner's standing batching directive), and section H refuses a CLOSED row with a HELD stage.\n\nTWO QUESTIONS FOR THAT SEAT, both raised by measurements this round rather than by design argument:\n\n1. ACCEPT-ROW FAILABILITY says build the JOINT growth mutant, because #449's guard-only widening measured EQUIVALENT. At THIS site the measuring seat found the opposite: the latch operand is a compile-time constant that cannot mask the guard, so guard-only and joint redden the accept rows byte for byte -- and guard-only ADDITIONALLY silences its own growth (one diagnostic line where joint prints two). So guard-only is the HARDER form here. Does the rule become 'build the form the latch operand does not mask', or does it stay 'joint' with this recorded as an exception?\n\n2. The detector grew 9 -> 14 rows across pass 2, every added row forced by a MEASURED escape. Two of the five were found only because seats were asked for a fifteenth mutant AFTER fourteen were already dead. Is 'ask again once the table is clean' worth making standing practice, or is that the #392 chain in a new coat?"}, {"id": "sh4pclock", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "sh4pclock  One guest TCR store ends the host on THREE machine types (hpcsh, baresh, testsh) -- the sh4 device is added by the CPU, but only 2 of 5 arch-4 SH machines set pclock", "hold": ""}, {"id": "sh4tlbda2", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "sh4tlbda2  Both TLB data-array devices claimed 16 MB and ended the host on the upper 8 MB -- one guest access, and the discriminator is ONE ADDRESS BIT", "hold": "Fix and detector SHIPPED; gate 10 PASS at 62 checks run singly; build rc=0 zero warnings. Held open, not closed: both stages carry a deliberate fable5 omission and section H refuses a CLOSED row with a HELD stage. The question is in the fable5 cells.\n\nTWO THINGS THIS ROUND MEASURED THAT ARE WORTH MORE THAN THE FIX:\n\n1. M1 -- dropping the declined read's memory_writemax64 -- was predicted by THREE pass-1 seats to hand the guest stale host bytes. Built, and it does NOT: memory_rw.c:484-491, correction #95 in this same tree, already zeroes the caller's buffer before every device read as the root-cause fix for that exact class. Measured identical in five contexts. It carries NO row, because a row that cannot fail is decoration. NOTE the precision a seat added: that is equivalence UNDER the current universal pre-zeroing invariant, not equivalence in principle.\n\n2. A REAL SH7750 HARDWARE MANUAL was obtained mid-round by owner directive and immediately REFUTED a finding this round had filed (sh4aaalias). The loop argued the `aa` handlers ignoring bit 23 while `da1` treats it as an array selector meant one must be wrong; the P4 map says both are right, because the address windows are undivided 16 MB and the data windows hold arrays 1 AND 2 split at the 8 MB mark. The manual also confirms the entry is selected by bits [9:8] and that array 2 holds SA and TC -- DIFFERENT fields from array 1 -- which is exactly why declining is right and aliasing would have been wrong."}, {"id": "sh4prio0", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "sh4prio0  Interrupt priority 0 ends the host -- a documented, RESET-DEFAULT state; one guest store on -E testsh and no guest write can prevent it", "hold": "regress HELD for the flagship seat -- see the queue entry"}, {"id": "sh4irq15", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "sh4irq15  machine_test.c picks the one INTEVT line that is priority 0 by construction -- and sh4prio0's fix turns that from loud to silent", "hold": "regress HELD for the flagship seat -- see the queue entry"}, {"id": "sh4iprbcd", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "sh4iprbcd  IPRB/IPRC/IPRD are stored by the device and read by nothing -- WDT, REF, SCI, GPIO, DMAC and the IRL lines are priority 0 permanently", "hold": ""}, {"id": "sh4prioraise", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "sh4prioraise  Raising a priority never re-examines an already-pending request", "hold": ""}, {"id": "sqreadtype", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "sqreadtype  A shipped gate row pins the OPPOSITE of the manual: the store-queue PREF transfer is READ type, and correcting the code turns the row red", "hold": ""}, {"id": "sh4sqwrite", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "sh4sqwrite  The SQ WRITE half is never judged -- so an SQ page can raise no initial-page-write and no write-side protection fault, while the PREF still raises the read-side set", "hold": ""}, {"id": "sh4itlbpr", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "sh4itlbpr  ITLB data array 1 is one bit too wide, and SIX shipped rows now REQUIRE the extra bit", "hold": ""}, {"id": "shprotld", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "shprotld  Every SH TLB row runs privileged, so the READ-side protection code is unreachable and corrupting it is invisible to all 31 rows", "hold": ""}, {"id": "sh4tlbmulti", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "sh4tlbmulti  A guest UTLB write producing two matching entries ends the host -- sh_exception() has no case for EXPEVT_RESET_TLB_MULTI_HIT", "hold": "regress HELD for the flagship seat -- see the queue entry"}, {"id": "sh4pcic15", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "sh4pcic15  PCIC interrupt line 15 can never be delivered -- it lands on 0x3e0, one slot past the priority loop's bound, and the obvious fix is a NO-OP", "hold": ""}, {"id": "sh4iprunpinned", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "sh4iprunpinned  #458's detector reads only four of the ~20 priority slots it maps, so deleting any single mapping line for an unread slot scores full marks", "hold": ""}, {"id": "sh4standinglower", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "sh4standinglower  No row asserts at a high priority and then LOWERS it while the request stands, so a rescan guarded by `int_to_assert == 0` is predicted to escape", "hold": ""}, {"id": "sh4irlm", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "sh4irlm  ICR IRLM=1 is refused outright, and that refusal is what makes #458's decision to decline IPRD correct -- implementing one requires the other in the same commit", "hold": ""}, {"id": "sh4testclock", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "sh4testclock  baresh and testsh have no pclock, so the harness cannot test the TMU path #459 just de-fatalised", "hold": ""}, {"id": "sh4cpudevscope", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "sh4cpudevscope  the SH-4 on-chip block is added PER-CPU but is machine-scoped, and sh4pclock is one symptom of three", "hold": ""}, {"id": "sh4frqcr", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "sh4frqcr  FRQCR is a bare latch with no reset value, so a guest reads back 0x0000 -- not a legal value for the register hardware derives pclock FROM", "hold": ""}, {"id": "hpcshcpu", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "hpcshcpu  the repo contradicts itself about whether hpcsh is an SH-3 or an SH-4 machine, and neither claim is settleable in this tree", "hold": ""}, {"id": "sh4pclockquiet", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "sh4pclockquiet  five measured mutants of #459's pclock guard all score 16/16, because the declining-write value set is exactly {0x0000, 0x0001}", "hold": ""}, {"id": "sh4gdromexit", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "sh4gdromexit  GDROM_DATA had NO LEGAL FIRST TOUCH -- four exit(1) in one register's arm, every access width and both directions fatal on a fresh device", "hold": ""}, {"id": "verdictbold", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "verdictbold  seat_verdicts.py cannot read a VERDICT line wrapped in markdown emphasis, so a correct answer is filed as a format miss", "hold": ""}, {"id": "gdromdefault", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "gdromdefault  both `default:` arms of dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c ran exit(1) -- roughly 248 of 256 control offsets and 27 of 32 DMA offsets were host-fatal to a read OR a write at any width", "hold": ""}, {"id": "gdromdmadefault", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "gdromdmadefault  the second `default:` kill in the same device, in the separately-registered DMA window -- named in no filing and no brief", "hold": ""}, {"id": "gdromdmawidth", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "gdromdmawidth  the DMA tail store ignores `len`: an ALIGNED byte or 16-bit write to an implemented offset clobbers all 32 bits of the shadow register", "hold": ""}, {"id": "gdromcondlies", "state": "closed", "sev": "high", "title": "gdromcondlies (BADVAL PAIR ONLY -- see gdromcondavail for the other defect this id used to cover)  GDROM_COND and GDROM_UNKNOWN_0x88 exit(1) on an unexpected VALUE", "hold": ""}, {"id": "probetimebased", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "probetimebased  pty probes with NO prompt detection: 15 live (11 grandfathered + 4 that arrived unnoticed), and gate_hygiene's ratchet was pinned at 11", "hold": "full panel OWED. This row was REFRAMED 2026-08-24 from a speed defect to a SOUNDNESS defect by one seat (five rows score `survived` as their PASS value, and `alive` is true for a HUNG emulator). Recording that single finding is what made the stage gate expect a full panel -- the row had none before, because it had never been worked. HELD rather than dropping the finding or firing a sixth panel today: the finding is worth more than the row's tidiness, and the sibling row probeslow just had its own nine-seat panel whose seats unanimously said to fold probeslow INTO this row. THE MERGE AND THE PANEL ARE ONE JOB; do them together rather than panelling a row that is about to absorb another."}, {"id": "makercfalse", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "makercfalse  a build that FAILED reported MAKE_RC=0 -- cause NOT established", "hold": ""}, {"id": "verdictprose", "state": "in work", "sev": null, "title": "verdictprose  the verdict extractor sweeps trailing prose into the captured value", "hold": ""}, {"id": "verdictstate", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "verdictstate  the extractor's selftest covers verdict_of only -- the wall/DOWN/echo state machine has zero rows, and all three historical misclassifications lived THERE", "hold": ""}, {"id": "gdromexitsix", "state": "closed", "sev": "high", "title": "gdromexitsix  the DMA+0x18 pair ends the host from a protocol-correct guest, and the four command-layer exits split out", "hold": ""}, {"id": "gdromkeyindex", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "gdromkeyindex  five mutants score a full 32/32 against #461's detector, and one of them writes the DMA arming registers from the CONTROL window", "hold": ""}, {"id": "gdromwarnbits", "state": "in work", "sev": null, "title": "gdromwarnbits  six of eight warn bits are used and they are hand-assigned hex masks; the ninth overflows a uint8_t SILENTLY and floods", "hold": ""}, {"id": "probeslow", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "probeslow  gdrom_default_probe.py's cost is SESSIONS, not rows -- the 1800s overrun was real at 44 rows and is measured false at 52", "hold": ""}, {"id": "asicbadaddr", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "asicbadaddr  dev_dreamcast_asic.c ends the host on a single guest LOAD at SIXTY-FOUR byte addresses, preempting a graceful default: arm that already exists", "hold": ""}, {"id": "gdromdriverclaims", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "gdromdriverclaims  #462's design rests on three guest-driver claims with no local artifact, and ONE of them was measured false the same day", "hold": ""}, {"id": "gdrompionull", "state": "closed", "sev": "high", "title": "gdrompionull  the PIO data read dereferences d->data with no NULL test, reachable with LEGAL values only and with no diagnostic at all", "hold": ""}, {"id": "gdromcondavail", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "gdromcondavail  COND_DATA_AVAIL permanently lies after an overread -- the device advertises data while returning 0xffff (split out of gdromcondlies)", "hold": ""}, {"id": "gdromdmaclear", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "gdromdmaclear  a decline path that CLEARS an already-pending DMA completion escapes all 52 rows -- nodata quiet pins one direction of a two-way transition", "hold": ""}, {"id": "gdromdmaguard", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "gdromdmaguard  `count == 1` -> `count <= 1` accepts a (0,1) arming the model refuses -- one character, and arm halfarm cannot see it", "hold": ""}, {"id": "gdromdmasilent", "state": "dropped", "sev": null, "title": "gdromdmasilent  `if (idata != 0)` -> `if (idata == 1)` at case 0x18: four characters, sticky AND silent, and every row triggers with the value 1", "hold": ""}, {"id": "proberowsubset", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "proberowsubset  the probes cannot score ONE row, so adding a row to a measured detector costs a full re-run -- 4 hours where 20 minutes would do", "hold": "The 2026-08-29 adjudication moved the row-filter SPEC here from `probeslow` and added the fifth guard, so this row carries a design that has never been panelled. The owner already ruled the filter ships as its own round with its own panel and its own negative controls; that panel has not run."}, {"id": "lethalonly", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "lethalonly  a row whose ONLY kill is a LETHAL mutant is not proven to observe its own claim -- under host death every row reads `died`, so the red says nothing about that row's subject", "hold": ""}, {"id": "witnessinvisible", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "witnessinvisible  gen_dashboard.py renders NEITHER `witness` NOR `state_note`, so two ledger fields -- one that orders the queue, one that carries a row's reasoning -- are absent from the published artifact", "hold": ""}, {"id": "killartifacts", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "killartifacts  kill-table drivers preserve no per-row artifact, so a retrospective audit of a PAST round's detector is already impossible -- measured by trying one and failing", "hold": ""}, {"id": "killdriverunTracked", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "killdriverunTracked  the kill-table driver lives in a SESSION TEMP DIR, so the apparatus producing this project's most-cited numbers is untracked and its outputs die with the session", "hold": ""}, {"id": "sectionEnarrow", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "sectionEnarrow  precommit section E sees only `regress/diff_*.c` -- 14 of 52 detectors -- and prints an UNQUALIFIED `no detector added or modified`", "hold": ""}, {"id": "asiceventaddr", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "asiceventaddr  SYSASIC_EVENT_TO_ADDR is correct only for e in [0,95]: high events decode to DROPPED fourth-word offsets OR to LEGAL mask-register cases that clobber guest state, and no r-guard can see the second kind", "hold": ""}, {"id": "probeabsentraise", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "probeabsentraise  three rows guard a THREE-state value with a TWO-state test and raise ValueError on `absent`, taking the whole probe down with no verdict line -- and the correct guard is already used by two other rows in the same file", "hold": ""}, {"id": "asicpermute", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "asicpermute  `(relative_addr / 4) % 3` permutes the ASIC register index bijectively and NO round-trip row can see it", "hold": ""}, {"id": "asicindexcase", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "asicindexcase  the deleted guard was ALSO a bounds check, so an INDEXING case added at an r==3 offset is now a SILENT out-of-bounds access", "hold": "#463b FINAL FORM, ADJUDICATED AT 9/9 WITH NO MARKERS. Ships a SILENT FALL-THROUGH decline. The LATCH is REFUTED, not deferred. Budget: the guarded arm, the r==3 row value upgrade, one contract sentence. Not run."}, {"id": "asicoffsetkill", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "asicoffsetkill  a per-offset kill at an offset no detector row touches -- `if (relative_addr == 0x1c) exit(1)` scores clean", "hold": ""}, {"id": "asickillsurvive", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "asickillsurvive  #463's four kill rows are SURVIVE-ONLY, so an OOB read returning garbage would score green", "hold": ""}, {"id": "asicarray4", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "asicarray4  sizing the ASIC's four register arrays [4] instead of [3] would de-fang the whole out-of-bounds class", "hold": ""}, {"id": "panelseatlabel", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "panelseatlabel  panel.sh reports a 403 quota wall as 'RATE-LIMITED (HTTP 429)' and its tally omits the substitute transport", "hold": ""}, {"id": "seatthinkburn", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "seatthinkburn  a FIFTH seat-failure class: an empty response wrapped in 260 KB of thinking, which every size check scores as the panel's best answer", "hold": ""}, {"id": "scoreclaimhex", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "scoreclaimhex  check_score_claims reads a slash-separated HEX LIST as a score claim, and reports a stale score that was never written", "hold": ""}, {"id": "ledgerstatelies", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "ledgerstatelies  the ledger's schema had NO WRITTEN SEMANTICS for `state`, so two seats read opposite conventions into one string and five agreed on 4 of 11 rows", "hold": "THIS IS ITS OWN ROUND. The matrix panel below is its PASS-1. Adjudicated 2026-08-25: #463 may not repair it -- ~50 instances across five classes is a records-correction round, and the standing rule for those (budget a SECOND review pass, because corrections repeat the errors they correct) cannot be honoured inside another round."}, {"id": "asicarraygeom", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "asicarraygeom  shrinking a modelled array to [2] scores a CLEAN SWEEP -- no row verifies the struct declaration the whole invariant rests on", "hold": ""}, {"id": "ledgerclosureby", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "ledgerclosureby  closure.by holds the WRONG KIND of value on two rows, and a role distinction the roster does not model on ten more", "hold": ""}, {"id": "asicdecline", "state": "dropped", "sev": null, "title": "asicdecline  #463b: an explicit decline at r == 3 is an ANCHOR that closes the whole translation class using rows already shipped", "hold": ""}, {"id": "citestale", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "citestale  a same-file line citation is invalidated by the round's OWN later edits -- six instances in one round, and no gate checks it", "hold": ""}, {"id": "wdcringambig", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "wdcringambig  the wdc inbuf ring uses head==tail for BOTH empty and full, and ONE legal ATAPI READ(10) overruns it -- GUEST-REACHABLE silent corruption, not latent", "hold": "regress"}, {"id": "asicannounce", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "asicannounce  #463 deleted the ASIC's only diagnostic on a warrant that was measured too broad -- a bounded latched announce IS constructible, on a predicate that is not empty", "hold": ""}, {"id": "gdrompiooddlen", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "gdrompiooddlen  the GDROM PIO read guards ONE byte and then reads TWO -- LATENT, because no path can produce an odd length today", "hold": ""}, {"id": "wdcperopcode", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "wdcperopcode  wdc_command()'s unimplemented-command latch is per-DEVICE, so a guest cycling opcodes gets ONE line and 255 silent aborts -- a SPEC choice, filed not fixed", "hold": ""}, {"id": "wdcexpecteddata", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "wdcexpecteddata  an EXPECTED DATA write has no quiet pin, so dropping !write_in_progress would warn-and-latch on legal I/O with every detector row green", "hold": ""}, {"id": "wdcflagindep", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "wdcflagindep  three of six wdc latch classes are pinned at one site each, and per-INSTANCE storage is unpinned entirely -- the fork has a two-controller rig", "hold": ""}, {"id": "wdchpcmipsaddr", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "wdchpcmipsaddr  DEVINIT(wdc)'s MACHINE_HPCMIPS special case is a NO-OP carrying a TODO: it recomputes the value the default already gives", "hold": ""}, {"id": "wdcsectorreset", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "wdcsectorreset  a one-line latch reset on a NORMAL register write restores the #464 width flood and leaves all 26 detector rows green", "hold": null}, {"id": "panelrefire", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "panelrefire  panel.sh printed 're-firing once' and re-fired nothing -- message corrected; the automatic retry itself is not implemented", "hold": null}, {"id": "depsrm", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "depsrm  the standing `rm src/cpus/*.o` rests on a FALSE reason -- the deps ARE tracked. Is the rm needed at all, and what does it cost every mutant table?", "hold": null}, {"id": "countderiver", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "countderiver  the transcribed-count class -- ADJUDICATED 2026-08-26: one prose rule, zero new tools, and a pre-specified escalation trigger", "hold": null}, {"id": "gdromallocpair", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "gdromallocpair  alloc_data() does not release before it allocates -- #465's free_data() pairing is CONVENTIONAL, not STRUCTURAL", "hold": null}, {"id": "preserveinstrument", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "preserveinstrument  the durable-path rule preserves MEASUREMENTS but not the INSTRUMENT -- and the instrument is what makes a result portable", "hold": null}, {"id": "probewiringscope", "state": "in work", "sev": null, "title": "probewiringscope  check_probe_wiring.py cannot see any detector below regress/'s top level, while its own docstring claims it covers them", "hold": null}, {"id": "panelcite0824", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "panelcite0824  EIGHT uncited panel dirs (08-24 and 08-25) hold seat answers section G has never scoped -- including a pass 2 on a shipped commit", "hold": null}, {"id": "loadfragile", "state": "in work", "sev": null, "title": "loadfragile  every pty probe waited on an ABSOLUTE deadline, so a merely SLOW run failed identically to a STUCK one -- the battery could only be trusted on an idle machine", "hold": null}, {"id": "baretitle", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "a large minority of OPEN rows are a bare title with an EMPTY note -- they name a defect and record no reachability, severity or reproduction.  COUNT DELIBERATELY NOT IN THIS TITLE: the note carries the deriver, because the literal that used to be here (20 of 55) was stale by 34 rows and a reader meets the title first", "hold": null}, {"id": "mb89352halt", "state": "dropped", "sev": null, "title": "mb89352halt  An unimplemented SCSI command stops the emulated CPU rather than refusing the command", "hold": null}, {"id": "m8sarrows", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "m8sarrows  The m88k CMMU seam is structurally blind: a ONE-IDENTIFIER edit sweeps both offline detectors 79/79 while half the machine's PATC flushes stop dispatching", "hold": null}, {"id": "exitdenom", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "exitdenom  The terminating-site census counts SITES, not defects -- and the number has had three generations (114 -> 170 -> 360), each recorded as if authoritative", "hold": null}, {"id": "sh4valm33", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "sh4valm33  COVERAGE DEBT: mutant M33 (reinstating a terminating site reachable by a guest mov.l to TCR2 at 0xffd80028) escapes the detector -- the arm itself was FIXED in #459 and is not live", "hold": null}, {"id": "offlinescope", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "offlinescope  The machine embargo was over-scoped: 11 of 14 offline differentials build and run in 10 seconds total, and six of the top eight ranked defects sit in files they already pull in", "hold": null}, {"id": "shortread", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "shortread  fread_helper's short-read path: len is decremented by the REQUESTED amount while the caller's zero-fill uses the ACTUAL, so a short read in a non-final iteration covers the wrong region", "hold": null}, {"id": "wdcrearmzero", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "wdcrearmzero  The ATAPI re-arm reports ZERO bytes remaining mid-transfer, so every transfer above 64 KB stalls and never completes", "hold": null}, {"id": "asanstaletree", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "asanstaletree  build-asan/ carries pre-#427 copies of at least two device files, so any ASan measurement there measures code four rounds old", "hold": null}, {"id": "rung1exempt", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "rung1exempt  THREE rounds have now shipped a fix on rung-1 evidence alone and no ladder term fits, so each sits `held` with the same unanswered question", "hold": ""}, {"id": "wdcoddstep", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "wdcoddstep  `atapi_len -= consumed` STEPS PAST ZERO on an odd-length chunk -- and the length is a GUEST-CHOSEN allocation byte, so a plain INQUIRY reaches it", "hold": ""}, {"id": "wdcaddtoinbufbound", "state": "in work", "sev": "med", "title": "wdcaddtoinbufbound  the ATAPI data-port WRITE arm has no phase guard, so a guest can inject bytes into a live DATA-IN transfer and desynchronise the next 32768-byte chunk", "hold": "regress"}, {"id": "wdcrearmwalk", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "wdcrearmwalk  the #473 detector pins the FIRST re-arm only; a transfer crossing several chunk boundaries is never walked end to end", "hold": ""}, {"id": "fbclearidempotent", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "fbclearidempotent  the DC21285 says a timer clear has no effect if the interrupt was already clear, but the write arm reloads a periodic timer unconditionally", "hold": "BRIEF-READY, NOT STARTED.  *** UNBLOCKED 2026-08-28: the stated blocker was `shortread`, and `shortread` SHIPPED as `#488` (commit 09362f0) on 2026-08-27.  The hold outlived its own condition by a day. ***  Nothing now contends for the seats on this row's account.  Recorded rather than silently cleared, because a hold whose condition has been met is a WRONG RECORD in exactly the way this project keeps paying for: a future reader scanning for available work skips a row that says it is blocked, and the row's own evidence is already complete.  The premise is settled (datasheet quoted, offsets straddle-calibrated, the defect located to one of three effects, existing rows proved blind by construction), so the round opens without re-deriving any of it."}, {"id": "fbclearaliases", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "fbclearaliases  the four-address timer fold is READ from the code and is not detector-backed -- every #474 row exercises timer 1", "hold": ""}, {"id": "wdccdbmidxfer", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "wdccdbmidxfer  a guest data-port WRITE mid-DATAIN frees the IN-FLIGHT ATAPI transfer -- REPRODUCED, `freed 1 live transfer(s)`", "hold": "held for the regress seat -- see the entry note and fable_queue.md"}, {"id": "wdcdataoutdrain", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "wdcdataoutdrain  after ANY ATAPI dispatch the local inbuf_len is 0, and `0 % 512 == 0` fires the write-back -- 512 stale ring bytes go to the DISK IMAGE", "hold": "held for the regress seat -- see the entry note and fable_queue.md"}, {"id": "bugfileledgerdir", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "bugfileledgerdir  precommit section P checks ledger -> OUTSTANDING_BUGS but NOTHING checks the reverse, and four rows were filed in prose only", "hold": ""}, {"id": "wdcwidthmatrix", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "wdcwidthmatrix  the #475 detector covers access widths 4 and 2; the full width x remainder matrix and exact-fit controls are not shipped", "hold": ""}, {"id": "wdcnullxfer", "state": "held", "sev": null, "title": "wdcnullxfer  #475's clamp WOKE a NULL dereference that the broken counter had been hiding -- two guest register accesses SIGSEGV the host.  FIXED in-round as #476", "hold": ""}, {"id": "wdccdbaccum", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "wdccdbaccum  give the ATAPI CDB its own accumulator, as dev_mb89352.c does, instead of trigger-matching on a SHARED ring occupancy", "hold": ""}, {"id": "wdcwipclear", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "wdc: a new command did not clear write_in_progress", "hold": ""}, {"id": "wdcringnoflush", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "wdc: a new command did not flush the input ring", "hold": ""}, {"id": "wdcstalephase", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "wdc: a new command did not reset atapi_phase", "hold": ""}, {"id": "wdcaltfixture", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "wdcaltfixture  every alt-ctrl row runs in a state NO REAL DRIVER IS EVER IN, so eleven single-field qualifications escape the whole file", "hold": ""}, {"id": "wdcaltwidth", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "wdcaltwidth  the alt-ctrl register's ACCESS WIDTH is unpinned: every row uses len == 1 while the region is registered TWO bytes wide", "hold": ""}, {"id": "wdcfloodstale", "state": "in work", "sev": null, "title": "wdcfloodstale  `overrun x2` in wdc_flood_probe.py cannot reach its target since #479 reset the ring on every command", "hold": ""}, {"id": "asanunbuildable", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "asanunbuildable  build-asan/ fails to build (103 errors, exit 0) and has NO binary; a .c was propagated without its header, and 363 files differ from SEC", "hold": ""}, {"id": "seatunfinished", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "seatunfinished  a seat that investigates and never concludes is scored 'answered, format miss' -- no answer recorded as bad formatting", "hold": ""}, {"id": "wdcaltctrlbit", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "wdcaltctrlbit  the ALT-CTRL arm triggered on WDCTL_4BIT (the head-count bit) instead of WDCTL_RST -- OVERTAKEN by #481", "hold": ""}, {"id": "landiskaux", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "landiskaux  DEVINIT has no MACHINE_LANDISK case, so twelve measured Device Control writes land on unmapped memory -- one line, and now UNBLOCKED", "hold": "READY TO IMPLEMENT.  The panel's gate -- 'ship, but not on this packet' -- is discharged: the census is re-taken, preserved at regress/artifacts/landiskaux/census_20260827/  *** MOVED THERE 2026-08-28.  It had been left in `_scratchpad/`, which is the SAME VOLATILE PATH that already destroyed BOTH of gdromexitsix's witnesses -- that row's `probe:` field cited two scripts a `find` over the whole project cannot locate.  So a round-ready row's entire warrant was one scratchpad rotation from evaporating for the second time, and the gate that catches non-existent citations could not have caught it, because the path still existed. ***, and its discriminating control came back on the missing-offset-case side.  Carry into the round: (1) the first write is 0x0c and DOES carry WDCTL_RST, so the arm is not inert and the round may not describe it as such; (2) grok's standing ruling that this must NOT be folded into wdcnoirq as a rider, because nIEN stays dead either way."}, {"id": "gdromdefaultstatusless", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "gdromdefaultstatusless  gdrom_default_probe.py's session() calls waitpid and DISCARDS the status, so NO row in 52 can tell a clean exit from a signal from a decline", "hold": ""}, {"id": "landiskunmapped4", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "landiskunmapped4  a landisk boot makes 26 unmapped accesses, deterministic across three boots, of which only ONE was previously carried -- the guest's whole external-interrupt setup is discarded", "hold": ""}, {"id": "hygienenonexistant", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "hygienenonexistant  the boot-log hygiene gate does NOT look for `non-existant` at all -- 26 guest stores per landisk boot fall off the memory map and no gate says a word", "hold": ""}, {"id": "gdromlatchreuse", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "gdromlatchreuse  reusing GDROM_W_LEN instead of #489's new warn bit scores 19/19 -- the detector cannot see one latch bit standing in for another", "hold": ""}, {"id": "gdromonce", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "gdromonce  the `(once)` suppression marker is claimed in prose by the house idiom and pinned by no row in any gdrom probe", "hold": ""}, {"id": "asanbaselinedrift", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "asanbaselinedrift  the propagation check is INCREMENTAL, so a stale BASELINE is invisible -- build-asan/ drifted until it would not compile while passing every commit", "hold": ""}, {"id": "gatebuildallow", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "gatebuildallow  gate_build.sh PINS its own twin-tree allowlist while est_divergence.txt exists to prevent exactly that -- one stale entry cost a RED weekly battery and a silently SKIPPED gate for five days", "hold": ""}, {"id": "gdromfmt24", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "gdromfmt24  the 0x24 data-format arm is left unlatched and unbounded beside a latched sibling, and its correctness is unsettleable without an SPI/GD-ROM doc", "hold": ""}, {"id": "gdromlatchcross", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "gdromlatchcross  two MEASURED 20/20 escapes past #490's detector: a one-character `|=` -> `=` that silently unlatches eight siblings, and an order-dependent latch widening", "hold": ""}, {"id": "hookctlgate", "state": "closed", "sev": "low", "title": "The wsl-dollar hook's 18 controls are run by nothing", "hold": null}, {"id": "hookdurability", "state": "closed", "sev": "low", "title": "round_reminder.sh is untracked -- lost on a fresh clone", "hold": null}, {"id": "batteryblindstart", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "The battery's pre-flight git status is a multi-minute BLIND WINDOW", "hold": null}, {"id": "diskimagesign", "state": "in work", "sev": null, "title": "#472's clamp introduced two -Wsign-compare warnings that cost a GATE", "hold": null}, {"id": "propagatecpsilent", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "The propagate script prints 'propagated' after a FAILED cp", "hold": null}, {"id": "divsweeplines", "state": "closed", "sev": null, "title": "diag_div_sweep.py reports line numbers that are systematically too small", "hold": null}, {"id": "divratchetband", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "The class ratchet is an equality; should it be a band?", "hold": null}, {"id": "ghostround", "state": "open", "sev": null, "title": "A round can ship without writing back, and no gate can see it", "hold": null}, {"id": "wdcaltoffset", "state": "closed", "sev": "high", "title": "wdc alt-ctrl: a guest write to the READ-ONLY Drive Address Register aborts a live transfer and purges the ring", "hold": null}, {"id": "killdrivercrash", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "Kill drivers score a CRASHED mutant as a table result", "hold": null}, {"id": "wdcaltdigin", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "wdc alt-ctrl: a READ of the Drive Address register returns the alt-status byte, which matches no hardware on any layout", "hold": null}, {"id": "wdcaltownership", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "0x3f6/0x3f7 are contested between wdc and the floppy controller, and the tree documents the collision from BOTH sides while resolving it from neither", "hold": null}, {"id": "novpadfourth", "state": "closed", "sev": "med", "title": "the no-VPATH rule has FOUR build trees and precommit section B enforces only three -- the WSL arc tree is invisible to it", "hold": null}, {"id": "wdcatapistswap", "state": "closed", "sev": "med", "title": "the #495 guard's flag operand can be swapped for `atapi_st != NULL` and the detector cannot see it -- measured to refuse every ordinary data-port write for the life of the device", "hold": null}, {"id": "wdcprobeendian", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "every wdc rung-3 probe has only a LITTLE-ENDIAN leg, so the big-endian half of each guard is unpinned by construction", "hold": null}, {"id": "outcomegap", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "ten non-grandfathered closed rows carry panel entries with no seat outcome -- and the recount that called that number wrong was itself the miscount", "hold": null}, {"id": "wdcwidth4", "state": "closed", "sev": "high", "title": "an INEQUALITY width qualifier swept the whole detector -- CLOSED by injecting a third access width, so the width family is closed on both sides", "hold": null}, {"id": "wdcringresetblind", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "the rung-3 wdc probe cannot see #479's ring-reset half: deleting the ring reset from wdc_new_command_reset() escapes all 26 rows", "hold": null}, {"id": "wdcflagswap2", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "a SECOND flag-operand replacement -- `cur_command == ATAPI_PKT_CMD` -- sweeps the 15-row probe 30/30, and the two-vehicle accept arm does not defeat it", "hold": null}, {"id": "relrefstale", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "a RELATIVE git ref inside a round's durable artifacts goes stale the moment the round commits -- twice in one day, in two files of one directory", "hold": null}, {"id": "wiringcliff", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "check_probe_wiring.py's EXEMPT table expires as a block and the checker then hard-fails -- and NOT ONE of the entries is a cheap wiring: every reason string names a prerequisite that does not exist.  DERIVE the population and the date, never quote them: `grep -n 2026-09-20 tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py' for the set (the file states the count in its own comment), and the comparison is `d < today' so the block is still VALID on its stamped date and first reds the day after.  (SUPERSEDED TITLE, kept only as the demoted record of what was claimed: \"EIGHT probe-wiring exemptions expire on 2026-09-20 together and the checker hard-fails -- four are cheap, only four need a design round\".  Wrong on the count, wrong on the date by one in the safe direction, and wrong that any are cheap; its own halves did not add up against the population.)", "hold": null}, {"id": "sgirekills", "state": "open", "sev": "high", "title": "dev_sgi_re.c: SIX active exit(1) statements remain -- the census is DERIVED 2026-09-08 by stripping comments, and supersedes the stale \"twelve / eleven guest-reachable\" headline; the six-row oracle re-pin this row was organised around is ALREADY DISCHARGED (the probe has no `ld kill` rows left)", "hold": null}, {"id": "widthkillclass", "state": "closed", "sev": "high", "title": "a GUEST CHOOSING AN ACCESS WIDTH ends the host -- #337 fixed this class in dev_wdc.c and 16 sites in two other devices were never swept", "hold": null}, {"id": "storesidedead", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "a probe row whose oracle is 'the store had no effect' PASSES when the store never executed -- seven of #496's twenty-two rows, and the class is tree-wide", "hold": null}, {"id": "panelargvcap", "state": "closed", "sev": "med", "title": "panel.sh WARNS that a brief exceeds the argv cap and then fires the two argv seats anyway -- measured, two seats lost on a 38 KB brief", "hold": null}, {"id": "sgitexindex", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "dev_sgi_re.c's CRIME_RE_TEX index uses >> 3 where its three sibling arms use >> 1 -- consecutive writes overlap by three entries and 81 of 112 are unreachable", "hold": null}, {"id": "sgidewidth", "state": "closed", "sev": "high", "title": "dev_sgi_re.c's sgi_de handler is a SEVENTH live width kill -- one guest `sb` to the DE window ends the host, and widthkillclass would have shipped 6 of 7", "hold": null}, {"id": "sgimtewidth", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "sgi_mte serves EVERY access width and a one-byte store silently ZEROES the adjacent register -- and the obvious fix for its dead arm would CREATE a width kill", "hold": null}, {"id": "sgilinearoob", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "dev_sgi_re.c: FIVE arms write past a CAPPED index on an unaligned len==8 access -- TLB_A/B/C overrun uint16_t[256] BY THREE (a wider overrun than the LINEAR arms this row was filed for, and unrecorded until 2026-09-08), LINEAR_A/B overrun uint32_t[32] by one.  ONE fix design covers all five; the sixth arm (TEX) is in bounds only because its index shift is WRONG", "hold": null}, {"id": "selftestaddition", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "check_probe_wiring.py's selftest builds 7 of its 22 mutants by SUBTRACTING from the live EXEMPT table, so the table emptying -- the outcome it exists to drive toward -- turns SELFTEST_PASS 22/22 into FAIL 12/22", "hold": null}, {"id": "exitcensus", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "exit_census.py's BY-ENCLOSING-CONSTRUCT split attributed every call in a plain non-static function to the preceding DEVICE_ACCESS -- inflating the bucket used to rank reachability", "hold": null}, {"id": "sgireunmapped", "state": "closed", "sev": "high", "title": "dev_sgi_re.c's unmatched-address else arm ends the host on ANY width in EITHER direction across 2080 bytes of its own registered window -- one guest `lb` at 0xb5001800 -- and NEITHER #497 nor sgirekills-as-scoped touches it", "hold": null}, {"id": "sgideoob", "state": "dropped", "sev": null, "title": "WITHDRAWN -- a FALSE out-of-bounds: de_reg/mte_reg are sized to the FULL window while the START strip HALVES the index range, so regnr+1 has 512 slots of headroom", "hold": null}, {"id": "fbfiqsilent", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "dev_footbridge.c answers FIQEnable reads with a silent constant 0 while its IRQ twin kills the host -- the whole FIQ column is softer than the IRQ column", "hold": null}, {"id": "witnesssilent", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "13 witnesses are ungated BY DESIGN, so a witness whose arms rot is indistinguishable from one that works -- and n6 is about to rot one", "hold": null}, {"id": "agyreservation", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "the owner's agy-reserved-for-measurement ruling lives in the carrier as PROSE and panel.sh knows nothing about it -- so agy was fired in the very next panel", "hold": null}, {"id": "gatedevicesplit", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "gate_arm.sh has become the tree's device-probe gate by accretion, and #497 is the FIRST probe in it on a foreign machine AND CPU family", "hold": null}, {"id": "otherkilldeath", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "sgi_re_width_probe.py's `other kill` row is a DEATH-ONLY oracle -- a one-character change to the text it claims to pin scores 35/35, MEASURED", "hold": null}, {"id": "holdmarkermulti", "state": "closed", "sev": "med", "title": "gen_codex_wall.py's marker regex matches ONE seat name, so a stage held awaiting SEVEN seats is invisible to section I -- the biggest holds are the ones that escape", "hold": null}, {"id": "holdphase", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "the hold-marker discriminator was implemented with `>=` where the carrier says LATER, and six holds were cleared by SAME-DATE siblings written by the batch that created the marker", "hold": null}, {"id": "gxprobepccache", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "gxprobe cached the pc across `step`, so a driver that stepped without _exec_at() had its next pc assignment silently skipped -- a detector row that could not fail", "hold": null}, {"id": "sgideprimop", "state": "closed", "sev": "high", "title": "dev_sgi_re.c draw_primitive(): a guest-written drawing op ends the host -- 254 of 256 top-byte values reach `default: fatal(); exit(1);`", "hold": null}, {"id": "pcc2widthkills", "state": "open", "sev": "high", "title": "dev_pcc2.c: TEN live width kills (of twelve guards -- #119 already fixed two in this same file, and its shape is the answer)", "hold": null}, {"id": "lcabadval", "state": "in work", "sev": "high", "title": "dev_lca.c: SIX chipset registers in DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_ioc) kill the host on a value outside a small accepted set (CONF, TB_ENA, W_BASE0/1, W_MASK0/1) -- plus a seventh exit in the default arm, which this round does NOT close", "hold": null}, {"id": "exit0oracle", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "shipped rows pin survival as an `exit0` want, so a mutant that announces and then exit(0)s satisfies them -- count DERIVED, never restated: grep -h 'row(.*\"exit0\"' regress/*.py | wc -l", "hold": null}, {"id": "sgirelatchenum", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "SGIRE_W_* spells its bits as literal `1 << N`, so a duplicate is legal C and silent", "hold": null}, {"id": "staticlatchscope", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "dev_*.c: warn-once latches that are FUNCTION-STATIC, so one guest access to one device instance permanently silences the diagnostic for every other instance -- count DERIVED BY SCOPE, never by variable name (three name-based censuses came back wrong; see the row's deriver)", "hold": null}, {"id": "lcaconfwidth", "state": "in work", "sev": "med", "title": "a 64-bit guarded value printed through (int) in a KILL diagnostic, so the message can name a value that would NOT have killed -- and at one site names the value the code calls ACCEPTABLE.  A CLASS, censused; derive the population, do not quote it", "hold": null}, {"id": "mtetlbflood", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "dev_sgi_re.c the MTE default dst_tlb arm is an UNLATCHED guest-drivable fatal() -- three of eight guest-settable values reach it", "hold": null}, {"id": "pcc2c040kill", "state": "open", "sev": "high", "title": "pcc2c040kill  dev_pcc2.c: a LEGAL byte write to GENCTL with the C040 bit set ends the host in reassert_interrupts() -- the sibling kill the width fix exposed rather than removed", "hold": ""}, {"id": "pcc2counter8", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "pcc2counter8  dev_pcc2.c: the counter/compare arm accepts an 8-byte write and raw-copies through its neighbours -- T2CTL (0x16) and T1CTL (0x17) are written by a guest st.d at the 8-aligned T2COUNT", "hold": ""}, {"id": "pcc2widecompose", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "pcc2widecompose  per-lane composition of a wide PCC2 write -- the faithful upgrade to DECLINE, filed as the design alternative the direction split did not take", "hold": ""}, {"id": "pcc2pscaleflood", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "pcc2pscaleflood  dev_pcc2.c: PSCALEADJ's write-side fatal() is unlatched and guest-drivable -- one of nine such prints (see pcc2huhflood)", "hold": ""}, {"id": "pcc2assertflood", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "pcc2assertflood  dev_pcc2.c: reassert_interrupts() prints `[ pcc2: IPL = ..., MASK = ... => ASSERT ]' on every tick while an interrupt is pending -- a debug() (silenced by -q) but a flood under -v", "hold": ""}, {"id": "pcc2latchwidth48", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "pcc2latchwidth48  coverage debt: the latch-SET decision (a wide write arriving with the block's latch already set is still refused) is pinned at width 2 only -- the 4- and 8-byte repeats are unrowed", "hold": ""}, {"id": "pcc2iclrdec", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "pcc2iclrdec  coverage debt: the TxICR interrupt-clear DECREMENT (ICLR with INT set and a count pending) is exercised by no row in the tree -- and the SIGALRM-vs-DEVICE_TICK window around it is unmeasured", "hold": ""}, {"id": "pcc2huhflood", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "pcc2huhflood  dev_pcc2.c: eight unlatched, guest-drivable fatal() HUH? prints in the pcc2 arms (TxCTL `idata & ~7', TxICR `idata & 0xe0', SCC*, PSCALEADJ...) -- siblings of pcc2pscaleflood", "hold": ""}, {"id": "pcc2defaultkill", "state": "closed", "sev": "high", "title": "pcc2defaultkill  dev_pcc2.c: the `default:' arm exit(1)s on any unimplemented offset -- and kills READS the top-of-function memcpy has already served; SCCTXIACK (0x25) has no case, SCCRXIACK runs on reads", "hold": ""}, {"id": "pcc2iackmodel", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "pcc2iackmodel  dev_pcc2.c: SCCTXIACK/SCCMOIACK (0x25/0x23) have no acknowledge semantics; model on the SCCRXIACK shape AFTER pcc2c040kill (that path enters the C040 kill)", "hold": null}, {"id": "pcc2anngrid", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "pcc2anngrid  harness: the pcc2 default arm's announce grid (channel x arm x width) has four disclosed open cells -- len-2 write announce (one ST_H + one row), len-4 read value (the st.d route couples to pcc2counter8), offset label per class, odd test values", "hold": null}, {"id": "pcc2latchinst", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "pcc2latchinst  harness: no fixture distinguishes a per-INSTANCE latch field from a function-static -- every probe session is a fresh process (pcc2 warned + warned_unimpl)", "hold": null}, {"id": "m88kexcdeliver", "state": "in work", "sev": "high", "title": "m88kexcdeliver  the m88k exception core stops EVERY guest CPU for five trap-like exception classes (PRIVILEGE_VIOLATION, ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE, INTEGER_OVERFLOW, MISALIGNED_ACCESS, UNIMPLEMENTED_OPCODE) instead of delivering VBR + 8*vector -- one guest `divu rX,rY,r0' ends the emulation, from user mode", "hold": null}, {"id": "lunaabsentcpu", "state": "closed", "sev": "high", "title": "lunaabsentcpu  dev_luna88k.c reassert_interrupts() calls the NULL interrupt callback of an UNCONFIGURED cpu slot: on the default one-cpu luna-88k two ordinary guest word stores (INT_ST_MASK1 then SOFT_INT1) kill the emulator process with SIGSEGV -- MEASURED, rung 3", "hold": null}, {"id": "m88kexcsfu1", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "m88kexcsfu1  the m88k exception core stops every guest CPU on SFU1_PRECISE (vector 114, 58 raise sites: any FP/mul/div with SFD1 set, or an FP divide by zero) and the latent SFU1_IMPRECISE, instead of delivering VBR+0x390/0x398", "hold": null}, {"id": "m88kbcndm6", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "m88kbcndm6  harness: the #323 bcnd detector (gate 11, BCND_ROWS in m88k_rounding_probe.py) has no row for mask 6, so `|| m5 == 6' on the generator's NULL-emission predicate returns a conforming encoding to `goto bad' and passes all seven rows", "hold": null}, {"id": "m88kidledup", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "m88kidledup  harness: the #380 idle-fold detector (m88k_idle_probe.py, gate anchor `idle counters: arm3 fired, slot==ntn, ntp=0, guard quiet') cannot prove exactly-once slot execution -- duplicating `ic[3].f(cpu, &ic[3]);' without duplicating `slot_runs++' keeps slot == ntn and the idempotent store, so it escapes", "hold": null}, {"id": "wdcbeoddtail", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "wdcbeoddtail  on a BIG-ENDIAN machine a 16-bit data-port read that pops the last valid byte plus the empty-ring sentinel folds the sentinel INTO the valid byte -- (v << 8) + 0xffff...ff = (v << 8) - 1, so the guest reads v-1 (0x00 becomes 0xff; a borrow can alter two valid bytes on a 4-byte read); the LE arm only pollutes the padding byte", "hold": null}, {"id": "wdcpktstatus", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "wdcpktstatus  a REJECTED or CHECK-CONDITION ATAPI packet command completes with the ERR bit CLEAR (status 0x50): `atapi_st->status' is never consulted, `d->error' stays 0, and the #416 CHECK CONDITION repair never reached the IDE/ATAPI path -- an out-of-range READ(10) is delivered as zeros with success", "hold": null}, {"id": "wdccylwrap", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "wdccylwrap  IDENTIFY word 1 truncates the cylinder count to 16 bits with no clamp: H=S=1 on a 32 MiB image gives 65,536 cylinders -> word 1 = 0; default 16/63 geometry wraps above 33,822,867,456 bytes; `row_selfconsistent' stops at 65,535 by design and its comment promised 'its own round' that was never filed", "hold": null}, {"id": "wdcidentorder", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "wdcidentorder  harness: swapping the two IDENTIFY transmit pushes byte-swaps every word the guest reads at ZERO differential failures -- the differential reads identify_struct directly; `row_command_fills_ring' checks occupancy (512), not bytes; the detector header said 'so it is filed' from #405 on with no row behind it", "hold": null}, {"id": "wdcstubtype", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "wdcstubtype  harness: the wdc differential's disk stubs ignore the bus `type' argument ((void) type in diskimage_exist/diskimage_access), so a wrong bus-type argument at the three call sites escapes", "hold": null}, {"id": "wdcgeomparse", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "wdcgeomparse  harness: the geometry differential's -ULLONG_MAX row covers the SPT position only, so a strtoll -> strtoull change at the HEADS parse site escapes", "hold": null}, {"id": "lbsmask", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "lbsmask  diskimage.c: `size & (logical_block_size - 1)' assumes a power-of-two block size at >= 3 sites (the #414 block names them); the vector is held behind `#ifdef GEOM_ROW_LBS257' in diff_diskimage_geom.c and was disclosed but never filed", "hold": null}, {"id": "wdcfloodprobe", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "wdcfloodprobe  harness: the #464 flood probe's `print r3' 'completion control' is printed and never parsed by any row; its `fire3' row comment claims every period N > 1 (it covers 2 and 3); its header says every row single-steps (false since #486's free-running runloop); the gate's fatal-call floor (>= 12) tolerates one fatal -> debug downgrade at HEAD's 13 sites", "hold": null}, {"id": "wdcdiagerr", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "wdcdiagerr  after EXECUTE DEVICE DIAGNOSTIC (and after a channel reset) dev_wdc.c sets `d->error = 1' -- the correct error-REGISTER diagnostic code (01h = no error detected) -- but `status_byte' ORs WDCS_ERR whenever d->error != 0, so the STATUS register shows ERR while the error register says 'no error'; upstream code", "hold": null}, {"id": "m88kdslotshadow", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "m88kdslotshadow  cpu_m88k.c: for a precise fault in the delay slot of a taken br.n the else-arm sets SNIP = slot+4, SFIP = target (upstream's 'Perhaps something like this could work'); the MC88100 UM says the shadows copy NIP = target, FIP = target+4 -- rte then executes slot+4 (skipped by the branch) before the target, or the target twice when target == slot+4", "hold": null}, {"id": "m88kexcfrozendmt", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "m88kexcfrozendmt  cpu_m88k.c m88k_exception(): the common path zeroes DMT0-2/DMD0-2/DMA0-2 (`Default to no memory transactions') OUTSIDE the update_shadow_regs guard, so a TRAP taken while shadowing is frozen clears the exception-time registers the manual says it leaves untouched", "hold": null}, {"id": "m88kexchwtraps", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "m88kexchwtraps  cpu_m88k.c: a supervisor tb0/tb1/tcnd naming vector 0, 1, 2 or 3 reaches the RESET/INTERRUPT/INSTRUCTION_ACCESS/DATA_ACCESS tail arms built for the HARDWARE event -- vector 0 dies in the RESET arm (the pass-1 finding); 1 does SNIP -= 4 (rte re-executes the trap forever); 2 sets SNIP = SFIP = 0 (rte to 0); 3 copies DMT from a stale dmt[]", "hold": null}, {"id": "m88kexcdebt", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "m88kexcdebt  #502's detector: disclosed clause-(e) coverage debt -- M_DELAYALWAYS (the post-delivery `cpu->delay_slot' else-branch is unpinned; needs a second UNFROZEN exception in one session), no rows for vectors 0..3's tail arms (see m88kexchwtraps; M_TAILOFF itself is EQUIVALENT for every vector), and the M_TRAPONLY / M_ISTRAPBOUNDS equivalence warrant (`implemented = is_trap' at BOUNDS is EQUIVALENT while no instruction raises vector 7 -- re-run when m88ktbnd lands)", "hold": null}, {"id": "m88kexctaildefault", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "m88kexctaildefault  DESIGN OPTION RECORD (D4 of #502, named by the pass-1 panel and NOT adopted): make m88k_exception()'s tail `default:' deliver every vector below 128 instead of stopping the cpu", "hold": null}, {"id": "m88ktbnd", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "m88ktbnd  cpu_m88k.c: `tbnd' (trap on bounds check) is DISASSEMBLY-ONLY (`case 0xf8' in the disassembler; no X(tbnd) handler), so no INSTRUCTION raises BOUNDS_CHECK_VIOLATION (7) -- only a supervisor tb0/tb1/tcnd naming 7 reaches it", "hold": null}, {"id": "m88kmxmkill", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "m88kmxmkill  cpu_m88k.c: writing PSR with the MXM (misaligned-access enable) bit set -- by stcr or by rte -- stops the cpu (`m88k_stcr' case M88K_CR_PSR); a supervisor guest reaches it with one instruction", "hold": null}, {"id": "m88ksfrzfatal", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "m88ksfrzfatal  cpu_m88k.c m88k_exception(): the SFRZ re-route's `fatal(\"[ SFRZ already set in PSR => ERROR ]\")' is an UNLATCHED guest-drivable diagnostic (supervisor-only: any non-trap fault inside a handler) -- a floodclass instance, reachable by five more classes since #502 (BOUNDS and ERROR are trap-only and bypass the branch; this title said seven until codex, pass 5)", "hold": null}, {"id": "asicplus2row", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "asicplus2row  dev_dreamcast_asic.c / regress/asic_badaddr_probe.py: the `+2' translation of the ASIC register index (`r = ((relative_addr / 4) + 2) & 3') is an ENUMERATED 14/14 total escape of the #463 detector and an UNDISCHARGED de-escalation obligation by the round's own membership test -- the warrant's `+2 reds safe 4' leg is false (safe 4 is survive-only) and +2 was never measured (only probe_Mplus1.raw exists)", "hold": null}, {"id": "mipstiny", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "mipstiny  cpu_mips_coproc.c fpu_subst_tiny(): does classifying tininess on the ENCODED destination word match the MIPS rule, or is after-rounding tininess judged with an UNRESTRICTED exponent range?  THE SOURCE ARGUES ONE WAY AND A REVIEWING SEAT THE OTHER -- unresolved, for the flagship seat", "hold": null}, {"id": "m88kexcrrtrapmode", "state": "closed", "sev": "med", "title": "m88k SFRZ re-route, TRAP side: MODE and SFD1 do not vary across the fourteen trap rows, so a widened inner guard escapes the 57-row detector", "hold": null}, {"id": "wdcconsumedattempts", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "dev_wdc.c ATAPI accounting: `consumed` counts helper CALLS, not successful pops -- over-counts when the input ring under-runs mid-access", "hold": null}, {"id": "lunancpus3", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "luna_absentcpu_probe boots only counts 1, 2 and 4 -- every ncpus-dependent fold is the identity at powers of two and escapes", "hold": null}, {"id": "m88ksfd1row", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "RULE QUESTION: does the addition licence authorise a zero-delta detector row that buys a distinguishing failure signature?", "hold": null}, {"id": "irqassertedunread", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "alpha, i960 and riscv set cd.<arch>.irq_asserted with no consumer -- a real gap, but two are non-executing ports (scaffold cleanup) and the third is Alpha port completion", "hold": null}, {"id": "briefforcedwrong", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "BRIEF_TEMPLATE's closing question asserts that a premise must be wrong -- an unsupported assumption when they are sound", "hold": null}, {"id": "sgirandompixel", "state": "closed", "sev": "high", "title": "dev_sgi_re.c horrible_getputpixel(): DE_MODE_LIN_B -- a DOCUMENTED NetBSD mode -- falls to the default arm, which assigns *color = random(); a copy then writes that random value to the destination", "hold": null}, {"id": "codexsubjectrefusal", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "codex content-filter refusal survived neutralisation -- the trigger is the REACH-THE-DEFECT framing, not the subject; and its signature reads as an ANSWER through panel.sh", "hold": null}, {"id": "sgirandomskipput", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "dev_sgi_re.c: a DECLINED pixel GET still lets the caller perform its dependent PUT, so the destination is written with fg", "hold": null}, {"id": "sgirelinb", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "dev_sgi_re.c: DE_MODE_LIN_B is documented in crmfbreg.h and not implemented -- and implementing it as LIN_A-with-the-other-array was REFUSED for want of a warrant", "hold": null}, {"id": "sgirandomcolour", "state": "in work", "sev": "high", "title": "dev_sgi_re.c: the COLOUR-mode PUT default arm writes FOUR random() bytes to guest memory, and DE_MODE_TYPE_YCRCB -- a DOCUMENTED mode -- reaches it (verified: YCRCB appears 0 times in the file)", "hold": null}, {"id": "sgitileptrsilent", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "dev_sgi_re.c: the tileptr high-bit arm declines SILENTLY -- the sibling of sgirandompixel's arm, with no announce at all", "hold": null}, {"id": "sgirenamedstmode", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "dev_sgi_re.c: the unimplemented-mode diagnostic says `dst_mode' although the same parameter is passed as src_mode on a GET", "hold": null}, {"id": "sgirebufuninit", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "dev_sgi_re.c horrible_getputpixel(): uint8_t buf[8] is never initialised and the guest picks how many of its bytes are used -- uninitialised host stack reaches guest-visible memory on PUT and *color on GET", "hold": null}, {"id": "sgiwidthwiredobl", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "sgi_re_width_probe.py's contract states an obligation ('MUST BE GATE-WIRED -- unwired, that coverage exists nowhere') that was DISCHARGED in the same commit that wrote it", "hold": null}, {"id": "sgirergbroundtrip", "state": "closed", "sev": "med", "title": "dev_sgi_re.c: the RGB colour arms are OFF BY ONE BYTE POSITION -- PUT writes buf[0..2], GET reads buf[1..3], so a stored colour reads back shifted", "hold": null}, {"id": "sgimtedepth4dead", "state": "in work", "sev": "high", "title": "dev_sgi_re.c do_mte_transfer(): `if (depth == 4)' is UNREACHABLE, so the MTE path ALWAYS constructs CI colour -- which makes every MTE fill at MTE_DEPTH_16/32 write 2 or 4 bytes with only buf[0] assigned.  The dead branch is the MECHANISM of [[sgirebufuninit]]'s strongest reachability, not a curiosity", "hold": null}, {"id": "sgigbeconvover", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "dev_sgi_gbe.c DEVICE_TICK: the tile-line conversion loop runs to 512 while memory_rw filled only `len' bytes -- an uninitialised read whose results are computed and then discarded", "hold": null}, {"id": "m88kexcdetectorl2", "state": "open", "sev": "high", "title": "m88k_exc_probe.py: MEASURED clean sweep -- deleting `implemented = true' at DATA_ACCESS or RESET scores 59/59 with ZERO row diffs.  The COVERAGE GAP is measured; whether it grades as an L2 floor breach or clause-(e) debt is a RULE QUESTION for the 2026-09-09 adjudication", "hold": null}, {"id": "codexbriefscoping", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "RULE QUESTION: BRIEF_TEMPLATE gained a C5 scoping codex's reading away from regress/artifacts and the ledger -- a template change made on measurement, owing adjudication", "hold": null}, {"id": "lcaisalane", "state": "in work", "sev": "high", "title": "dev_lca.c DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_isa): THREE defects in eleven lines -- an off-by-one guard that lets a bus-hitting read return all zeros, a discarded downstream failure result, and an uninitialised byte consumed on that failure", "hold": null}, {"id": "lcashiftub", "state": "in work", "sev": "low", "title": "dev_lca.c: 0x800 << dev with dev reaching 20 evaluates 2^31 in a signed 32-bit int -- undefined behaviour, in the loop the fork's own comment treats as the safe path", "hold": null}, {"id": "alphapfnoffset", "state": "in work", "sev": "high", "title": "memory_alpha.c alpha_translate_v2p(): the page-table walk returns PFN << pageSizeShift, dropping the within-page offset, while memory_rw derives its offset from that value -- every walked access would land at its page base", "hold": null}, {"id": "alphavmkill", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "memory_alpha.c: a guest virtual address of 0xfffffffd80000000 ends the host process -- fatal() + exit(1) on a specific vaddr, inside the not-yet-implemented VM path", "hold": null}, {"id": "alphafaultclaim", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "the fork's claim that a failed Alpha page-table walk makes the caller raise a fault is UNSUPPORTED by the code -- the access is abandoned with no Alpha exception delivered", "hold": null}, {"id": "alphaswpctxreg", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "cpu_alpha_palcode.c swpctx returns the old context in a0 while the ABI wrapper in alpha_cpu.h reads it from v0", "hold": null}, {"id": "alphaswpctxptbr", "state": "in work", "sev": "high", "title": "cpu_alpha_palcode.c swpctx: apcb_ptbr is assigned TWICE -- the second write puts new_flags into the PAGE TABLE BASE REGISTER, and apcb_flags is never assigned at all", "hold": null}, {"id": "alphatbinegsel", "state": "open", "sev": "high", "title": "cpu_alpha_palcode.c tbi: the selector is compared as UNSIGNED, so every negative selector takes the VADDR branch where the header requires all-entry and all-per-process invalidation", "hold": null}, {"id": "alphactxfail", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "cpu_alpha_palcode.c swpctx: the context save and load ignore every memory-access failure, and this fork zeroes read buffers on a failed translation -- so an ignored failure becomes committed zero-valued PCB state", "hold": null}, {"id": "sgilinearnegidx", "state": "closed", "sev": "low", "title": "*** WITHDRAWN -- FALSE FINDING. *** dev_sgi_re.c case 4 does NOT admit a negative index: `tile_nr' is UNSIGNED, so a negative x converts to a huge value that the `>= 32' guard REJECTS.  Kept as a record of the error, not as a defect", "hold": null}, {"id": "sgirespanchk", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "dev_sgi_re.c: the TLB_A/B/C and LINEAR_A/B write loops bound only the FIRST element of a multi-element span -- a handler ROBUSTNESS gap, with the alignment that makes it unreachable VERIFIED in the CPU load/store path rather than assumed", "hold": null}, {"id": "sgilinearnegidxpostmortem", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "RULE QUESTION: a chain resting on an index or a bound should quote the DECLARATION of every variable in it -- raised by the withdrawal of a HIGH finding that was false because I never read a type three lines above the code I quoted", "hold": null}, {"id": "sgirgbscanoutrow", "state": "open", "sev": "low", "title": "#505's arm-attribution warrant is a claim about dev_sgi_gbe.c's scanout that NO row observes -- the detector runs the drawing engine only and has no framebuffer oracle", "hold": null}, {"id": "sgirebufshiftub", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "title": "dev_sgi_re.c: FOUR of the five GET arms compute `buf[i] << 24` on a uint8_t, which promotes to signed int -- for any byte >= 128 that is a signed left-shift overflow, i.e. UB, on all four arm