codex assess *** VERDICT: REFUTE THE 170-SITE / PER-DEVICE PROGRAMME. Close `exitsweep` as an INVENTORY / INSTRUMENT row once its records are discharged; keep fixes in separate semantic batches. *** *** THE DENOMINATOR WAS STALE BY MORE THAN 2x, AND I RE-MEASURED IT HERE RATHER THAN TAKING IT: *** running `census_exits.py` on HEAD `733a4b6` reports **360** terminating sites -- 90 ACCESS, 101 HELPER, 169 INIT; 177 direct and 183 macro-mediated. The row's title said 170. Corrected. **AND THE LIST IS HETEROGENEOUS, WHICH MATTERS MORE THAN THE COUNT.** The census includes `exit(any status)`, `abort()`, `CHECK_ALLOCATION` and `FAILURE` -- not only `exit(1)` -- and it contains known NON-defects: the intended `dev_cons.c:80` `exit(0)`, the unreachable `dev_ram.c:132`, and init failures where terminating is CORRECT. The script's own output says it plainly: *'This counts SITES, not defects.'* So '170 guest-reachable exit(1) defects' was never an honest work list, at either number. *** ON THE DE-ESCALATION COST, IT SEPARATED THREE DENOMINATORS THIS QUEUE HAS ALREADY CONFUSED ONCE: *** the clause fires when a DIFF removes a host-fatal guard and makes the operation survive -- **inventory membership alone does not fire it**, and sites kept as legitimate init failures or converted to an honest emulator stop are not fatal->survive conversions at all. For real conversions the cost is ONE MUTANT PER DE-FATALISED PREDICATE, which the carrier already says is 'not per round or necessarily per call site'. **Predicate count, call-site count and accept-row count are three different numbers**: one predicate can serve several case labels, one predicate can need several legal-value rows, and write-to-clear or read-only values cannot carry a readback accept row at all. **A different obligation IS per fixed source site**: floor clause (b) requires the detector to fail on a verbatim reinstatement at EVERY fixed site, so a shared detector may exercise them together but every site needs an attributable kill mapping. IT ALSO FOUND AN UNDISCHARGED OBLIGATION ON THE SHIPPED WORK: `cf4b083` predates the de-escalation clause by two days. Its C2 row proves a legal `0xfc000000` write lands, but the record does not show that row measured against the later rule's guard-growth mutant -- so the accept row exists and its FAILABILITY is unproven. Line numbers checked and agreeing with the fork (luna88k case at 805, shared `cpunr` at 816, predicate at 818, and the remaining `exit(1)` at 1038 is init-time) -- no wrong-tree reading.

