1: #!/usr/bin/env python3
2: """pcc2defaultkill DETECTOR (#501) -- the `default:' arm of DEVICE_ACCESS(pcc2) in dev_pcc2.c.
3: 
4: Before #501 that arm ended the emulator process (`exit(1)') on ANY access to an offset with no `case'
5: -- READS included, although the memcpy above the switch had already served them.  Forty-three of the
6: 0x40 byte offsets reach it (derived from mvme_pcctworeg.h against the case list): eighteen NAMED
7: registers the model does not implement (PSCALECNT, GPIO_ICR/PCR, SCCMOIACK, SCCTXIACK, IEERR, IEICR,
8: IEBERR, SCSIERR, the printer block, SPEED, PRTDATA), the bytes INSIDE the four 32-bit counter/compare
9: words, and the unnamed offsets.  A single guest `st.b' or `ld.bu' at any of them was a host kill
10: (rung-3 witness, 7/13 on the committed build: regress/artifacts/pcc2defaultkill/witness_20260905/).
11: 
12: THE FIX (pass-1 panel of 2026-09-05, nine seats, converged): READ side -- keep the byte the memcpy
13: already served (the register array's CURRENT byte -- the tick's, inside a running COUNT word: the announce says so);
14: WRITE side -- DECLINE + a per-offset latched `fatal()' ("(once)", the house idiom), NOT a backed
15: accept: the file's own cased siblings contradict a blanket store (SCCERR is write-to-clear, the ICRs
16: mask and reassert, PSCALECNT/SPEED/the IACKs are read-only), the addition licence is unmet (no guest
17: reaches these offsets on this model's inspected driver paths -- if_ie never attaches), and a store into pcctwo_reg[] from the
18: one arm reached by 43 offsets adds a bounded-write surface the decline does not have.
19: 
20: CONTRACT -- what this detector is built to catch, by row (46 rows: 23 at pass 2, +13 by the pass-2 repair, +10 by the
21: pass-3 repair; every pass-3 row was APPENDED to an existing session):
22:   member 6 on the READ side of the latched path -- a read whose bit is already set gets its RESPONSE zeroed (codex
23:       M_LATCH_RD_ZERO: every rowed read after a preceding access expected 0 anyway)
24:                                                     -> `srvd rd latch' (0x34 after a read set the bit), `srvd wr latch'
25:                                                        (0x78 after a write set it)
26:   the announce's width label constant (`(int) len' -> `4', codex: no byte row required `(len 1)')
27:                                                     -> `srvd val text' (`0x5 (len 1) -- served 0x34'), `wr val text'
28:                                                        (`0x30 (len 1): 0x5a'), `srvd half text' (`(len 2)')
29:   (b) the verbatim `exit(1)' reinstated             -> every survival row (named/inword/unnamed wr AND rd, alias, wide)
30:   (d) the announce unlatched                        -> `latch sameoff' (3 lines, not 1: two writes and the appended
31:                                                        read-back; the row said 2 until pass 4 -- measured 3 under M_UNLATCH)
32:   (d) one whole-arm latch bit instead of per-offset -> `latch twooff' (1 line, not 2), `named wr msg' (1, not 18)
33:   (d) the latch SET conditioned on writes (grok A)  -> `latch rd rd' (2, not 1), `latch rd wr' (2, not 1): with
34:       `latch sameoff' (W,W) and `latch rw one' (W,R) these are ALL FOUR setter x tester direction cells
35:   (d) the latch SET conditioned on OFFSET CLASS (Opus M_LATCHOFF, MEASURED 36/36)
36:                                                     -> `inword msg' (12), `unnamed msg' (13): a second write to one
139: def enc(op26, rd, rs1, imm): return (op26 << 26) | (rd << 21) | (rs1 << 16) | (imm & 0xffff)
140: def LD_BU(rd, off): return enc(0x03, rd, 2, off)
141: def LD_W(rd, off): return enc(0x05, rd, 2, off)
142: def ST_B(rs, off): return enc(0x0b, rs, 2, off)
143: def ST_W(rs, off): return enc(0x09, rs, 2, off)
144: def LD_HU(rd, off): return enc(0x02, rd, 2, off)   # pass 3: a len-2 default read
145: def ST_H(rs, off): return enc(0x0a, rs, 2, off)    # pass 3: a len-2 default write
147: def program(insns, at=CODE):
149:     return ["put w 0x%x, 0x%08x" % (at + 4 * k, w) for k, w in enumerate(words)] + ["pc=0x%x" % at, "step %d" % len(insns)]
