{"id": "n6", "title": "n6  The record says the footbridge host-halts were made graceful; the guest-reachable kills are not", "state": "held", "state_note": "*** RANKING AND ONE REFUTED DOUBT, 2026-08-27. ***\n\n**Ranked 6th of 8** in today's adjudication -- loud, guest-drivable host termination, placed BELOW the silent interrupt-fidelity rows, following this project's own revealed ordering (a loud-termination row already sits below a lost-interrupt row in the queue). **The adjudicator flagged this as its softest call**: a pure denial-of-service view would lift it above an INFERRED hang, since a guest reading a documented register ends the emulator process. Recorded as a flagged call, not a settled one.\n\n*** THE ADJUDICATOR DOUBTED THE ACCEPT PREMISE ON THE GROUND THAT NO DC21285 MANUAL IS IN `_scratchpad/` -- AND THAT IS REFUTED. *** The manual is present: `dc21285.pdf` (1,059,970 bytes) and `dc21285.txt` (445,677 bytes). The statement was verified twice, from two channels:\n  * Table 7-10, `dc21285.txt:6826` -- `188h    IRQEnable    IRQEnableSet`;\n  * prose at `:6913-6915` -- *\"**This read-only register** is used to mask the interrupt input sources... The value of this register can only be changed by writing to the EnableSet and EnableClear registers.\"*\n\n> **The doubt's ROOT CAUSE is the defect this round already fixed:** the carrier's primary-sources list named about seven manuals when fifteen are present, and this datasheet was one of the six it omitted. **A seat checking that list would conclude the manual is absent.** That is the stale-inventory failure reaching one step further than expected -- it did not just stop a round finding the manual, it caused a later seat to doubt a correct finding drawn from it.\n\n**THE THREE OBLIGATIONS OF THE ACCEPT ROUND STAND, and the adjudication sharpened one:**\n  1. **re-point the probe's failability control** -- `+0x188` is row K1, the probe's only failing arm; there are **eight other** guest-reachable footbridge `exit(1)` sites to move it to (IRQ_STATUS / RAW write, ENABLE_CLEAR read, FIQ_STATUS write, SDRAM_BA_MASK / OFFSET read);\n  2. **a non-vacuous ACCEPT row** -- it must prove the read returns `irq_enable` post-fix **and redden under a guard-growth mutant** (return 0, or the wrong register). *'Returns some value' is vacuous*, and the reset value is 0, which is what an absent device returns;\n  3. the premise itself -- **discharged above.**\n\n*** EVIDENCE 2026-08-27, moved here from [[devexit]] where I had filed it on the wrong row, and then CONFIRMED and SHARPENED by an adversarial pass that changed the round's whole shape. ***\n\n**THE KILL IS REAL AND UNCONDITIONAL.** `dc21285reg.h:335` gives `IRQ_ENABLE_SET 0x188`; `dev_footbridge.c:578-591` has, on the `MEM_READ` branch:\n```c\n} else {\n        odata = d->irq_enable;\n        fatal(\"[ WARNING: footbridge read from ENABLE SET? ]\");\n        exit(1);\n}\n```\nNo state precondition -- `d->irq_enable`'s value is irrelevant to reaching it. The only rewrite of `relative_addr` before the switch is the timer fold at `:507-510`, gated to `0x300..0x36C`, so `0x188` passes through untouched. CATS maps footbridge at `0x42000000` unmodified (`machine_cats.c:63-64`), `DM_DEFAULT`, so every access reaches the handler. **The MMU-off precondition is load-bearing, not decoration:** under the boot translation table (`cpu_arm.c:239-255`) only the low 256 MB is mapped with the top VA nibble ignored, so `VA 0x42000000` aliases to `PA 0x02000000` and returns 0.\n\n*** THE DATASHEET REFUTES THE FIX I HAD IN MIND, AND IT WAS IN `_scratchpad/` ALL ALONG. *** `_scratchpad/dc21285.pdf` / `.txt` (Intel 278115-001) has been present since 2026-08-15 and is **not listed in the carrier's 'Primary sources actually present' section.** Table 7-10 (`dc21285.txt:6823-6833`), calibrated on three straddling rows rather than one point:\n```\n  Address     Read           Write\n  180h        IRQStatus      --\n  184h        IRQRawStatus   --\n  188h        IRQEnable      IRQEnableSet\n  18Ch        --             IRQEnableClear\n```\nAll three neighbours match what the code already does -- it kills on exactly the `--` sides at 180h-write, 184h-write and 18Ch-read -- so the 188h row is trustworthy. The unstaggered prose (`:6911-6935`) confirms: *\"IRQEnable/FIQEnable Register - Offset 188h/288h. **This read-only register** is used to mask the interrupt input sources... Reset value: 0.\"*\n\n> *** SO A GUEST READ OF `+0x188` IS A DOCUMENTED, LEGAL, READ-ONLY ACCESS THAT THE MODEL ALREADY ANSWERS CORRECTLY (`odata = d->irq_enable`) AND THEN KILLS THE HOST ANYWAY. *** The right answer is computed and then discarded.\n\n*** THIS PRODUCES A **FOURTH MEMBER** OF THE DE-FATALISATION OPTION SET, WHICH THE CARRIER'S THREE-MEMBERED RULE DOES NOT CONTAIN: **ACCEPT.** *** delete / decline / decline+bounded-announce all presume the access is unserviceable. Here it is serviceable and already served, so the disposition is **delete the `fatal()`+`exit(1)` outright -- no latch, no message** -- and keep the value. Transferring the `m8820x_unimplemented()` warrant would have silently imported the premise that the read is unserviceable, which the code refutes on its own line, and would have shipped a **wrong record in product code**: printing *'unimplemented; ignored'* about a read that is implemented and is not ignored.\n\n**Four further reasons the m8820x template misfits, each measured:** its latch is a documented-defective template (ONE `reported_offset` flag covers FOUR kinds, per-kind latch FILED not done -- and footbridge has EIGHT sites across SIX kinds, so it imports the known defect at twice the scale); its marker `(reported once)` is the tree's MINORITY spelling and **both** uses are in `dev_m8820x.c` itself; and there is **no transferable flood measurement** -- m8820x's latch is warranted by 640,016 CMMU_SCR writes in 132 s on a rig that boots, while `regress/images.md` lists NO cats rig and `REVIEW_FINDINGS.md:171,:186` record that a real NetBSD/cats boot hits **none** of the eight sites.\n\n*** THE EIGHT SITES ARE TWO SEMANTIC CLASSES, NOT ONE, so the reopening rule SPLITS them: *** `0x188` read is a **correctness defect** (right answer computed, then discarded); the other seven -- including `IRQ_ENABLE_CLEAR` read at `:604`, and the write sides of `IRQ_STATUS`, `IRQ_RAW_STATUS`, `FIQ_STATUS`, `FIQ_RAW_STATUS` -- are **declinations of accesses the datasheet marks `--`**, which IS the m8820x class. One round cannot cover both.\n\n*** THE COST THAT MAKES THIS A REAL ROUND RATHER THAN A ONE-LINE DELETE: `0x188` IS THE PROBE'S ONLY FAILABILITY CONTROL. *** `footbridge_sites_probe.py`'s own header says so -- *\"an UNFIXED site, used here rather than repaired, because a probe that reports SURVIVED for every row is vacuous unless something in it can still die.\"* **Fixing `0x188` turns row K1 RED and takes gate 14 with it.** The round therefore owes, IN-ROUND:\n  1. re-point arm K to another still-unfixed site -- `+0x18c`, `0xE590718C`, fatal text `\"ENABLE CLEAR\"` -- a one-word change; and\n  2. a de-escalation ACCEPT row for `0x188`.\n\n**And that accept row is VACUOUS if it only READS `0x188`:** after `memset`, `d->irq_enable == 0`, and `0` is exactly what an absent device returns -- the precise trap this probe's header records surviving once before (*returned 0x0 on every device row WITH ITS RAM CONTROL STILL GREEN*). **It must `str` a known value to `+0x188` first** (the write arm ORs it in; with `irq_status == 0` the follow-on `INTERRUPT_DEASSERT` is inert), then read it back and assert that value.\n\n**Propagation note: `dev_footbridge.c` is byte-identical in `est/` and is NOT on `tools/est_divergence.txt`, so any edit must land in BOTH trees.**\n\n"}
{"id": "m88kexit", "title": "m88kexit  memory_m88k.c still calls exit(1) on the CMMU_PFSR_SUCCESS internal-error branch -- the host-kill shape #433 removed from this same device", "state": "held", "state_note": "*** EVIDENCE 2026-08-27: the CALLS EXIST (claim true) but BOTH SITES ARE UNREACHABLE. This is dormant defensive code, NOT a live host kill -- a real severity downgrade, and the point of establishing it is to stop a round being opened on 'a surviving exit(1)'. ***\n\n`src/cpus/memory_m88k.c` contains exactly **two** `exit(1)` calls, both inside the `switch (pfsr_status)` that runs after the `exception:` label:\n  * `:390-394` -- `case CMMU_PFSR_SUCCESS:`, whose own diagnostic reads *\"HUH? CMMU_PFSR_SUCCESS:, but exception? TODO\"*;\n  * `:408-413` -- `default:`, *\"Internal error in memory_m88k?\"*.\n\n**Both are internal-invariant assertions, and the invariant holds structurally.** `pfsr_status` is initialised to `CMMU_PFSR_SUCCESS` at `:118`, which is what makes the first case look live. But the exception block is reached ONLY through the `exception:` label at `:373`, and:\n\n  * there are **seven** `goto exception` sites -- `:173`, `:213`, `:267`, `:275`, `:300`, `:309`, `:341` -- and **each is preceded, ONE LINE ABOVE, by a fault assignment** at `:172`, `:212`, `:266`, `:274`, `:299`, `:308`, `:340`. Seven gotos, seven assignments, gap of 1 in every case.\n  * **control cannot FALL THROUGH into the label**: `:370` is `return (accumulated_flags & PG_RO)? 1 : 2;`, immediately above it.\n\nSo `pfsr_status` is never `CMMU_PFSR_SUCCESS` at `:388`, and the `default:` arm is unreachable for the same reason -- the seven assigned values are exactly WRITE, SFAULT, SUPER and PFAULT, and all four have their own cases at `:397-406`.\n\n**DISPOSITION: assessed, NOT a live defect, and it ranks BELOW anything guest-drivable.** The fork de-fatalises sites a guest can drive; these are sites a guest cannot reach without a code change. That is the distinction the queue ordering depends on, and the row previously read as though this were an ordinary surviving host kill.\n\n**WHAT WOULD RE-OPEN IT, stated so the absence record is falsifiable:** any new `goto exception` that does not assign `pfsr_status` first, or any new writer of `pfsr_status`. The check is the same one run here and it is mechanical -- **the seven gotos and seven assignments must stay paired.** If that pairing is ever broken, the `SUCCESS` case becomes live and a guest-triggered fault kills the host.\n\n*Method note: the pairing was derived by matching each `goto exception` to the nearest preceding non-SUCCESS assignment, then confirming no fall-through path into the label. Both halves are needed -- the gap check alone would miss a fall-through, and the fall-through check alone would miss an unassigned goto.*\n\n"}
{"id": "sgirekills", "title": "dev_sgi_re.c: SIX active exit(1) statements remain -- the census is DERIVED 2026-09-08 by stripping comments, and supersedes the stale \"twelve / eleven guest-reachable\" headline; the six-row oracle re-pin this row was organised around is ALREADY DISCHARGED (the probe has no `ld kill` rows left)", "state": "open", "sev": "high", "state_note": "OPEN, and the committed artifact in `witness` is the REPRODUCTION, not the fix.  `regress/sgi_re_width_probe.py` shipped with #497 and carries SIX `ld kill` rows that assert THE HOST STILL DIES on an 8-byte access at each of the six windows -- they pin the defect, they do not repair it.  #497 de-fatalised the WIDTH guard only; the width test is evaluated FIRST, so a legal `ld` still reaches the kill.  *** THE ORDERING OBLIGATION IS ALREADY RECORDED IN #497's ROUND BLOCK AND IS THE REASON THIS ROW MUST NOT BE CLOSED BY THE CHECKER: fixing sgirekills will turn a GREEN GATE RED BY SUCCEEDING, six rows' worth. ***  It therefore inherits the oracle-correction ordering -- re-pin the six rows FIRST, measure the committed build RED under the new expectations, THEN edit the code.  A row whose own detector must invert is exactly the case the shipped-work heuristic cannot read.\n\n*** COUNT CHECK 2026-09-06, AND MY FIRST CORRECTION WAS ALSO WRONG.  THE LIVE COUNT IS TWELVE. ***\n\nThree numbers have now been attached to this row: the title says TWENTY-ONE, my correction an hour ago said\nNINETEEN, and the live count is **TWELVE**.\n\n    grep -c 'exit(1)'                    19   <- ALL occurrences, comments included\n    grep -cE '//[[:space:]]*exit\\(1\\)'    4   <- commented-out kills (:218, :227, :689, :1251)\n    (three more sit inside comment PROSE at :321, :325, :945, discussing exit(1))\n    grep -cE '^[[:space:]]+exit\\(1\\);'    12   <- LIVE, statement position\n\n*** THE INSTRUCTIVE PART IS WHY I BELIEVED 19, BECAUSE IT IS A NEW COROLLARY OF A RULE THIS PROJECT ALREADY\nHAS. ***  I wrote that `grep -c 'exit('` and `grep -c 'exit(1)'` BOTH returned 19, and treated the agreement\nas ruling out the very error that had just made `lcabadval`'s enumeration wrong -- a commented-out kill\ncounted as live.  **Both greps share the same blindness: neither excludes `//`.**  The carrier says when two\nderivers DISAGREE neither is trustworthy until you look at what each matched.  The corollary, learned here:\n**when two derivers AGREE that is not evidence either, if they can fail the same way.**  Agreement between\nmethods is worth exactly the independence of the methods -- which is the unanimity-on-a-brief-supplied-fact\nrule, one level down and pointed at greps instead of seats.\n\nAnd it is the third instance tonight of *a correction is not safer than the thing it corrects*: `#503`'s\npass-3 refutation displaced a truer sentence, `83df95c` sharpened a vague claim while reading as though it\nfixed a false one, and this correction replaced a wrong number with another wrong number.\n\n**THE TITLE IS LEFT AS WRITTEN AND IS NOT SILENTLY RE-GRADED**: 21 may have been right when the row was\nopened, with sites removed since -- four of the nineteen are commented out, which is consistent with someone\nhaving disabled them.  A round on this file DERIVES the count from the statement-position pattern above\nbefore acting, and does not trust any of the three numbers in this row.\n\n\n*** MAIN-LOOP RECORD 2026-09-07 (triage stage) -- written into state_note, NOT into `entries'. ***\n`entries' is the SEAT ANSWER list: check_stage_panels.py counts entries to decide whether a stage's\nnine seats were fired, and gen_dashboard.py REFUSES TO RENDER on a seat id no column declares.  A\nmain-loop note parked there is not merely mis-filed -- it MANUFACTURES A SEAT ANSWER THAT NEVER\nHAPPENED, which is the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape inverted.  Seven such entries were\nwritten during this session and all seven were migrated here when the generator refused.\n\nL4 -- TITLE CORRECTED.  The state_note has carried the derived count of **TWELVE** since 2026-09-06, and the\nTITLE still said TWENTY-ONE.  **A note that corrects a title does not correct the title**, and the title is\nwhat every queue listing, dashboard row and triage pass shows first -- so the corrected figure was sitting\none field away from the wrong one, losing every time.\n\nOld title, kept because the quote-what-you-refute rule applies to my own records too:\n  \"dev_sgi_re.c carries 21 live terminating calls -- 15 of them inside a DEVICE_ACCESS and 13 in DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_re) alone -- and the six READ arms are all guest-reachable\"\n\nRe-derived at HEAD before this edit: `grep -cE '^[[:space:]]+exit\\(1\\);' src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c' = **12**,\nagainst 19 raw hits.  The new title NAMES ITS DERIVER, per the counts rule, so the next reader re-runs it\ninstead of trusting it -- which is the whole remedy for a class that has now had three generations on this\none row.\n\n**The DEVICE_ACCESS half of the old title (`15 of them inside a DEVICE_ACCESS ... 13 in DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_re)\nalone') IS DELIBERATELY NOT REPLACED WITH A NEW NUMBER**, because [[exitcensus]] is open against exactly that\nattribution: the census's construct split *\"attributed every call in a plain non-static function to the\npreceding DEVICE_ACCESS -- inflating the bucket used to rank reachability\"*.  Quoting a fresh figure out of a\ntool with an open defect against the very field I am quoting would be the same error in a newer coat.  The\nreachability claim is left to the round that does the work.\n\n*** MAIN-LOOP RECORD 2026-09-07 (assess stage) -- written into state_note, NOT into `entries'. ***\n`entries' is the SEAT ANSWER list: check_stage_panels.py counts entries to decide whether a stage's\nnine seats were fired, and gen_dashboard.py REFUSES TO RENDER on a seat id no column declares.  A\nmain-loop note parked there is not merely mis-filed -- it MANUFACTURES A SEAT ANSWER THAT NEVER\nHAPPENED, which is the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape inverted.  Seven such entries were\nwritten during this session and all seven were migrated here when the generator refused.\n\nPREP (no code touched) -- the RE-PIN DESIGN, taken from the sibling that already did this rather than\ninvented.  `#499' re-pinned the neighbouring `other kill' row in this same probe when it removed the\ncatch-all kill, and its comment records that **BOTH obvious oracles were MEASURED VACUOUS before the third\nwas chosen**.  That is a transferable warrant, not a suggestion, because this row inverts the same way.\n\n**WHAT THE SIX ROWS ASSERT TODAY** -- `row(nm + \" ld kill\", how + (\"+txt\" if ok_txt else \"-txt\"),\n\"exit1+txt\")', i.e. the host DIES and the per-site literal `TODO: read from <sym>' appears.  Fixing the six\nread kills turns all six RED **by succeeding**, which is why the row carries the ordering obligation.\n\n**THE TWO ORACLES `#499` MEASURED VACUOUS -- do not re-propose either:**\n  * **DEATH-ONLY** (`how == \"exit1\"'): observes THAT it killed, never WHICH kill.  A message change leaving\n    `exit(1)' standing scored **35/35** against a matched control at 34/35.  Filed as [[otherkilldeath]].\n  * **`exit0`**: exit status cannot separate *declined, then quit* from *died with status 0*.  The variant\n    `mexit0' scores GREEN on an `exit0+txt' oracle while `alive=False'.  Filed as [[exit0oracle]].\n\n**THE THREE-CHANNEL SHAPE THAT SURVIVED, which the six should copy:**\n  * `how' -- the host survives;\n  * `txt' -- **ONE CONTIGUOUS literal including the address and data fields.**  Two independent substring\n    tests are a WEAKER claim: `-- declined.' is emitted by the six `#497' width declines and by `#498's\n    sgi_de decline too, so a bare substring cross-matches.  These six already have per-site symbols, which\n    is the natural discriminator;\n  * `v0' -- **a declined READ reaches the common tail and lands a DEFINED 0.**  `v0' is preset to SENTINEL\n    by `load()', so *wrote odata (0)* and *never landed* are distinguishable.  This is the channel that kills\n    `mret0' (return 0 -> memory_rw coerces to -1 -> EXCEPTION_DBE) and `mrdexit0' (exit(0) in the READ branch\n    only), **neither of which any write-only oracle can see** -- and these six ARE read arms, so this channel\n    is not optional here.\n\n**ORDER, per the oracle-correction rule:** re-pin the six FIRST, measure the COMMITTED build RED under the new\nexpectations, THEN edit `dev_sgi_re.c'.  `#499' additionally showed the stronger form -- its oracle had been\nPRE-REGISTERED six hours before its own baseline -- and this row inherits that: the design above is being\nwritten down BEFORE the round starts, in a dated entry, for exactly that reason.\n\n**Cite the sites by SYMBOL, never by line.**  The probe's own header lists `dev_sgi_re.c lines 245 262 279\n296 346 362'; `#504' inserted ~40 lines above them, so those numbers are already stale.  The probe carries a\n`SITES' table of symbolic names -- use it.\n\n*** CENSUS + RANKING, 2026-09-07 (codex, `regress/artifacts/sgirekills/codex_census_20260907.txt`).\nTHE ANSWER TO 'WHICH ARE CHEAP' IS 'NONE ARE', AND THAT IS THE USEFUL PART. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"The count is twelve.  ELEVEN ARE GUEST-REACHABLE; one is unreachable.  **There are no\ntwo or three genuine one-line repairs already covered by success tests.**  The cheapest plausible\nfunctional pair is LINEAR_A and LINEAR_B readback -- but both need corrected oracles.\"*\n\n**The count is CONFIRMED at twelve** (I re-ran the deriver before writing the brief; the title's 21\nand the intermediate 19 both counted comments).  **Eleven of twelve guest-reachable** is a stronger\nseverity statement than this row carried.\n\n*** I ASKED FOR THE CHEAP WINS AND WAS TOLD THERE ARE NONE.  Recorded as a NEGATIVE RESULT, because\nit stops the next round hunting for them. ***\n\n**THE COST RANKING, which gives a coherent round instead of a scattering:**\n\n    rank  site                        new latch bit   work\n    1     #12 dead MTE width branch   no              source cleanup only -- NO RUNTIME IMPROVEMENT\n    2=    #5  LINEAR_A read           no              replace the kill oracle with readback\n    2=    #6  LINEAR_B read           no                assertions; extend EXISTING sessions\n    3=    #1  TLB_A read              no              reuse existing sessions; add returned-value,\n    3=    #2  TLB_B read              no                packing and cell-selection assertions\n    3=    #3  TLB_C read              no\n    4=    #7  STIPPLE                 ONE             new targeted MTE rows AND session scenario\n    4=    #9  linear COPY             ONE             same\n    4=    #10 linear depth            ONE             same\n\n**RANKS 2 AND 3 ARE ONE CLASS: the five READ arms.**  No new latch bits, no new sessions, and they\nshare the oracle inversion this row already predicted -- the six `ld kill` rows in\n`regress/sgi_re_width_probe.py` that assert the host still dies.  **That is the round to run**, and it\nis the class-closure shape rather than a cell at a time: `#505` paid three floor breaches for\nrepairing cell-by-cell, and this row's sites are already grouped for me.\n\n**Rank 1 agrees with the separate `sgimtedepth4dead` audit**, which independently said DEFER and\nreduce that row to a comment correction -- two briefs, two seats, same disposition, reached from\ndifferent directions.\n\n**RANK 4 IS THE EXPENSIVE GROUP AND SHOULD NOT BE MIXED IN**: one latch bit each plus new MTE\nsessions.  Those are three separate rounds, not part of the read-arm round.\n\n*** GROK DESIGN PASS 2026-09-07.  I ASKED IT TO ASSUME THE THREE `lcabadval` ERRORS WERE REPEATED\nHERE.  ALL THREE WERE. ***\n\n**1. THE CLASS COUNT IS WRONG IN THE HEADLINE, FOR THE FOURTH TIME ON THIS ROW.**  `grep -cE\n'^[[:space:]]+exit\\(1\\);'` gives 12 STATEMENTS, but `:1537` **cannot run**: it is the trailing `else`\nof `if (len == 4) ... else if (len != 4) ... else { fatal; exit(1); }` in `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_mte)` --\nan exhaustive pair with a third arm.  Already filed as [[sgimtewidth]].\n\n> **ELEVEN guest-reachable kills, in two classes: 6 DIRECTION + 5 MTE-feature.**  This row has now\n> carried 21 -> 19 -> 12 -> 11, and every correction was the same comment-or-reachability blindness.\n\n*Two more shapes sit beside the MTE five and are NOT among the twelve: `dst_y_step` fatals with its\n`exit` commented out, and the `dst_tlb` `default:` fatals and CONTINUES.  Same unimplemented-feature\nclass, different severity.  CLIP_IDS at `0x16e0` is a seventh NAMED RE window but is the `#499`\ncatch-all, not a seventh read kill.*\n\n*** 2. MY S3 WAS THE FLOOR-(a) MISS I ASKED ABOUT -- AND THE INSTRUCTION IN IT IS WRONG. ***\nI wrote that *\"a guest `lw` of each register kills the host on HEAD, so the rows are RED pre-fix by\nconstruction\"*.  **A `lw` does not kill: width is checked FIRST and `len != 8` DECLINES.**  The\nshipped detector already holds the counter-example -- `load()` defaults to `OP_LW_V0_AT` and every\n`lw surv` row wants `exit0/0x0`.  **The kill is `ld` (len = 8).**\n\n> **So a detector of six `lw` reads would be GREEN ON HEAD** -- precisely the miss I claimed this row\n> was immune to, arrived by a different door.  *The asymmetry I asserted between this row and\n> `lcabadval` was real in direction and wrong in fact.*\n\n**AND THE `lcabadval` ANALOGY IS THE WRONG CLASS ANYWAY:** those are WRITE VALUE-GUARDS, where legal\nwrites already survive; these are DIRECTION kills on a legal-width READ.  That difference is why S3\ncould look like the opposite of the `lcabadval` miss until `lw` is put in the sentence.\n\n**3. THE CITED PRECEDENT IS A FILED DEFECT, AND I CITED THE WRONG PROBE TOO.**\n  * S2 claimed *the model already stores the right thing* for all six.  **For TEX that warrant IS\n    [[sgitexindex]]**: `re_tex` is written with `((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3) & 0xff`, consecutive\n    `sd`s overlap by three, 81 of 112 cells are unreachable, and there are **zero consumers**.\n    Backing state exists; the correct answer does not.\n  * S4 cited `sgi_re_rgb_probe.py` -- which is uncommitted `#505` work that **never issues an RE\n    register read**.  The committed vehicle is `sgi_re_width_probe.py`, whose six `ld kill` rows ARE\n    the witness -- **and they invert the day this round succeeds.**\n  * I also mis-cited `#508` for a packing hazard; `#508` is `alphapfnoffset` (the page walk dropping\n    the offset).  The packing hazard here is real and is a different defect.\n\n*** THE DESIGN THAT SURVIVES: FIVE ACCEPT, TEX SPLITS, MTE STAYS OUT. ***\nOne SEMANTIC class (a legal `ld` into an implemented window dies) but TWO members:\n  * **TLB_A/B/C and LINEAR_A/B -> member 4 ACCEPT.**  Stores already land and consumers exist\n    (`horrible_getputpixel` for the three TLBs and LINEAR_A; the MTE linear fill for LINEAR_A/B).\n    One round, one detector, five value rows.\n  * **TEX -> DECLINE, do NOT mirror.**  A mirrored `>> 3` read is the identity only for *write one\n    slot, read it back, touch nothing else*: a store at `+8` writes cells `[1..4]` and one at `+16`\n    writes `[2..5]`, so a mirrored read at `+8` returns a MIX.  **Mirroring also plants a green\n    round-trip that `sgitexindex` must later invert.**\n\n**AND A FIVE-ARM ROUND MAY NOT CLAIM \"the RE read arms no longer kill\"** -- the contract has to name\nTEX as still fatal.\n\n**THE ROWS ACTUALLY OWED, which is not \"six survival rows\":**\n\n    5 packed `ld' round-trips, one per ACCEPT arm            floor (b)/(d), packing-sensitive\n    the same five at a slot with bit 3 SET *and* at the       near AND far bound; `+8' alone is\n      window's LAST 8-byte slot                               the `#497' write-accept shape\n    5 pre-write `ld' -> 0                                     the memset ABI, distinct from\n                                                              \"the stored value came back\"\n    TEX: a 3-channel decline (how / contiguous TODO or new    a different member\n      latch text / sentinel -> 0)\n    RE-PIN all six existing `ld kill' rows FIRST              oracle-correction: today they want\n                                                              exit1 + text\n\n*Survival-only is enough for verbatim `exit(1)` reinstatement and NOT enough for the null-repair.*\n\n*** THE grok DESIGN ANSWER WAS UNREAD FOR A DAY, AND IT REFUTES FIVE OF THE BRIEF'S PREMISES --\nINCLUDING ONE THAT WOULD HAVE SHIPPED A DETECTOR THAT IS GREEN ON HEAD.  Read in full and\nSPOT-CHECKED 2026-09-07; artifact `regress/artifacts/sgirekills/grok_design2_20260907.txt`. ***\n\n**This is the fourth recorded instance of the unread-review class in this project, and by some\nmargin the most consequential: the answer names a floor-clause-(a) failure in the detector the round\nwas about to build.**\n\n### 1.  *** THE ACCEPT ROWS WERE ABOUT TO USE THE WRONG INSTRUCTION.  VERIFIED, NOT TAKEN ON TRUST. ***\n\nThe brief's S3 said *\"a guest `lw` kills\"*.  **It does not.**  Measured on the tree today:\n\n    regress/sgi_re_width_probe.py:196   def load(A, addr, op=OP_LW_V0_AT):      <- defaults to `lw'\n    src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:421,440,459    if (len != 8) {                     <- WIDTH CHECKED FIRST\n\nThe `len != 8` arm DECLINES, so a `lw` never reaches the direction kill.  **The kill is `ld`\n(len == 8).**  An accept suite written with `lw` rows would be **GREEN ON HEAD** -- vacuous by\nconstruction, floor clause (a), the detector-that-cannot-fail shape.  grok put it exactly: *\"Counting\n'one read per register' as six `lw`s gives a detector that is green on HEAD.\"*\n\n### 2.  THE COUNT IS WRONG FOR THE FOURTH TIME, AND THIS TIME THE DERIVER IS INNOCENT\n\n    1537|   if (len == 4) {\n    1542|   } else if (len != 4) {        <- the EXACT NEGATION of the if\n    1550|   } else {\n    1552|           exit(1);              <- PROVABLY UNREACHABLE\n\n`grep -cE '^[[:space:]]+exit\\(1\\);'` returns **12**, and it is right: there are twelve\n*statements*.  **One of them cannot run**, so there are **11 guest-reachable kills**.\n\n> **THE DERIVER RULE'S OWN LESSON, LANDING ON THE ROW THAT ALREADY EMBODIES IT.**  This title was\n> corrected twice for counting comments (21 -> 19 -> 12) and the deriver was added precisely so the\n> number would be re-derivable.  **It still says LIVE, and no text pattern can test reachability** --\n> so the deriver measures a CORRELATE of the class, which is the failure mode the rule names.  A\n> census keyed on what defines the class has to ask whether the arm can execute, and that is a\n> reading, not a grep.\n\n**AND TWO ROWS OF THIS QUEUE DISAGREE ABOUT ONE ARM.**  `sgimtewidth`'s own title calls `:1552`\n*\"its dead arm\"* -- so the knowledge was already in the ledger, under a different id, while this row\ncounted the same statement as live.  *Not a new fact; a fact filed where this row does not look.*\n\n### 3.  THE CITED IN-FILE PRECEDENT IS ITSELF A FILED DEFECT\n\nS2 claimed *\"the model already stores the right thing\"* for all six arms.  For TEX that warrant **is\n`sgitexindex`**: `re_tex` is written with `((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3) & 0xff`, consecutive `sd`s\noverlap by three, 81 of 112 cells are unreachable, and there are ZERO consumers.  *Backing state\nexists; the correct answer does not.*  So TEX cannot be member 4 (ACCEPT) on today's stores.\n\n### 4.  THE BRIEF CITED THE WRONG PROBE AND THE WRONG ROUND NUMBER\n\n* S4 cited `sgi_re_rgb_probe.py`.  That is **uncommitted `#505` work that never issues an RE\n  register read at all.**  The committed vehicle is `sgi_re_width_probe.py`, whose six `ld kill`\n  rows **are** the witness -- and they must be **RE-PINNED FIRST**, because today they want\n  `exit1+txt` and a correct fix turns them red.  That is the oracle-correction rule's ORDER\n  obligation, and citing the width probe as an accept suite treats a filed obligation as discharged.\n* `#508` is `alphapfnoffset`, a different defect.  The packing hazard it was cited for is real; the\n  citation is not.\n\n### 5.  WHAT THE ROUND SHOULD BE (grok's design, recorded as the seat's position)\n\n**Five ACCEPT (TLB_A/B/C, LINEAR_A/B) + TEX DECLINE, latched, falling through to the tail.**  MTE\nstays OUT: different function, different trigger, different member, different detector.\n\n*A five-arm ACCEPT that leaves TEX fatal **cannot honestly take the headline** \"the RE read arms no\nlonger kill\" -- one of six still does.*\n\nThe packing is the whole substance of the fix, and a naive version is a null repair:\n\n    TLB_A/B/C   uint16_t[256], i = ((relative_addr & 0x1ff) >> 1) & 0xff, FOUR BE halfwords\n                odata = (tlb[i]<<48) | (tlb[i+1]<<32) | (tlb[i+2]<<16) | tlb[i+3];\n                naive `odata = d->re_tlb_a[i]' keeps 16 of 64 bits\n    LINEAR_A/B  uint32_t[32],  i = ((relative_addr & 0x7f) >> 2) & 0x1f, TWO words\n                odata = ((uint64_t)lin[i] << 32) | lin[i+1];\n                naive form drops [i+1]\n\n**Do NOT mirror `>> 3` for TEX**: a mirrored read is identity only for write-one-slot-read-it-back;\na store at `+8` writes `[1..4]` and one at `+16` writes `[2..5]`, so a mirrored read returns a mix --\nand it plants a green round-trip that `sgitexindex` would later have to invert.  **Do not widen the\nTEX window toward `+0x100` either: `CRIME_RE_TEX + 0xe0` is `CRIME_RE_CLIP_IDS`, already `#499`.**\n\n### 6.  THE CLAUSE-(d) MUTANTS IT ENUMERATED, all of which a SURVIVAL row leaves green\n\n    delete exit(1), never assign odata          -> host lives, guest reads 0\n    odata = d->re_tlb_a[i]                      -> host lives, 16 of 64 bits\n    LINEAR without [i+1]                        -> host lives, high word wrong\n    ACCEPT only at the window BASE              -> green while the probe ld's at base\n    shrink one window into the #499 catch-all   -> host lives, 0, catch-all text\n\n> **The oracle for the five ACCEPT arms is the VALUE, not `alive`** -- ACCEPT is a success-effect\n> site.  Use a pattern whose every halfword lane is load-bearing (`0x1122334455667788`, already\n> `ACC_VAL`); a value with zero lanes hides a 16-bit pack.  **TEX is the refusal-effect case** and\n> takes the `#499` three-channel shape (survival + contiguous announce + sentinel -> 0), not a\n> round-trip.  The in-tree proof that survival at a refusal site is vacuous is `#497`'s\n> `d_returnzero` 35/35 escape.\n\n**Site omission is the expected mutant and is why the detector needs SIX rows, not five:** leaving\nLINEAR_B's `else` fatal keeps every other row green.\n\n### 7.  RESIDUALS IT RAISED (filed, not actioned here)\n\n* **`:1404` must NOT be merged with `sgimtedepth4dead`.**  `depth = 8 << field` is 8/16/32/64 and\n  never 4; the dead `if (depth == 4)` is on the TILED path.  *\"Fixing depth == 4 alone would mask\n  the depth-32 leak and leave depth 16.\"*\n* Two further shapes sit beside the MTE five and are in NEITHER class: `dst_y_step` fatals with its\n  `exit` commented out, and `dst_tlb`'s `default:` fatals and CONTINUES.\n* Opening LINEAR reads makes `sgilinearoob` observable from a guest (`re_linear_a[32]` aliases\n  `re_linear_b[0]`) -- a residual witness, **not** a `put d` reproduction.\n* **The witness obligation for the read class is ALREADY DISCHARGED:** the six `ld kill` rows are\n  rung-3 at all six windows on unmodified `-E sgi -e ip32`.  What remains is oracle-correction\n  ORDER, not building a probe.\n\n*The brief also carried a stale HEAD (`7a41b3f` against the checkpoint's `a264a34`), which the seat\ncaught and worked around by verifying the arms in the tree rather than trusting the packet.*\n\n*** THE codex ROUND DESIGN, 2026-09-08.  It SPLITS the round, corrects a false correction of mine,\nand names a gate coupling that would have turned the battery red. ***\n\n### 1.  THE ROUND SPLITS: FIVE ACCEPT NOW, TEX DECLINE SEPARATELY\n\ngrok's design joined them.  codex refuses the join **using this project's own scope rule**: the five\nACCEPT sites share one contract (*\"reconstruct the complete stored value\"*) while TEX DECLINE is a\ndifferent one (*\"completed read returning zero, a specific diagnostic, and independent once-only\nsuppression\"*).  *\"Putting both contracts in one Python file does not establish 'same detector, no\nnew oracle'.  Your scope rule also requires ONE FIX DESIGN.\"*\n\nAnd it refuses the other direction too: **do not wait for the TEX index repair**, because that\n*\"unnecessarily couples read termination to backing-state corruption\"* -- and *\"declining TEX does\nnot close `sgitexindex`\"* either.\n\n### 2.  THE PACKING, with the index expressions stated against the ADJUSTED `relative_addr`\n\n    TLB_A/B/C   uint16_t H[256]   i = ((r & 0x1ff) >> 1) & 0xff   four halfwords, highest first\n    LINEAR_A/B  uint32_t W[32]    i = ((r & 0x7f)  >> 2) & 0x1f   two words, highest first\n    TEX         (separate round)  no array read; leave odata == 0, announce via its own latch\n\n### 3.  *** A GATE COUPLING THAT WOULD HAVE TURNED THE BATTERY RED.  VERIFIED HERE. ***\n\n`regress/gate_arm.sh:1181` names **all six `ld kill` rows by label** in its one-line row list, and\n`:1185-1190` **pins a 35-row denominator in BOTH halves** (35 emitted AND all 35 green, deliberately\ntwo checks so a LOST row is distinguishable from a FAILING one).\n\n> **So re-pinning those rows in the probe WITHOUT editing the gate leaves the gate RED.**  codex:\n> *\"A successful probe edit can otherwise leave the gate red.\"*  **The round owes a gate edit\n> alongside the detector edit**, and the gate's own comments at `:1178-1180` warn about precisely\n> this family -- an unsatisfiable check whose row quietly stops being tested, and a row list that\n> once gained a bogus word and shipped a red gate.\n\n### 4.  *** C4 WAS A FALSE CORRECTION AND I RECORDED IT WITHOUT CHECKING. ***\n\nI wrote that `sgi_re_rgb_probe.py` is *\"uncommitted `#505` work\"*, on grok's say-so.  **It is\ncommitted** -- `git ls-files` lists it, last changed in `7a41b3f`.  One command would have settled\nit and I ran none.\n\n**grok's OTHER half survives and is the load-bearing one:** the RGB probe writes RE state and\nobserves framebuffer memory *without reading RE registers*, so `sgi_re_width_probe.py`'s six\n`ld kill` rows remain the relevant witness.  *A correction can be half right, and the half I\nrepeated was the checkable half.*\n\n### 5.  C2, C3 AND C5 CONFIRMED, each with a better derivation than mine\n\n* **C2** -- 12 active statements, inspected by enclosing control flow: **six RE read exits, five in\n  `do_mte_transfer`, one unreachable** in `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_mte)`, whose chain really is\n  `len == 4` / `len != 4` / `else`.  **11 reachable exit sites file-wide, six in the RE handler** --\n  *\"source-derived reachability, not eleven fresh demonstrations\"*, which is the honest label.\n* **C3** -- all 28 aligned TEX positions enumerated: starting indices 0-27, touched cells 0-30, so\n  **31 touched and 81 untouched**, and no functional consumer of `re_tex`.\n* **C5** -- the ordering is right, but **its scope depends on the round**: for five ACCEPT sites,\n  re-pin those FIVE and leave TEX's existing expectation until its own round.  The baseline RED\n  measurement under the revised expectations is still owed.\n\n### 6.  A SEVENTH NAMED RE REGION -- and the distinction it forces\n\n`CRIME_RE_CLIP_IDS` (`crmfbreg.h:234`, `0x16e0`) is a named region with **no dedicated handler arm**;\nit falls into the existing catch-all.  Verified: zero occurrences in `dev_sgi_re.c`.  **So \"six\ndedicated read-kill ARMS\" is correct and \"only six named RE REGIONS\" would be false.**\n\n*** THE STANDING RULINGS THAT GOVERN THIS ROW, COPIED IN 2026-09-08 -- because the round before this\none was designed against a ruling that lived only in the CHANGELOG, and the fix for that\n(`rulingnotinrow`) is to put the disposition WHERE THE DECIDER LOOKS. ***\n\n**1.  THIS ROUND IS SANCTIONED.**  The 2026-09-02 flagship adjudication ranks it:\n\n> *\"2. `sgirekills` (high, open) -- named for honesty, not scheduled here: the six RE read arms are\n> the same live-kill severity and **the natural next round**.\"*\n\n**There is no do-not-code here** -- the opposite.  *(I checked because the previous round was\nstopped by one, not because I expected one.)*\n\n**2.  WHAT THE \"SIX-WINDOW SINGLE ROUND REFUSED\" RULING ACTUALLY FORBIDS, and it is NOT this row.**\n\n> *\"Q2.1 six-window single round -- REFUSED.  It bundles at least three semantic classes (read-exit\n> de-fatalisation, index scale, OOB bounds), which the round-scope rule forbids.  The file's own\n> comment already partitions those windows across named rows: `sgirekills`, `sgireunmapped`,\n> `sgilinearoob`, `sgidewidth`, `sgirelatchenum`.\"*\n\n**The refusal is of a round bundling ACROSS THOSE ROWS.**  `sgirekills` alone is ONE class --\nread-exit de-fatalisation -- and is permitted.  *That also independently vindicates the codex design\nanswer, which split TEX out for a different reason (its ACCEPT depends on `sgitexindex`, which is\nunder a DO-NOT-CODE).*\n\n**3.  THE ORDERING OBLIGATION IS ALREADY RECORDED AND IS NOT OPTIONAL.**  From the `#497` block:\n\n> *\"**Fixing `sgirekills` will turn a green gate RED by succeeding**, six rows' worth, so it inherits\n> the oracle-correction ordering -- re-pin first, measure the committed build red, then edit.\"*\n\n**And codex independently found the mechanism:** `regress/gate_arm.sh:1181` names all six `ld kill`\nrows by label and `:1185-1190` pins a 35-row denominator in BOTH halves.  **So the round owes a GATE\nEDIT alongside the detector edit**, or a successful fix leaves the battery red.\n\n> **THE ROUND, as it now stands: five ACCEPT (TLB_A/B/C, LINEAR_A/B) with the packed inverse of each\n> store loop; TEX DECLINED and latched rather than ACCEPTed, because its index repair is barred; the\n> six `ld kill` rows RE-PINNED FIRST and measured RED on the committed build; and `gate_arm.sh`'s row\n> list and denominator updated in the same commit.**\n\n*** THE RE-PIN TEMPLATE IS ALREADY IN THE FILE, MEASURED, WITH ITS VACUOUS ALTERNATIVES NAMED.\n`#499` did this exact re-pin one arm over (`other decl`, `sgi_re_width_probe.py`), and its comment\nis the round's instructions. ***\n\n**IT PINS THREE THINGS, and the comment says why each is load-bearing:**\n\n    how   the host SURVIVES the access\n    txt   ONE CONTIGUOUS literal including the address and data fields.  *\"Two independent\n          substring tests are a weaker claim\"* -- `-- declined.` is emitted by the six `#497`\n          width declines AND by `#498`'s sgi_de decline, so a bare substring cross-matches.\n    v0    the access REACHES THE COMMON TAIL and lands a DEFINED value.  **`v0` is preset to\n          SENTINEL by `load()`**, so \"wrote odata\" and \"never landed\" are distinguishable.\n\n*** AND IT NAMES THE ORACLES IT MEASURED VACUOUS, WHICH IS WHAT SAVES THIS ROUND A TABLE: ***\n\n* **`how` ALONE IS VACUOUS.**  Variant `mexit0` -- reinstate the death as `exit(0)` -- prints the\n  announce and then dies, and *\"exit status CANNOT distinguish 'declined, then quit' from 'died with\n  status 0'\"*.  The `exit0+txt` oracle scored GREEN with `alive=False`.  Filed as `exit0oracle`.\n* **A WRITE-ONLY oracle misses two more:** `mret0` (`return 0` -> `memory_rw.c` coerces to -1 ->\n  EXCEPTION_DBE) and `mrdexit0` (`exit(0)` in the READ branch only).  **Only the `v0` channel sees\n  them.**\n\n**THE ORDER PROOF THAT ROUND USED IS THE ONE THIS ROUND OWES:** its new oracle was measured RED on\nthe COMMITTED pre-fix build BEFORE the code changed, and it was PRE-REGISTERED six hours before the\nbaseline -- *\"not fitted to the fix either\"*.\n\n> **SO THE PLAN, and every element of it is now sourced rather than invented:**\n> 1. **RE-PIN the six `ld kill` rows FIRST** -- they are generated by ONE loop at\n>    `sgi_re_width_probe.py:269-274` all wanting `exit1+txt`.  Five become value round-trips\n>    (`how`/`v0` against the packed inverse of each store loop); TEX becomes a DECLINE\n>    (`how`/contiguous-literal/`v0 == 0`), because its ACCEPT is barred by `sgitexindex`'s\n>    DO-NOT-CODE.\n> 2. **MEASURE THE COMMITTED BUILD RED** under the new expectations -- same script, opposite\n>    colour -- and preserve that artifact.\n> 3. **ONLY THEN** edit `dev_sgi_re.c`: five ACCEPT arms packing four BE halfwords (TLB) or two\n>    words (LINEAR), TEX declined and latched.\n> 4. **EDIT `gate_arm.sh` IN THE SAME COMMIT** -- `:1181` names all six `ld kill` rows by label and\n>    `:1185-1190` pins a 35-row denominator in BOTH halves, so a successful fix leaves the gate RED\n>    otherwise.\n> 5. Panel pass 2 on the diff; the flagship stages stay HELD for the 09-09 adjudication.\n\n*** THE STEP-1 RE-PIN PANEL REFUSED MY ROW SET AND SUPPLIED A BETTER ONE.  grok, 2026-09-08:\n\"do not write the proposed six-row re-pin.\" ***\n\n**TWO FLOOR-SHAPED HOLES IN WHAT I PROPOSED**, plus one oracle this file had already measured wrong:\n\n1. **The read-side FAR BOUND is unpinned.**  A row at the window BASE tests the near bound twice and\n   the far bound never -- the accept-row rule, which I QUOTED IN THE BRIEF and then did not apply to\n   my own row set.\n2. *** THE TEX TRIPWIRE WOULD HAVE BEEN VACUOUS, and this is the sharpest thing in the answer: a\n   LOAD-ONLY decline row wants v0 == 0, and a future ACCEPT round returning the ZEROED ARRAY also\n   yields 0.  The row could not redden when TEX later becomes ACCEPT. ***  That is exactly the\n   question I asked as Q3 and could not answer.  **The fix is to STORE a non-zero value first**, so\n   an ACCEPT would return it and the row goes red.\n3. **exit0 is the wrong oracle** and #499 measured why: an exit(0) reinstatement prints and dies,\n   scoring green with the process dead.  **Survival is implied by the VALUE.**\n\n*** AND MY Q2 WAS NOT AN OPEN QUESTION. ***  I asked whether an anchored form makes one row per arm\nenough instead of two.  **#499 already measured on this same device that it does not.**  Asking it\nas open was a failure to read the record I was otherwise citing.\n\n### THE ROW SET TO WRITE -- 14 rows replacing 6\n\n**FIVE ACCEPT ARMS, TWO ROWS EACH (window base and last slot):**\n\n    tlb_a ld lo    0xb5001000    store VAL_LO then ld    v0 == VAL_LO, TODO-text ABSENT,\n                                                         store debug re_tlb_a[0] = ... present\n    tlb_a ld hi    0xb50011f8    store VAL_HI then ld    same shape, debug re_tlb_a[252] = ...\n    tlb_b, tlb_c   +0x200/+0x400                         the same pair; indices still 0 and 252\n    linear_a lo/hi 0xb5001700 / 0xb5001778               debug re_linear_a[0]/[1], [30]/[31]\n    linear_b lo/hi 0xb5001780 / 0xb50017f8               debug re_linear_b[0]/[1], [30]/[31]\n\n**VAL_LO may stay 0x1122334455667788; VAL_HI MUST DIFFER IN EVERY HALFWORD LANE** (e.g.\n0xa1b2c3d4e5f60718), so a lane-swapping edit cannot round-trip.  **Want the SAME 64-bit value that\nwas stored**, not a separately invented packed encoding -- *inventing one would re-implement the\npacking inside the test and grade the code against my arithmetic instead of against the store.*\n\n*** THE ANCHOR IS THE STORE'S OWN debug(), WHICH NAMES THE INDEX. ***  That is what makes these rows\nABSOLUTE rather than relative: re_tlb_a[252] = ... pins WHICH CELL, so a translation that slides the\nwindow reddens.  **And no new debug() goes on the read path** -- the store's existing one is the\nexternal channel, and adding a print would be an unlicensed addition.\n\n**TEX -- FOUR ROWS, decline, and NOT the #499 template:**\n\n    tex ld lo     store VAL_LO at 0xb5001600 then ld    v0 == 0x0, ONE CONTIGUOUS TEX-read-decline\n                                                        literal, TODO-text absent\n    tex ld hi     the same at the last slot 0xb50016d8  same, and the TEX literal -- NOT the\n                                                        catch-all unimplemented-read literal\n    tex rd lat1   two ld's at two different offsets     TEX-read literal count == 1\n    tex xbit      fire the other five width latches     TEX-read literal still appears exactly once\n                  first, then a TEX ld\n\n*The xbit row is the latch-collision shape #490 measured in another file, applied here before it can\nbite.*\n\n*** PASS 2: SIX SEATS, ALL SIX ANSWERED, THREE SAID DO NOT SHIP -- AND TWO OF THE THREE WERE\nRIGHT. ***\n\n    codex     DO NOT SHIP   an L2 FLOOR BREACH, with a predicted score and its mechanism\n    grok      DO NOT SHIP   records; and it re-measured every claim against the tree\n    glm       DO NOT SHIP   FALSE ALARM -- but it named the settling fact instead of guessing\n    kimi      SHIP, cond.   the enum record -- a real condition, now met\n    deepseek  SHIP\n    minimax   SHIP\n\n## THE L2 -- a clause-(d) floor breach, repaired in-round\n\n`tex xbit` fires the six WIDTH latches only, so it sweeps bits 0-5.  codex named the escape:\n\n    SGIRE_W_TEX_RD = 1 << 11  ->  1 << 8  (= SGIRE_W_UNMAP_RD)      PREDICTED 44/44\n\nand gave the reason from the detector's own session schedule -- **the four TEX-read scenarios and\nthe unmapped-read scenario never share an emulator instance**, so bit 8 is clear whenever TEX is\nread.  *\"M10 establishes detection of a width-bit collision, not every existing-bit collision\"* --\nwhich is exactly the over-generalisation my CHANNEL table had made in a parenthesis.\n\n**Repaired with two SAME-INSTANCE rows, `tex/unmap` and `unmap/tex`, in BOTH orderings** (one\nordering would leave the mirror open -- the `#499` shape).  Gate raised 44 -> 46 in the same edit.\n\n## THREE WRONG RECORDS, ALL MINE, ALL CORRECTED IN PLACE\n\n* **the enum comment** -- *\"The next free bit is 1 << 11; the enum ends HERE on purpose\"* went false\n  twice over when this diff took `1 << 11`.  *** THE FILE DOCUMENTS THIS EXACT FAILURE TEN LINES\n  ABOVE WHERE I EDITED *** -- the identical claim on `SGIRE_W_PRIM_OP` went false when\n  `SGIRE_W_BADMODE` was added below it, and that comment records both the failure and the repair.\n  **I repeated a documented failure while editing the enum that documents it.**  Found by kimi,\n  confirmed independently by codex and grok.\n* **my LINEAR comment** claimed the `len == 8` guard \"keeps tlbi even\".  **Width does not imply\n  alignment** -- the guard tests LENGTH; CPU-side alignment is what keeps it even, and the OLDER\n  surrounding comment says so correctly.  My new wording was worse than the one it sat beside.\n* **the probe's contract header** still described six live read kills and row names this round\n  deleted -- clause (e), the thing a future reader trusts about what the detector claims.\n\n## *** A CLAIM OF MINE WAS CONFOUNDED, AND I HAD REPEATED IT ALL EVENING ***\n\nI said `M8_texaccept` measures the store-first TEX design.  codex: **M8 also removes the announce**,\nso its four failures do not isolate the store-first contribution -- both channels red under it.\nIsolating it needs a mutant that converts TEX to ACCEPT while KEEPING the announce.  *The\nstore-first design is still right for the reason grok gave at design time; what is not established\nis that M8 measured it.*\n\n## THE `odata` GAP -- three seats, independently\n\ncodex, glm and grok each found the same missing cell in my (d) enumeration.  **Coverage is\nunaffected** -- `M2_datafill` IS that mutant and it kills -- but the ENUMERATION could not have\ngenerated it.  Corrected in `PRETABLE.out` as an L4.\n\n## WHAT glm'S FALSE ALARM ACTUALLY FOUND -- a defect in MY BRIEF\n\nIt reasoned from HUNK BOUNDARIES that `check \"sgi_re controls live\"` sits in no hunk while the\ncontrol count went 2 -> 3, and enumerated two ways that could break the gate.  Reading the line\nsettles it: the check greps `OPUS497T_CONTROL=OK`, does not count controls, and the probe emits\nthat token over all three.  grok reached the same resolution independently and refused to ratify\nthe inference.\n\n> **A DIFF-ONLY PACKET OMITS UNCHANGED CONTEXT BY CONSTRUCTION, so a seat asking \"is the\n> surrounding code still consistent with this change?\" -- which is what pass 2 is FOR -- cannot\n> see it.**  That is the sketch rule one level up: the sketch rule says inline the enclosing\n> FUNCTION for a proposed edit; this says inline the enclosing BLOCK for a reviewed one.\n\n*Not proposed as a general rule on one instance: the seat named its settling fact, the cost was one\n`sed`, and a brief that inlines every adjacent block is a brief nobody reads.*\n\n*** THE L2 REPAIR IS MEASURED, NOT ASSERTED.  L2VERIFY.txt, scratch clone, frozen instrument. ***\n\n    BASE_pass2        46/46   the repaired detector, CLEAN\n    M13_latchcollide  44/46   KILLED   SGIRE_W_TEX_RD 1<<11 -> 1<<8 (= SGIRE_W_UNMAP_RD)\n\n**46 - 44 = exactly TWO red rows -- precisely `tex/unmap` and `unmap/tex`, the two the repair\nadded, with nothing else perturbed.**  That is the cleanest possible signature for a targeted\nrepair: the new rows catch the named escape and no existing row's score moved.\n\n*** ONE THING THIS DOES NOT SHOW, and it is stated rather than glossed: the panel PREDICTED 44/44\nagainst the PRE-REPAIR 44-row detector, and that prediction was NOT re-measured. ***  The 44-row\nform no longer exists once the two rows are added, so the escape itself is a PREDICTION with a\nmechanism (the four TEX-read scenarios and the unmapped-read scenario never share an instance;\n`tex xbit` sets bits 0-5 only), not a measured score.  **What is measured is that the repaired\ndetector kills it.**  Re-measuring the escape would mean rebuilding the 44-row detector to prove a\nhole I have already closed -- and the mechanism is checkable by reading the probe's session list,\nwhich is how the panel derived it.\n\nClone left `cmp`-CLEAN against SEC with no sentinel.\n\n*** THE FOURTH RECORDED INSTANCE OF THE UNREAD-REVIEW CLASS, AND THE MOST EXPENSIVE: IT COST AN\nENTIRE EXTRA PASS.  Mine, on pass 2, found by grok on pass 3. ***\n\nPass 3 opened with four seats saying DO NOT CLOSE THE CHAIN, and grok's answer contained the line:\n\n> *\"The grok pass-2 seat already named bit 10 (`SGIRE_W_BADMODE`); that cell is still open.\"*\n\n**It had.**  grok's PASS-2 answer says, in full:\n\n> *\"`tex xbit` does `sw` at the six `SITES` bases, then one TEX `ld`.  That fires\n> `SGIRE_W_TLBA_LEN` ... `SGIRE_W_LINB_LEN` and nothing else.  **`SGIRE_W_BADMODE` (1<<10),\n> `SGIRE_W_UNMAP_RD` (1<<8), `SGIRE_W_DE_LEN` (1<<6), `SGIRE_W_PRIM_OP` (1<<9) are never set in\n> that session.**  `other decl` is a different process.  M10 only tested the WIDTH alias the row\n> actually populates.\"*\n\n**grok named FOUR bits on pass 2.  codex named ONE (bit 8).  I acted on codex's single cell,\nshipped a repair for it, and pass 3 then found the other three -- which one pass could have\nclosed.**\n\n*** AND I HAD THE FILE OPEN. ***  I read grok's pass-2 answer far enough to quote its resolution of\nglm's `controls live` objection -- which sits about twenty lines ABOVE the passage quoted here --\nand stopped at the premises table.  **This is not \"a seat answered and nobody looked\"; it is \"I\nlooked, took the part that confirmed my reading, and stopped before the part that would have cost\nme work.\"**  That is the harder version of the class and the one no checker can catch.\n\n**THE MEASURED COST:** one full extra pass -- a six-seat panel, a rebuild, a mutant table, and the\nclass-closure amendment invoked -- to reach a repair grok had specified a pass earlier.  It also\nmeans my pass-2 record said *\"repaired in-round\"* about a repair that closed one cell of five.\n\n> **THE HABIT THAT WOULD HAVE CAUGHT IT is the one this project already has and I did not apply:\n> READ THE BODY, NOT THE VERDICT LINE AND NOT THE FIRST SCREEN.**  The carrier states it for\n> verdict lines -- *\"a verdict line is written against the brief's framing and is the LEAST likely\n> part of an answer to hold the correction\"* -- and the same is true of an answer's opening\n> section, which addresses the brief's questions in the brief's order.  **grok's finding was in\n> its Q1 body, past the premises table.**\n\n*Recorded here rather than in a records row because the cost landed on THIS round, and because the\ninstance is specific: two seats answered the same question and I integrated the narrower answer\nwithout diffing it against the wider one.  When two seats answer one question, DIFF THEM.*\n\n*** A READING SEAT FOUND A LIVE BUG THAT A GREEN 46/46 RUN HAD JUST ENDORSED.  This is the most\nimportant thing in the round. ***\n\n`store()` is `store(A, addr, op=OP_SW_T0_A3, val=\"0x12345678\")`.  I wrote the six drawing-engine\nsetup calls as\n\n    cmds += store(A, DE_MODE_DST, MODE_LIN_B)\n\n**so MODE_LIN_B was assembled as an INSTRUCTION and the register received the default value.  All\nsix were wrong the same way.**  The row executed garbage opcodes and performed none of the register\nwrites its own comment described.\n\n*** AND IT SCORED 46/46. ***  `BASE_pass3b` came back CLEAN with the broken setup, because the row's\nonly oracle was the TEX decline count -- which garbage stores do not disturb.  **A row that passes\nwhile doing nothing it claims is exactly the vacuity the floor exists to prevent**, and no\nmeasurement could see it: the in-flight mutant run then scored `M_bit9` 46/46 ESCAPED, *which is\nwhat a no-op setup predicts*, and I would have blamed my choice of `PRIM_BAD` rather than my call.\n\n> **THE STRUCTURAL DEFECT, which is worth more than the typo: A CROSS-BIT ROW WHOSE ORACLE CANNOT\n> SEE ITS OWN PRECONDITIONS IS ONE TYPO AWAY FROM VACUOUS, PERMANENTLY.**  Counting only the TEX\n> decline cannot distinguish *\"ten latches fired and TEX still announced\"* from *\"the setup did\n> nothing and TEX announced\"*.  Both give 1.\n\n**REPAIRED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS:** the six calls now pass their value as `val`, and the row now\nASSERTS ITS OWN SETUP -- it counts how many of the eleven other announces actually appeared and\nwants all of them, so a silently-failing setup (a mistyped call, a moved register, a changed\nmessage) REDS the row instead of passing it.\n\n*** THE POINT ABOUT SEAT ROLES: THIS WAS FOUND BY READING, NOT BY MEASURING, AND NO MUTANT COULD\nHAVE FOUND IT. ***  Every mutant is scored against the same broken row, so the bug is invisible to\nthe entire kill table.  It took a seat reading the helper's signature against the call site.  *The\ncarrier's \"a reading seat buys the CLASS, a measuring seat buys the INSTANCE\" understates it here:\nsome defects are visible ONLY to reading.*\n\n**codex also returned EIGHT further record defects on the same brief**, several in text this round\nwrote: the contract header still says an 8-byte `ld` \"STILL ENDS THE HOST\" one paragraph below my\ncorrection saying it does not; two probe sites still say \"every read arm here still exit(1)s\" and\n\"An 8-byte read still dies\"; my own \"No row below reads exit status\" is false of the `other decl`\nrow, which uses `how`; and \"these four TEX rows\" have the store-first shape when only two do.\n**Those are being fixed; they are listed here so the count is honest rather than rounded down.**\n\n*The brief was framed to avoid both known codex triggers -- \"no mutant, no evasion, no guest\nsequence\" -- and it answered with zero refusals.  That framing is now a proven third form alongside\ndiff review and source analysis.*\n\n*** THE LATCH-COLLISION CLASS IS CLOSED, MEASURED, WITH THE BEFORE/AFTER PRESERVED. ***\n\n    CLASSVERIFY.txt    M_bit6 45/46 KILLED   M_bit7 45/46 KILLED\n                       M_bit9 46/46 ESCAPED  M_bit10 46/46 ESCAPED   <- disclosed at the time\n    CLASSVERIFY2       M_bit9 46/46 ESCAPED  -- against the BROKEN row, so this is evidence of\n                       the store bug and NOT of an open cell\n    CLASSVERIFY3       M_bit9 45/46 KILLED   M_bit10 45/46 KILLED    <- fixed, self-checking row\n\n**Every one of the eleven other used bits of `d->warned` is now covered**: bits 0-5 by `M10`, bit 8\nby `tex/unmap` + `unmap/tex`, bits 6, 7, 9 and 10 by the widened `tex xbit`.\n\n*** AND `BASE_fixed` 46/46 MEANS MORE THAN THE EARLIER 46/46s. ***  The row wants `1/11` -- the TEX\ndecline once AND all eleven other announces present -- so a clean run now PROVES the setup fired.\nThe earlier clean runs did not: they scored a row whose six drawing-engine stores passed their VALUE\nas the OPCODE.\n\n**THE DISCLOSURE WAS WITHDRAWN AND REPLACED BY COVERAGE**, which also dissolves the rule conflict I\nhad escalated for the adjudication: the class-closure amendment permits disclosing uncovered cells,\nkimi and grok argued an in-closure cell on the edited line is a breach regardless, and rather than\nhave that adjudicated I closed the cells.  *The \"settling fact\" I had written down as unknown was\none grep away, in a shipped probe that already drives the drawing engine.*\n\n*** A WRONG RECORD IN A MEASUREMENT LOG, CORRECTED IN PLACE. ***  `CLASSVERIFY3.txt` carried its\ndriver's template footer from the previous run, saying bits 9 and 10 *\"ESCAPE BY DESIGN and are\nDISCLOSED\"* -- **directly above two KILLED results**.  A generated artifact whose boilerplate\ncontradicts its own numbers is the wrong-record class arriving in the one place a future round\ntrusts most.  *The driver writes that text unconditionally; the fix here is the artifact, and\nteaching the driver to derive its footer from the results is filed rather than done -- it is a\ntooling change owing its own controls.*\n\n*** PASS 4 -- THE CLOSING PASS.  FIVE SEATS, 3-2 TO CLOSE, AND I AM NOT CLOSING ON THE COUNT. ***\n\n    grok      YES, the chain closes -- 11/11, verified against the LIVE SOURCE\n    glm       CLOSE, conditionally -- 11/11\n    minimax   CHAIN CLOSES -- 11/11\n    deepseek  NO\n    kimi      DO NOT CLOSE AS MEASURED\n\n**Count the arguments, not the seats.**  Two of the objections do not survive and one does.\n\n## *** THE ONE THAT SURVIVES, AND IT TURNS THIS ROUND'S OWN DOCTRINE ON ME ***\n\nkimi: *\"against the FINAL, self-checking row, exactly **2 of 11** aliasing mutants have a logged\nkill -- the other nine were measured against earlier versions of the very row that kills them, and\nthis round's own doctrine (value-as-opcode scoring 46/46 while doing nothing) forbids carrying kills\nacross a row edit by construction.\"*\n\n**That is unanswerable.**  I proved that a probe edit can silently void a measurement, wrote it up as\nthis round's central lesson, and then presented nine kills carried across exactly such an edit.  Its\nformulation is the right one: **\"close every cell in ONE diff\" deserves \"kill every cell on ONE\nprobe version.\"**  Running as `SWEEP11` -- eleven aliases, one frozen probe, one table.\n\n## THE TWO THAT DO NOT SURVIVE, and why -- stated so the dissent is not lost\n\n* **deepseek: \"the widened row tests only one ordering for bits 6,7,9,10.\"**  The row's TEX load is\n  LAST, so under an alias the setup fires the bit FIRST and the TEX announce is suppressed -> count\n  0 -> red.  The mirror (TEX suppressing a setup announce) would show as `fired == 10` -> also red.\n  **A single-bit alias is symmetric; catching it in one ordering kills it.**  The bit-8 pair needed\n  both orderings because those are two DIFFERENT sites, not one bit.\n* **deepseek's Q2 escape** posits a session ordered TEX-read-first.  **The row's order is fixed in\n  the probe and is not reachable by a mutant of the DEVICE**, which is what the table perturbs.\n* **deepseek/kimi: \"enumeration exclusivity is not checkable from the packet.\"**  Correct about the\n  packet, and now SETTLED from source, twice independently -- my own derivation and grok's:\n  twelve enumerators `1<<0`..`1<<11`, no duplicates, no thirteenth, twelve latch sites all on\n  `d->warned`, no raw `|= 1<<k`, and no second bitmask in `struct sgi_re_data`.\n\n## *** A DEFECT INSIDE ONE OF MY CORRECTIONS, FOUND BY TWO SEATS INDEPENDENTLY ***\n\ngrok and kimi both caught it.  My in-diff note said `tex rd lat1` and `tex xbit` *\"would not redden\non an ACCEPT\"*.  **False: both oracle the decline-message COUNT, which an ACCEPT drives to 0, so both\ngo red.**  What they cannot do is distinguish an ACCEPT from a DELETED MESSAGE -- only the value pair\ncan.  **The correction UNDERSTATED the detection surface**, which is the same wrong-record class this\nround has spent the evening fixing, arriving inside the fix for it.  Corrected; and grok's\ndisposition is adopted -- *\"fix the sentence when convenient; do not reopen the chain for it.\"*\n\n**kimi's Q3 residual, kept because it is right and unfixed:** the self-check tests `lit in t`, which\nis EMISSION not LATCHING, and the literals are unanchored substrings.  So the `/11` half proves the\nmessages appeared, not that any bit was set.  For this class that is harmless -- the kill signal is\nTEX's own count -- but the contract should say so rather than imply the row verifies latching.\n\n*** THE CHAIN CLOSES.  ELEVEN OF ELEVEN CELLS KILLED ON ONE PROBE VERSION, WITH THE DENOMINATOR.\nSWEEP11.txt. ***\n\n    SWEEP_BASE   46/46   the final self-checking probe, CLEAN\n    bits 0-7, 9, 10      45/46 each   red: `tex xbit`\n    bit 8                43/46        red: `tex xbit`, `tex/unmap`, `unmap/tex`\n    NOT KILLED: none\n\n**kimi's pass-4 objection is discharged BY MEASUREMENT, not by argument.**  It held that only 2 of\n11 aliases had a logged kill against the FINAL row, and that the other nine were carried across a\nrow edit -- which this round's own doctrine forbids, having proved that a probe edit can silently\nvoid a measurement.  **Every cell is now killed on one frozen probe, in one run.**\n\n*** BIT 8 IS THE MOST INFORMATIVE ROW IN THE TABLE, AND IT WAS NOT DESIGNED TO BE. ***  It reds\nTHREE rows rather than one, because `SGIRE_W_UNMAP_RD` is exactly what the two pass-2 ordering rows\ntarget.  **So a single mutant demonstrates that the pass-2 repair and the pass-3 class closure are\nboth live and INDEPENDENT on the same probe version** -- which no separate table could have shown,\nbecause each was previously measured against its own instrument.\n\n**THE CLOSING PASS IS VALID BY ITS OWN RULE:** the diff was delivered and inlined, the roster is\nrecorded with its misses named (codex deliberately not fired -- the ask-again shape), and the zero\ncarries its denominator -- **eleven cells, enumerated from the twelve used bits of `d->warned`,\nderived from source twice independently rather than asserted.**\n\n*What closed it was not the 3-2 vote.  Three seats said close and two did not; both dissents were\nadjudicated on their arguments -- one refuted from source, one right and now measured away.\nCounting seats would have closed the chain an hour early on a denominator of 2 of 11.*\n\n*** THIS ROW'S CENSUS IS STALE BY A FACTOR OF TWO, AND THE BLOCKER IT IS BUILT AROUND IS ALREADY\nDISCHARGED.  Found by a codex ANALYSIS job 2026-09-08, VERIFIED here twice. ***\n\ncodex, on the current files: *\"The current files contradict the historical counts: I find SIX active\n`exit(1)` statements, and the probe's six former death rows have already been replaced.\"*\n\n**DERIVED, not transcribed -- and my FIRST attempt was wrong, which is worth recording because it is\nthe deriver rule landing on me again.**  A line-prefix test (*does this line start with a comment\nmarker?*) reported SEVEN, because `dev_sgi_re.c:259` is a CONTINUATION line of a block comment that\nbegins with neither `/*` nor `*`.  **I classified by a CORRELATE of being commented rather than by\nbeing commented.**  Stripping block and line comments properly, preserving line numbers:\n\n    ACTIVE exit( in dev_sgi_re.c:  6      :1463  :1471  :1547  :1552  :1563  :1685\n    (six further `exit(' texts are inside comments, several of them prose ABOUT this very row)\n\n**So the title's \"TWELVE exit(1) STATEMENTS and ELEVEN GUEST-REACHABLE KILLS\" is stale by 2x.**  The\nintervening rounds -- `#497`'s width de-fatalisation and `#499`'s work in the same file -- removed\nor commented six of them, and this row's headline was never re-derived.\n\n*** THE MORE IMPORTANT HALF: THE ORDERING OBLIGATION THIS ROW IS ORGANISED AROUND NO LONGER EXISTS. ***\n\nThe row says, in capitals: *\"FIXING sgirekills WILL TURN A GREEN GATE RED BY SUCCEEDING, SIX ROWS'\nWORTH ... it therefore inherits the oracle-correction ordering -- re-pin the six rows FIRST.\"*\n\n**MEASURED against the shipped probe:**\n\n    grep -c 'ld kill'                        regress/sgi_re_width_probe.py   ->  5\n    grep -n 'row(' ... | grep -i kill        ->  ZERO row() calls.  All five hits are COMMENTS,\n                                                 and both are written in the PAST TENSE:\n        :370  \"THE OLD ROWS were `row(nm + \\\" ld kill\\\", how + txt, \\\"exit1+txt\\\")' -- they asserted\"\n        :527  \"THE OLD ROW was `row(\\\"other kill\\\", how, \\\"exit1\\\")' -- DEATH-ONLY.\"\n\n**There are no death-only rows left to re-pin.**  A later round already replaced them -- the probe's\nown header says so at `:33`: *\"CRIME_RE_TEX became a latched decline -- so `<s> ld kill' x6 NO LONGER\nEXIST\"*.  So the oracle-correction ordering this row inherits is **already satisfied**, and the\nsequencing constraint that made it look expensive is gone.\n\n> **THE ROW IS THEREFORE CHEAPER AND SMALLER THAN IT READS, AND BOTH ERRORS POINT THE SAME WAY:\n> toward over-stating the work.**  A future round reading the headline plans for eleven kills and a\n> six-row re-pin; the real job is six kills with no re-pin owed.\n\n*** THIS IS THE CARRIER'S OWN COUNT RULE, EARNED RATHER THAN QUOTED: \"the rounds that consult the\nnumber are the rounds that change it.\"  `#497` and `#499` both edited this file, and each left this\nrow's headline one step further from the source. ***\n\n**WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED, and codex said so itself:** it could give *\"a defensive census and\nguard-order analysis\"* but *\"can't map guest-controlled values or access sequences to\nhost-termination paths\"* from the three permitted files.  **So the count of ACTIVE exits is\nmeasured; the count of GUEST-REACHABLE ones is not**, and the title's \"eleven guest-reachable\" has no\ncurrent replacement figure.  The row should carry the six with their guards and leave reachability\nopen until it is measured, rather than swapping one unverified number for another."}
{"id": "sgimtewidth", "title": "sgi_mte serves EVERY access width and a one-byte store silently ZEROES the adjacent register -- and the obvious fix for its dead arm would CREATE a width kill", "state": "open", "sev": "med"}
{"id": "lcabadval", "state": "in work", "sev": "high", "title": "dev_lca.c: SIX chipset registers in DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_ioc) kill the host on a value outside a small accepted set (CONF, TB_ENA, W_BASE0/1, W_MASK0/1) -- plus a seventh exit in the default arm, which this round does NOT close", "state_note": "*** RECORDS CORRECTION 2026-09-06, from RE-DERIVING this row's own citations instead of trusting them.  THE\nHEADLINE COUNT (eleven live terminating calls) IS RIGHT.  THE ENUMERATION UNDER IT IS WRONG IN TWO WAYS, AND THE\nFIRST ONE CHANGES THE FIX DESIGN. ***\n\n**1.  `dev >= 21` IS NOT A LIVE KILL.  IT IS ALREADY DE-FATALISED -- AND IT IS THIS FILE'S OWN FIX PRECEDENT.**\n\nThe row lists it among \"two range guards that also kill\".  The source says otherwise: the guard is at `:127`, its\nbody is a COMMENTED-OUT `fatal()` + `exit(1)` at `:128-129`, and what actually executes is `dev = 0;` at `:130`.\n\n```c\n\tif (dev >= 21) {\n\t\t/*  fatal(\"[ LCA: No bus 0 device? TODO ]\\n\");\n\t\texit(1);  */\n\t\tdev = 0;\n\t}\n```\n\nSomeone has already made, in this very file, the decision this row exists to make: **delete the kill, substitute a\nsafe default, keep going.**  That is a TRANSFERRED WARRANT from inside the same file, which the six-member\noption-set rule says to go looking for -- *\"grep the fork for an existing idiom; the tree's own precedents are part\nof the design space\"* -- and which nobody had found because the row was read rather than re-derived.  So only ONE\nrange guard is live: `ofs > len` at `:163`.\n\n**2.  THE ENUMERATION COVERED 8 OF THE 11, AND THE THREE IT MISSED ARE NOT VALUE GUARDS AT ALL.**\n\nRe-derived by `grep -n 'exit(1)' dev_lca.c` minus the commented one (12 matches, 11 live), the eleven are:\n\n| line | class | condition |\n|---|---|---|\n| 73  | **INTERRUPT STUB** | `lca_interrupt_assert` -- bare `fatal(\"...TODO\"); exit(1)`, **NO GUARD** |\n| 85  | **INTERRUPT STUB** | `lca_interrupt_deassert` -- likewise |\n| 134 | BUS | `bus != 0`, i.e. `d->ioc_conf & 1` |\n| 163 | RANGE | `ofs > len` |\n| 206 | VALUE | `idata & ~1` (IOC_CONF) |\n| 226 | VALUE | `idata & ~IOC_TB_ENA_TEN` |\n| 241 | VALUE | `idata != 0 && idata != 0x300800000` |\n| 256 | VALUE | `idata != 0x700000` |\n| 280 | VALUE | `idata != 0x240000000` |\n| 295 | VALUE | `idata != 0x3ff00000` |\n| 315 | CATCH-ALL | `default:` -- any unimplemented ioc offset, read OR write |\n\n**THE TWO INTERRUPT STUBS ARE PLAUSIBLY THE MOST SEVERE OF THE ELEVEN, AND A ROW FRAMED AS A VALUE-GUARD CLASS\nCOULD NOT SEE THEM.**  They are not a wrong value being written -- they are an ENTIRE UNIMPLEMENTED PATH with no\npredicate at all, so *any* device asserting an interrupt through the LCA ends the process regardless of what it\nwrites.  The four \"accepts exactly one value\" registers need a firmware revision that writes something else; these\ntwo need only that an interrupt is asserted.\n\n*A row named after one class hides the members of the others.  That is this project's cell-not-class pattern\nwearing an enumeration instead of a detector -- the same shape the m88kexcdeliver chain hit four times.*\n\n**WHAT IS UNCHANGED:** the severity (HIGH), the single consumer (`machine_alpha.c`, `device_add(machine, \"lca\")`),\nthe absence of any alpha rig, and the observation that a guest here is FIRMWARE so the reaching value is whatever\nSRM or NetBSD writes.  The scope caveat about range-vs-value guards also stands and is now sharper, because there\nis only one live range guard to reason about.\n\n--- the original note follows, unaltered ---\n\n\n=== DESIGN PASS, 2026-09-06 (codex / GPT-6 Astra at max, read-only).  Answer preserved at\n=== regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_design_20260906.txt.  IT CORRECTED THREE OF THE FIVE PREMISES I HAD\n=== JUST RE-DERIVED, and one of the corrections changes what this round may even choose from.\n\n**SEVERITY ORDER CONFIRMED: the two INTERRUPT STUBS first** -- but \"provisionally; firmware execution order is\nunproved\", which is the honest qualifier my own framing lacked.\n\n*** A SEVENTH OPTION-SET MEMBER: IMPLEMENT. ***  The carrier's set is DELETE / DECLINE / DECLINE+LATCHED\nANNOUNCE / ACCEPT / FAULT / BACKED ACCEPT -- all six presume the access is a REFUSAL DECISION.  For the two\ninterrupt stubs none of them applies: *\"declining an interrupt leaves the machine incomplete.\"*  A stub is not a\nrefusal to service a value; it is an unimplemented PATH, and the disposition is to write the code.  This is the\noption-set completeness rule paying off exactly as it is meant to -- the member was found by a seat reading the\ntree, not by argument.\n\n*** AND THE FAULT MEMBER IS NOT AVAILABLE ON THIS MACHINE AT ALL. ***  `dev_unreadable.c` works because\n`memory_rw.c` dispatches an exception on a failed device access **for MIPS and M88K, with NO ALPHA EQUIVALENT**.\nSo the idiom this fork installs ten times in `machine_sgi.c` does not transfer here, and any round reaching for\nFAULT owes Alpha exception delivery first.  *A precedent that is real elsewhere in the tree is not thereby\ntransferable; that is the warrant-transfer rule, and it took a seat opening `memory_rw.c` to see it.*\n\n**AND A FINDING ABOUT THE ALPHA PORT THAT OUTRANKS THIS ROW:** the Alpha interrupt callbacks merely set\n`irq_asserted`, and **the execution loop never consumes that flag**.  So implementing the two stubs would not by\nitself deliver an interrupt -- removing the exits cannot complete the path.  Filed as its own question rather\nthan folded in here.\n\n## THE PREMISE AUDIT, and I was wrong three times\n\n* **PREMISE 1 HOLDS.**  Twelve textual occurrences, eleven outside comments.\n* **PREMISE 2's REMOVAL IS REAL; ITS \"USABLE PRECEDENT\" CONCLUSION IS WRONG.**  I read the commented-out\n  `dev >= 21` kill plus its live `dev = 0` as the file's own answer, transferable to this round.  It is not: it\n  **ALIASES \"no selected device\" to DEVICE ZERO**, which is harmless today only because device 7 is the sole\n  installed device and slot 0 happens to be empty.  Populate slot 0 and it is a latent alias defect.  *So the\n  question I asked -- is this a sound precedent or a latent defect of its own -- has the second answer, and my\n  brief had already leaned on the first.*\n* **PREMISE 3's CLASS COUNTS HOLD, ITS SINGLETON CLAIM DOES NOT.**  `W_BASE0` accepts **TWO** values, `0` and\n  `0x300800000` (`idata != 0ULL && idata != 0x300800000ULL`), so \"four registers accepting exactly ONE value\" is\n  wrong -- only **three** window guards accept a single value.  *This one is mine twice over: the original row\n  note said it, I re-derived the enumeration tonight and reproduced it, and the guard's own text was in my\n  terminal when I did.*\n* **PREMISE 4 HOLDS** as one machine-setup consumer (AlphaBook 1), with no dedicated boot rig -- **but\n  \"never exercised\" DOES NOT FOLLOW**: a generic constructor sweep exists (`gate_asan_sweep.sh`), without\n  proving coverage of these branches.  My \"no rig means no coverage\" was too strong.\n* **PREMISE 5 IS AN UNSUPPORTED RESTRICTION.**  I said a guest here is firmware, so the reaching value is\n  whatever SRM or NetBSD writes.  Guest KERNEL stores use the same MMIO path, and interrupt callbacks arise from\n  DEVICE events -- neither is firmware.\n\n## DISPOSITIONS IT WOULD TAKE, per class\n* **INTERRUPT STUB (2):** IMPLEMENT forwarding and CPU interrupt delivery.\n* **BUS (1):** implement Type-1 decoding then ACCEPT through `bus_pci`, using its existing absent-device\n  handling.  `ioc_conf & 1` selects the CONFIGURATION-CYCLE TYPE and is **not the decoded bus number** -- and\n  deleting this exit alone leaves `dev` uninitialised.\n* **RANGE (1):** DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE with initialised read results and no forwarded side effects.  Note\n  `ofs` is at most three, **so this guard cannot fire for four- or eight-byte accesses** -- changing `>`\n  mechanically is not a design.\n* **VALUE (6):** ACCEPT documented register fields using the existing backing, latched announce for the missing\n  DMA/TLB effects.  **Every guarded assignment already happens BEFORE its check**, so no invented backing is\n  needed -- but readback alone does not implement translation.\n* **CATCH-ALL (1):** split documented registers (`STAT0`/`STAT1`) from genuine holes; FAULT only for confirmed\n  holes and only once Alpha exception delivery exists.\n\n**WHAT IT COULD NOT ESTABLISH, said plainly:** source shows connected interrupt paths and conditional MMIO paths\nfor the other nine sites, but **no particular SRM/NetBSD execution of any terminating site**.  Firmware\nreachability and failure frequency remain unmeasured, so the severity order is provisional on exactly that.\n\n\n*** THE SEVERITY WARRANT THIS ROW WAS MISSING: THE REJECTED VALUES INCLUDE A DOCUMENTED ONE, AND THE\nSTORE LANDS BEFORE THE PROCESS ENDS.  Found by the codex seat 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_scoping_20260907.txt),\nverified against the source and the header before recording. ***\n\nEach of the three window guards has this shape:\n\n    d->window_mask_0 = idata;                          <- the store LANDS\n    if (idata != 0x700000ULL) { fatal(...); exit(1); } <- then the process ends\n\n**And zero is a documented encoding.**  From src/include/thirdparty/alpha_lcareg.h:\n\n    #define IOC_W_MASK_1M   0x0000000000000000ULL   /* 1MB window */\n\nSo a guest selecting the SMALLEST DOCUMENTED WINDOW SIZE ends the emulator.  The same argument covers\nW_BASE1, whose one accepted constant 0x240000000 carries IOC_W_BASE_WEN (0x200000000, *Window enable*) --\n**so a driver DISABLING a window, by writing a base with WEN clear, is killed too.**\n\n*** THIS IS #504's WARRANT AT A SECOND DEVICE: documented values that the model kills on. ***  It is not a\njunk-value guard; it is a whitelist of ONE that excludes documented members of its own encoding.  codex adds\nthat all three registers start at zero through constructor initialisation, so the rejected value is also the\nRESET value.\n\n**AND THE RANGE SITE IS FAR MORE REACHABLE THAN THIS ROW SAID.**  It was recorded as `ofs > len'.  Reading\nDEVICE_ACCESS(lca_isa): `relative_addr >>= 5; ofs = relative_addr & 3;' -- **ofs is guest ADDRESS BITS 5-6**,\nnot a written value.  So an ordinary 1-byte access at an address with those bits set to 2 or 3 ends the\nprocess.  That is address decoding, not a value whitelist, and it belongs in a different disposition class\nfrom the six value guards.  The rest of that function is filed separately as [[lcaisalane]].\n\n*** DISPOSITION PASS, codex 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_disposition_20260907.txt).  IT NARROWS MY REACHABILITY CLAIM,\nCONFIRMS TWO OTHERS, AND SUPPLIES THE IN-TREE PRECEDENTS THE OPTION-SET RULE ASKS FOR -- INCLUDING\nCONFLICTING ONES, WHICH IS WHAT I ASKED IT TO SURFACE. ***\n\nVerdict: *\"Disposition 5 is unavailable through Alpha's failed-device-access path.  Five candidate rounds fit\na uniform VALUE-refusal policy.  The tree supplies CONFLICTING precedents, especially for lane handling and\nstoring unsupported values.\"*\n\n*** MY `AN ORDINARY READ-MODIFY-WRITE ENDS THE EMULATOR' CLAIM IS TOO BROAD, AND I HAVE REPEATED IT IN THREE\nPLACES. ***  codex: *\"they read zero INITIALLY, then echo accepted writes.  Writing back the initial zero\nkills the last three; an arbitrary RMW does not necessarily kill them -- its resulting value matters.\"*\n\n**The precise claim is the INITIAL-ZERO readback**: before any accepted write, `W_MASK0', `W_BASE1' and\n`W_MASK1' read 0 and accept only one non-zero constant each, so a read-modify-write performed on a\nfreshly-reset device writes 0 back and ends the process.  **Once a valid value has been written the register\nechoes it**, and an RMW of that value is accepted.  So the window is early -- driver probe or\ninitialisation -- rather than any RMW at any time.  That is still a routine thing for a driver to do at\nattach, and it is still reached with a documented encoding (`IOC_W_MASK_1M` = 0), but the sentence I wrote\nwas wider than the fact.\n\n**CONFIRMED, both of which I had asserted:** every VALUE arm STORES BEFORE CHECKING; and the accepted sets\nare exactly as filed (`IOC_CONF' {0,1}, `IOC_TB_ENA' {0,0x80}, `W_BASE0' {0,0x300800000}, and the three\nsingle-value ones).\n\n**DISPOSITION 5 IS UNAVAILABLE -- now established from a third direction.**  The carrier already recorded it\ngenerally, [[alphafaultclaim]] reached it from the instruction side, and this reaches it from the\nfailed-device-access path.  **Any proposal for a bus-error refusal on an Alpha device owes Alpha exception\ndelivery first**, which is a far larger round than the one it would be riding on.\n\n**THE PRECEDENT I ASKED FOR, AND IT IS AN EXACT ONE:** `cpu_sh.c' defines `sh3_cpu_interrupt_assert()' and\n`sh3_cpu_interrupt_deassert()' with EMPTY `/* TODO */' bodies -- *\"an exact unimplemented-callback precedent\nfor 2, ignore silently\"* -- and `core/interrupt.c' defines empty `no_interrupt_assert/deassert' callbacks,\nthough those represent intentionally absent connections rather than unimplemented ones.  **So the two\nINTERRUPT STUBS have a direct in-tree answer** and do not need disposition 7 after all, which is what I had\nassumed they needed.\n\n**AND IT REPORTS THE PRECEDENTS CONFLICT** -- especially on lane handling and on whether to store an\nunsupported value.  That is the more useful answer: it means the choice is genuinely open at those sites\nrather than settled by the tree, so a round there must argue rather than transfer.\n\n**SCOPE: FIVE candidate rounds** under a uniform VALUE-refusal policy -- consistent with the earlier read's\n*\"this needs several rounds\"* and finer than it.\n\n**WHERE THE OVER-BROAD FORM SURVIVES, named because it cannot be edited:** commit `de96f11`'s MESSAGE\nsays *\"each reads back 0 while its guard accepts one non-zero constant, and AN ORDINARY READ-MODIFY-WRITE\nENDS THE EMULATOR on all three\"*.  A commit message is immutable, so the house rule -- correct the false\nrecord IN PLACE -- cannot be followed literally.  **The next best thing is to point at it from the record a\nreader will reach**, which is this row.  The narrowed claim above is the operative one: the kill needs the\nINITIAL zero readback, not any RMW at any time.\n\n*** THE PREREQUISITE IS DISCHARGED.  `#506` LANDED THIS AFTERNOON. ***\n\nThis row's HOLD-Q asked: *\"given that every row must match the `fatal()` TEXT rather than the exit\nstatus, and given that `lcaconfwidth` means the IOC_CONF arm's text can PRINT THE WRONG VALUE, is the\ntext-matching oracle sound at THAT arm -- or must `lcaconfwidth`'s printf fix land FIRST, making it a\nprerequisite of this row rather than a sibling?\"*\n\n**It landed.**  `#506` repaired both narrowing sites in `dev_lca.c` -- `:205` (IOC_CONF) and the\n`default:` arm -- to `0x%\" PRIx64` with `(uint64_t)idata`, matching the five siblings.  Measured by\n`regress/lca_confwidth_probe.py`: **HEAD 4/6 -> fix 6/6, same script**, with `kill width` now reading\n`0x100000000` instead of `0x0`.\n\n> **So the text oracle is now sound at the IOC_CONF arm, and the ordering question in the HOLD-Q is\n> answered by events rather than by adjudication.**  What remains of that HOLD-Q is its SECOND half,\n> untouched: *the de-escalation clause owes an accept row PER PREDICATE, and the ledger's earlier\n> census dropped `W_BASE1` and `W_MASK1` -- confirm the accept-row set is six, not four.*\n\n*The interrupt-stub confound is also unchanged and still governs the witness design:\n`lca_interrupt_assert` and `lca_interrupt_deassert` are bare `fatal()`+`exit(1)` with the ISA PIC\nchain wired to them, so ANY interrupt ends the host with the same status as the value guards under\ntest.  That is why the rows must match text, which is why `#506` was the prerequisite.*\n\n*** THE HOLD-Q'S SECOND HALF IS ANSWERED BY MEASUREMENT: THE ACCEPT-ROW SET IS SIX. ***\nEnumerated by reading `if (idata` in `dev_lca.c`, 2026-09-07:\n\n    :203  IOC_CONF   idata & ~1\n    :231  TB_ENA     idata & ~IOC_TB_ENA_TEN\n    :246  W_BASE0    idata != 0ULL && idata != 0x300800000ULL\n    :261  W_MASK0    idata != 0x700000ULL\n    :285  W_BASE1    idata != 0x240000000ULL\n    :300  W_MASK1    idata != 0x3ff00000ULL\n\n**Six, not four** -- the earlier census in this row dropped `W_BASE1` and `W_MASK1`, exactly as the\nHOLD-Q suspected.  *Note two shapes, which may matter for the fix design: two BITMASK tests\n(`& ~mask`) and four EQUALITY tests, one of which accepts two values.*\n\n**And the eleven-live count is confirmed rather than carried:** `grep -cE '^[[:space:]]+exit\\(1\\);'`\ngives **12**, of which `:129` sits inside a commented-out block -- so 11 live, in **FIVE** classes:\n2 interrupt stubs, 1 bus/cycle-type, 1 range/lane, 6 value, 1 default.\n\n> #### *** THIS SENTENCE SAID \"four classes\" AND THEN LISTED FIVE.  Corrected 2026-09-07 after the\n> #### grok design pass named it -- and the same slip was in the brief that asked it to. ***\n> The opener asks seats to check enumerations; mine was wrong in its own headline, with the\n> refutation printed three words later.  *A count and the list beside it are two claims, and\n> nothing checks them against each other.*\n\n*** AND THE FIRST HALF OF THE HOLD-Q IS DISCHARGED BY EVENTS: `#506` LANDED (`7a41b3f`). ***\nThe question was whether `lcaconfwidth`'s printf fix had to land first, since this row's rows must\nmatch the `fatal()` TEXT rather than the exit status (the two interrupt stubs kill with the same\nstatus as every value guard).  **It landed and is measured** -- both narrowing sites now print\n`0x%\" PRIx64` with `(uint64_t)idata`, HEAD 4/6 -> fix 6/6 -- so the IOC_CONF arm's text is sound.\n\n**Design brief out to grok** (`regress/artifacts/lcabadval/DESIGN_brief.md`) rather than codex: the\ncodex seat refused the sibling `lcaconfwidth` census on its content filter, and a brief enumerating\neleven host kills is the same recorded trigger shape.\n\n*** GROK DESIGN PASS 2026-09-07: **NOT RATIFY** as a six-armed one-design round until two of my\nclaims are dropped.  The ROUND SHAPE survives; two warrants and one count do not. ***\n\n**1. \"THE ACCEPT-ROW SET IS SIX\" STILL UNDER-COUNTS.**  De-escalation owes a row per PREDICATE, but\nthree of the six predicates accept TWO values:\n\n    IOC_CONF  {0, 1}          TB_ENA   {0, 0x80}      W_BASE0  {0, 0x300800000}\n    W_MASK0   {0x700000}      W_BASE1  {0x240000000}  W_MASK1  {0x3ff00000}\n\n**Nine pinning values, not six rows** -- and three of the nine are the reset `0`, which is vacuous\nunless sequenced after a nonzero write.  *So replacing the old \"four\" with \"six\" corrected one\nunder-count into another.*\n\n**2. S5 DOES NOT TRANSFER, AND CITING IT WOULD CITE A DEFECT AS A WARRANT.**  I offered the live\n`dev = 0` path (`dev >= 21`, kill commented out) as this file's own *delete-the-kill, substitute-a-\nsafe-default* precedent.  **It is PCI type-0 TAG DECODE, not a register-value policy**: it aliases\n\"no device selected\" onto SLOT 0, which is empty today only because the installed SIO is device 7.\n**And that loop is already filed as [[lcashiftub]] -- `0x800 << 20` in a signed `int` at `dev == 20`\nis UB.**  Substituting `0x700000` when firmware wrote `IOC_W_MASK_1M` (0) would be that same alias\npointed at a documented encoding.\n\n> **THE RIGHT IN-FILE WARRANT IS `W_T_BASE0` / `W_T_BASE1`**: the sibling TRANSLATED-window registers\n> already store any value and do not kill.  *Base/mask killing on a firmware snapshot while\n> translated-base accepts everything is the file's own outlier* -- which is a far stronger warrant\n> than the one I proposed, and one grep away.\n\n**3. THE ROUND SHAPE IS RATIFIED: ONE ROUND, MEMBER 4 ACCEPT, ALL SIX.**  Delete `fatal`+`exit` and\nkeep the store that already ran.  Five of the six fields are pure storage (`window_base_*`,\n`window_mask_*`, `tlb_enable` appear only as `odata = d->X` / `d->X = idata`); the sixth, `ioc_conf`,\nis consumed only as `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1` in `DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)`, **so extra CONF bits\nare already discarded at the only consumer** and a masked store would be an unlicensed addition.\n**Bitmask vs equality is a DETECTOR-SHAPE split, not a fix-design split.**  FAULT is unavailable on\nAlpha.\n\n*** 4. THE FLOOR-(a) TRAP, and it is the finding that most changes the detector: AN ACCEPT-ONLY\nDETECTOR IS GREEN ON HEAD. ***  Writing the six magic constants SURVIVES on the committed build.  So\na detector that writes only currently-legal values **is green under verbatim reinstatement of all six\noriginal guards** -- the vacuity-floor miss in its purest form.  **The rows that make (b) bite are the\ndocumented-REJECTED writes**: MASK0 `0`, BASE1 with WEN clear, CONF bit 32, a non-TEN TB_ENA bit.\n\n**5. THE (d) FORMS HERE ARE SITE OMISSION AND DISJUNCT-DROPPING, not an operand swap.**  Leave\n`W_MASK1` (or `W_BASE1`) fatal while de-fatalising the other five -- incomplete clause (b), and\n**`ioc rt` in `lca_confwidth_probe.py` stays green because nothing in that probe touches MASK1.**\nSecond form: on `W_BASE0`, drop `idata != 0ULL &&` and keep the magic comparison -- the magic accept\nrow stays green, *which is exactly why BASE0 owes TWO pins*.\n\n**6. `lca_confwidth_probe.py`'s `kill fired` and `died` WANTS WILL INVERT IF THIS ROUND SHIPS.**  It\nis a `#506` witness, not this round's detector.  **Re-pin it under the oracle-correction rule -- do\nnot launder by editing wants after the product change.**\n\n**7. THE STUBS ARE A SIBLING, NOT A PREREQUISITE.**  Construction does not fire them today (PIC\n`ier`/`irr` start 0, 8253 `in_use=0`, WDC only with an IDE image, ns16550 needs `com_ier` bits), and\n`#506`'s legal session already survived on this machine.  **Text is sufficient to ATTRIBUTE a kill\nand is NOT a survival oracle** -- an accept row wanting `alive` still false-reds if a stub fires, and\nthat is controlled by SESSION SHAPE (cold prompt, `step`, don't touch the ISA window), not by\nde-fatalising the stubs first.  *A BOOT witness would make them a prerequisite, because `timer_stop`\nis why stepping does not tick the PIT.*\n\n**8. AND AN HONESTY CONSTRAINT FOR THE CONTRACT:** `default:` still kills `STAT0`/`STAT1`, which the\nheader documents as R/W1C.  **This round can be green and firmware can still die**, so the contract\nmust name that as out of scope or the round will be reported as a false pass of *\"LCA no longer\nhost-kills on IOC MMIO\"*.\n\n**Split out, each its own round:** the interrupt stubs (member 7); `bus != 0` -- *deleting that\n`exit(1)` leaves `dev` UNINITIALISED in the `else`*, which is the sketch rule and not a rider;\n`ofs > len` (already [[lcaisalane]]; `ofs` is address bits 5-6); and the `default:` arm.  **HAE at\n`case LCA_IOC_BASE` already silently ignores writes to a documented register -- that is member 2, not\n4, and must not be unified with the six.**\n\n*** 2026-09-07, CODEX FIX-CHECK: QUALIFIED YES, AND IT FOUND A COUNTEREXAMPLE TO THE ROUND'S CENTRAL WARRANT. ***  All six stores confirmed prior to their guards (table, full denominator); the consumer analysis confirmed with one sharpening -- masking CONF would change READBACK policy, not merely bus selection.  **But `dev_sh4.c's RCR1 comment records that its store USED TO run before the exit and was DELIBERATELY MOVED BELOW THE GUARD**, so \"the store already ran\" is not a general warrant in this tree.  The round now carries an open A-vs-B design question (keep the store vs decline the write) that the W_T_BASE* siblings CANNOT settle, since accepting-anything there may be an absent guard rather than a decision.  Disposition set also grew by two source-backed members (accept/decline WITH REPEATED announcements; complete with a GUEST-VISIBLE DEVICE ERROR, dev_wdc.c's WDCE_ABRT+interrupt).  And a correction to my own brief: only TWO BITS IN STAT0 are R/W1C, not STAT1.\n\n*** TITLE CORRECTED 2026-09-07: it said FOUR registers and the true count is SIX. *** Derived, not recalled: `exit(1)` occurs SEVEN times in DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_ioc) -- six guarded value-arms (LCA_IOC_CONF :215, TB_ENA :235, W_BASE0 :250, W_MASK0 :265, W_BASE1 :289, W_MASK1 :304) and the `default:` arm at :324, which this round does NOT close.  The four window registers are presumably what the row was opened on; CONF and TB_ENA carry the same shape and were folded in later without the title following.  **A stale title is how a queue row is READ** -- the brief that goes to the panel says six, and a reader comparing the two would have had to guess which was right.  W_T_BASE0 (:272) and W_T_BASE1 (:311) confirmed UNGUARDED: plain readback/store, no value test -- which is the sibling warrant the design brief puts to the panel.\n\n*** AND THE PARAGRAPH ABOVE WAS ITSELF CORRUPTED WHEN FIRST WRITTEN -- BY THE SHELL, QUIETLY\nENOUGH TO SURVIVE REVIEW. ***  It was applied through `python -c \"...\"` with DOUBLE quotes, and\nbash performed COMMAND SUBSTITUTION on the backticks inside it: `exit(1)` and `default:` were\nEXECUTED and replaced by their empty output, leaving readable prose with two words silently\nmissing -- \"Derived, not recalled:  occurs SEVEN times\".  bash did print two errors, but python\nran on the mangled source anyway and the write SUCCEEDED, so the record was wrong while the\ncommand reported success.\n\n**Inside a double-quoted shell argument a backtick is a COMMAND, not a quote mark.**  That is the\ncarrier's backslash-through-heredoc class arriving on a new character, and with the same\nsignature: the failure lands in the FILE, not in the exit status.\n\n*** AND THE REPAIR FAILED TWICE MORE BEFORE IT LANDED, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING. ***  The\nfix was retried as `python -c '...'` with SINGLE quotes -- correct for backticks -- and bash\nrejected it, because the note explaining the hazard contains the word \"carrier's\", whose\nAPOSTROPHE terminated the shell quote.  So: double quotes lose to backticks, single quotes lose to\napostrophes, and English prose about shell quoting reliably contains both.\n\n**The rule is not a better quoting style.  It is: PROSE DESTINED FOR A RECORD GOES INTO A SCRIPT\nFILE, never through `-c`.**  This paragraph was written that way and landed intact on the first\nattempt.\n\n*** PASS-1 DESIGN PANEL 2026-09-07 (preserved: regress/artifacts/lcabadval/panel_p1_20260907/ -- _scratchpad is never committed).  PROVISIONAL: FIVE OF THE\nLANDED SEATS ARE READ HERE AND **CODEX WAS STILL STREAMING** WHEN THIS WAS WRITTEN (its file grew\n14,943 -> 30,594 bytes between two reads and its tail is mid-`exec`, not a verdict).  Any ruling\nshort of the roster is provisional on its face, so no disposition is adopted below. ***\n\nSEATS: grok, glm, deepseek, minimax and the kimi-k3:cloud SUBSTITUTE answered (5); codex IN FLIGHT;\nthe kimi CLI WALLED on an explicit 403 weekly limit, so that seat is COVERED, not lost; agy\nDELIBERATELY NOT FIRED (measurement reserve, owner ruling 2026-08-30); opus5 and fable5 NOT FIRED,\nreserved for the 2026-09-09 adjudication.\n\n*** THE PANEL'S DOMINANT FINDING IS THAT MY BRIEF POSED THE WRONG QUESTION, AND FOUR SEATS REACHED\nIT INDEPENDENTLY: THE FORK IS MIS-POSED AS ONE DISPOSITION FOR ALL SIX ARMS. ***\n\nThe kimi substitute put it sharpest, and it is a self-inflicted wound: *\"The fork as posed (one\nanswer for the file) repeats the exact error the #447 census inlines a warning against.\"*  **My\nbrief QUOTED `#447`'s own sentence -- \"Saying 'the store is upstream' once for the file would have\nbeen wrong three times out of four.  Check it per site.\" -- and then asked for a single answer for\nsix arms.**  The warning was inlined as a settled premise and violated in the questions below it.\n\n**WHERE THE SEATS AGREE (4 of 5):** the five PURE-STORAGE arms and `LCA_IOC_CONF` must be decided\nseparately, because CONF is the only one with a consumer (`bus = d->ioc_conf & 1`).  For the five,\npremise 6 survives -- grok's formulation is the one to keep: **\"B copies RCR1's *edit* without\nRCR1's *reason*.\"**  RCR1's warrant was that the stored value has a meaning the model otherwise\nhonours; for the five window/TB_ENA fields nothing honours anything, so the edit does not transfer.\nThe kimi substitute sharpens even the agreement: the right analogy for the five is not \"the lie is\npre-existing\" but **`#447`'s ICR cell -- *no honored state to corrupt*** -- which is a stronger and\ndifferently-grounded warrant than the one my premise 6 offered.\n\n**WHERE THEY SPLIT, AND IT IS A REAL DISPUTE TO SETTLE BY MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN BY COUNT:**\n\n    CONF -> B                 glm       \"premise 2 names a live consumer, so CONF is exactly RCR1-shaped\"\n    CONF -> A, more so        minimax   \"the consumer uses only bit 0, so a rejected value leaves it\n                                         correct -- and B can SILENTLY BREAK it if the retained old\n                                         value differs in bit 0\"\n    CONF -> decline or mask   kimi      split per arm\n    CONF -> neither           grok      \"treat it as a bit-mask leftover, not a window\"\n\n**minimax's objection to B is the one with a measurable consequence** and it is the opposite of\nglm's: under B a rejected CONF write leaves the PREVIOUS value in place, and if the previous value\ndiffers in bit 0 the PCI bus selection silently changes -- so B is not merely conservative there, it\ncan alter a consumer that A leaves correct.  That is checkable and should be checked before either\nis adopted.\n\n**THE DISSENT, recorded as a dissent and not as noise:** deepseek alone says premise 6 is unsound\nfor all six -- *\"the guard's accepted-value set is the model's CONTRACT.  Storing a rejected value\nand returning it on readback creates a new false belief that the value is supported, even if\naccepted values are also unimplemented.  The existing lie for accepted values does not license\nexpanding the set of lies.\"*  It also warns the reasoning generalises: it would justify removing any\nstore-before-guard exit anywhere.  **That generalisation objection is the strongest single argument\nagainst A and is not answered by the five-versus-one split**, because it attacks the warrant rather\nthan its scope.\n\n**A BRIEF DEFECT OF MINE, named by glm and correct:** premise 2's field counts were *asserted, not\ninlined* -- I ran the greps and wrote the conclusion instead of the OUTPUT.  This project's own rule\nis that a brief inlines the DERIVER'S OUTPUT rather than a literal, precisely so a packet-fed seat\ncan check it.  glm could not settle five of the six arms from the packet for exactly that reason and\nsaid so honestly rather than ratifying.\n\n**NOT ADOPTED, PENDING codex AND THE ROSTER.**  The next brief is per-arm by construction, carries\nthe deriver output rather than its summary, and puts minimax's silently-broken-consumer prediction\nas a question a measurement can answer.\n\n*** THE grok BODY, READ IN FULL 2026-09-07 -- AND NONE OF THE FOLLOWING WAS IN ITS VERDICT LINE.\nThis is the read-the-body rule paying off on the same day a row was filed about not reading. ***\n\n**1. IT ANSWERS deepseek's DISSENT, which the five/one split could not.**  deepseek's objection was\nthat premise 6 generalises -- it would license removing any store-before-guard exit anywhere.  grok\nscopes it precisely: *\"premise 6 is not the reasoning that lets every store-before-guard site keep\nits store.  It is the reasoning that lets a site keep its store WHEN NO ACCEPTED VALUE OF THAT FIELD\nIS CONSUMED.  That is #447's own per-site rule, not an evasion of it.\"*  The warrant is a per-site\ntest with a checkable predicate, not a blanket.\n\n**2. PREMISE 5 SCORED: \"The comments exist.  The transfer does not.\"**  RCR1 refuses CONSUMED\nFEATURE BITS; these guards whitelist UNIMPLEMENTED CONSTANTS.  That is the distinction the whole\nfork turned on, and it is sharper than my premise 6 offered.\n\n**3. *** OPTION A OPENS A NEW KILL PATH, AND THIS IS THE ROUND'S SAFETY FINDING. ***  Under A, a CONF\nwrite with extra bits that ALSO sets bit 0 reaches `lca_pci_conf`'s `bus != 0` exit(1) -- *\"newly\nreachable ... today that write dies in the CONF guard first.\"*  **A is not purely a reduction; it\nconverts one kill into a different reachable one.**  Any round shipping A must say so, and should\nconsider whether `lca_pci_conf` is in scope with it.\n\n**4. THE FILE'S OWN IDIOM IS A, AND THE GUARDS ARE THE OUTLIERS.**  In the SAME switch:\n`LCA_IOC_BASE` (HAE) and `TBIA` already silently ignore; `W_T_BASE0/1` already accept-and-store; and\nthe `dev >= 21` loop already deletes a kill and substitutes a default.  *\"The value guards are the\noutliers in THIS switch, not the house style.\"*  That is a stronger warrant than anything my brief\noffered, and it is one grep away.\n\n**5. THE ANNOUNCE TEXT WOULD BECOME A WRONG RECORD THE MOMENT A LANDS.**  The current string says\n*\"the only implemented value\"* about a value that **is not implemented**.  The honest once-message is\n*\"window translation / extra CONF bits not implemented\"*, not *\"illegal encoding\"*.  Rewrite it or\ndelete it with the exit -- do not carry it forward.\n\n**6. OPTION-SET CORRECTIONS.**  `#443`'s restore is *\"B wearing extra lines\"* -- observationally B\nfor readback, since nothing re-enters between the store and the `break`; not a distinct member.\n**STAT0 is the architecturally correct error path for LCA** (`IOC_STAT0_ERR` is R/W1C) and is\n**BLOCKED**: STAT0/STAT1 have no case and hit `default:` exit(1) -- *\"You cannot report an error\nthrough a register that currently kills the host.\"*  That member owes a STAT0 implementation first,\nwhich is a different round.  **FAULT confirmed unavailable**: `memory_rw.c` raises on `res <= 0` only\nunder MEM_MIPS and MEM_M88K; Alpha compiles with MEM_ALPHA and has no arm, so `return 0` fails the\naccess with no guest exception.\n\n**7. MASKING CONF IS UNLICENSED, WITH A REASON I DID NOT HAVE: THERE IS NO DATASHEET.**  No 21066\ndocument is in the packet or in the tracked `_scratchpad/*.pdf` list, so RAZ/WI versus store-full is\n**not settleable from this tree**.  Premise 3 stands and masking would be an addition without a\nwarrant.\n\n*** 8. THE WITNESS IS NOT PENDING -- THE RECIPE EXISTS.  This moves the round off documented-only. ***\n* **Machine: `alphabook1` ONLY.**  `machine_alpha.c`'s `device_add(machine, \"lca\")` is under\n  `ST_ALPHABOOK1` and no other subtype.\n* **Addresses** (K0SEG | physical, because `alpha_translate_v2p` identity-maps K0SEG in kernel mode):\n  CONF `0xfffffc0180000020`, TB_ENA `...00a0`, BASE0 `...0100`, BASE1 `...0120`, MASK0 `...0140`,\n  MASK1 `...0160`.\n* **`regress/lca_confwidth_probe.py` ALREADY issues a guest `stq`/`ldq` through this exact decode at\n  CONF on unmodified `alphabook1`**; the other five are further cases of the same DEVICE_ACCESS on\n  the same mapping.\n* **Three traps that file already paid for, so a new probe need not rediscover them:** debugger\n  `dump` skips `DM_DEFAULT` handlers; a debugger `put d` of a killing value IS a host kill but is\n  NOT a guest instruction (so it does not witness); and HAE at window offset 0 silently succeeds, so\n  **a miss control must not use `LCA_IOC_BASE+0`**.\n\n**9. THE KILL CENSUS FOR THE WHOLE FILE:** eleven live `exit(1)` today; **A removes six; five\nremain** -- the `default:` arm (both reads AND writes, covering STAT0/STAT1 and the rest of the\n512 MB IOC window), `lca_pci_conf`'s `bus != 0`, `lca_isa`'s `ofs > len` (filed as [[lcaisalane]]),\nand the bare `lca_interrupt_assert`/`_deassert` TODO pair (member 7, not this round).  Residuals not\nof this class: HAE writes discarded with no storage; TBIA ignored; `default:` still narrows\n`relative_addr` through `(int)` -- **`#506` fixed the `idata` half only**; and [[lcashiftub]].\n\n**10. A CENSUS ERROR IN THIS ROW'S OWN HISTORY:** the guards are TWO predicate shapes, not one --\nbit-masks (CONF, TB_ENA) and equality whitelists (the four windows) -- **and `W_BASE0` accepts TWO\nvalues**, so the row's original \"four registers accept exactly one value each\" framing was false of\n`BASE0` before the title was even corrected to six.  One DESIGN still covers all six (delete the\nexit, keep the store, optional latch); one design does NOT if you pick B, because *\"B is a coherent\nanswer only for a consumed field, which five of these are not.\"*\n\n*** THE codex SEAT LANDED LAST AND OVERTURNED THE PANEL ON A FACT CHECKABLE IN ONE GREP.  MY\nPREMISE 6 IS DEAD, AND SO IS THE FORMULATION I HAD CREDITED grok WITH. ***\n\n**codex's verdict:** *\"Your argument for A fails as written: RCR1 also has no functional consumer.\nI recommend B for pass 1 as a conservative policy choice; hardware-correct write/readback behavior\nremains unproved.\"*\n\n**VERIFIED HERE, not taken on trust** -- `grep -rn rtc_rcr1 src/` returns exactly FOUR occurrences:\n\n    dev_sh4.c:388    uint8_t   rtc_rcr1;              declaration\n    dev_sh4.c:2776   odata = d->rtc_rcr1;             READBACK\n    dev_sh4.c:2781   ...landed in rtc_rcr1 and...     INSIDE THE COMMENT\n    dev_sh4.c:2805   d->rtc_rcr1 = idata;             STORE\n\n**Zero functional consumers.**  And the comparison that settles it: `ioc_conf` also has four\noccurrences, but its fourth is `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1` -- a real consumer.  **So RCR1 is consumed\nLESS than LCA's CONF, not more.**\n\n*** WHAT THIS DESTROYS. ***  My premise 6 claimed LCA differs from RCR1 because *\"RCR1's stored value\nhas a meaning the model otherwise honours\"*.  It has no such meaning: the model stores it and reads\nit back, exactly like the five LCA window fields.  **grok's formulation, which I recorded above as\n\"the keeper\" -- *\"RCR1 refuses CONSUMED FEATURE BITS; these guards whitelist UNIMPLEMENTED\nCONSTANTS\"* -- is FALSE IN ITS FIRST HALF.**  So is my own gloss that `#447`'s edit \"does not\ntransfer\".\n\n**IT TRANSFERS EXACTLY.**  `#447` faced a store-before-guard field with NO functional consumer,\nand chose to MOVE THE STORE BELOW THE GUARD.  That is the LCA shape, arm for arm.  The tree's own\nprecedent is therefore evidence FOR B, which is what codex, deepseek and (for CONF) glm all said.\n\n*** AND THE SURVIVING ARGUMENT IS THE ONE deepseek RAISED FIRST AND codex SHARPENED: THE GUEST IS\nITSELF A CONSUMER OF READBACK. ***  codex: *\"Previously, a rejected write ended execution before\nsubsequent guest code could observe that value.  A creates that observation and allows the guest to\nmake further decisions from it.  Existing incomplete behavior for accepted settings does not\nestablish that these newly admitted executions are equally harmless.\"*  That argument is untouched by\nthe consumer census, because it does not depend on a MODEL consumer at all -- and it is precisely\nwhat `#447`'s comment means by *\"the guest writes CIE|AIE, reads CIE|AIE back, and believes the RTC\nwill interrupt it.\"*\n\n**TALLY, and the argument decides it rather than the count:** codex -> B; deepseek -> B; glm -> B for\nCONF and not settleable for the five; kimi substitute -> split per arm; grok -> A; minimax -> A.  But\ngrok's and minimax's A both rest on the consumed/unconsumed distinction, **and that distinction is\nnow measured false.**  Count the arguments, not the seats: the load-bearing premise under the A\nposition has been refuted by a grep.\n\n**THIS IS THE THIRD REVERSAL IN THIS ROUND'S DESIGN AND IT COST NOTHING, WHICH IS THE POINT OF THE\nHOLD DISCIPLINE.**  No code is committed; the round has produced adjudication text and zero product\nchanges.  The earlier records above -- premise 6 \"sound for five arms\", grok's formulation as \"the\nkeeper\" -- are left standing WITH this correction beneath them rather than silently edited, because\na future reader needs to see that the panel's majority reasoning was overturned and by what.\n\n*** WHAT IS NOW OWED BEFORE ANY FIX: the ARM-BY-ARM design, with the RCR1 precedent read as\nSUPPORTING B, and codex's readback-consumer argument answered rather than assumed away.  The\nquestion that remains genuinely open is minimax's measurable one -- under B a rejected CONF write\nretains the previous value, and codex adds that W_MASK0/W_BASE1/W_MASK1 RESET TO ZERO, which is NOT\nin their accepted set, so \"retain the last accepted value\" is not even well-defined at reset.  That\nis a concrete defect in B as stated and it needs an answer (retain? force the reset value? decline\nonly the guarded bits?) before B can ship. ***\n\n*** AND THE SHARPEST LESSON IS NOT THE REVERSAL, IT IS HOW MY GLOSS BEAT MY OWN PRIMARY SOURCE. ***\n\nThe brief inlined #447s RCR1 comment VERBATIM.  That comment argues purely from READBACK:\n\n    the guest writes CIE|AIE, reads CIE|AIE back, and believes the RTC will\n    interrupt it.  It never will.\n\n**It never claims a model consumer.**  The consumed/unconsumed framing is MINE -- premise 6 glossed\nit as RCR1s stored value having a meaning the model otherwise honours -- and grok adopted my\ngloss rather than the quoted text sitting directly above it, producing B copies RCR1s edit\nwithout RCR1s reason.  codex went to the source, grepped the field, and found the gloss false.\n\n> **INLINING THE PRIMARY SOURCE DOES NOT PROTECT A BRIEF FROM A WRONG SUMMARY PLACED BESIDE IT.**\n> A seat reads the summary as the QUESTION and the quote as SUPPORTING MATERIAL, so a false gloss\n> steers the answer even when the refuting text is on the same page.  This projects premise-audit\n> rule says to state load-bearing premises AS QUESTIONS; the refinement is that a premise which\n> RESTATES an inlined quotation is the most dangerous kind, because it looks already verified.\n\n*The cheap defence is the one codex used and I did not: when a premise characterises what some\nother code does, GREP THAT CODE.  `grep -rn rtc_rcr1 src/` is four lines of output and it was\navailable to me before the brief went out.*\n\n*** codex, `#511` PASS 2, CHALLENGES THE \"GRACEFUL DEGRADATION PRECEDENT\" THAT BOTH LCA ROWS LEAN\nON. ***\n\n*\"`DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)`'s fallback selects device zero and forwards the access through\n`bus_pci_setaddr`.  **That is an ALIASING DECISION, not by itself proof of harmless degradation.**\nThe comment's 'transferable warrant' needs its own justification; the shift repair does not establish\nit.\"*\n\nThe `dev >= 21` arm -- commented-out `fatal()`/`exit(1)` with `dev = 0;` substituted -- has been\ncited repeatedly here, including by [[lcabadval]], as the file's own in-tree warrant for deleting a\nhost kill.  **codex's point is that substituting device ZERO is not a decline: it silently redirects\nthe access to a DIFFERENT DEVICE.**  Whether that is harmless is a separate question nobody has\nasked, and \"someone already did it in this file\" is not the same as \"it is right\".\n\n> **A TRANSFERRED WARRANT NEEDS ITS OWN JUSTIFICATION, NOT JUST A PRECEDENT.**  This is the\n> option-set rule's own caution turned around: grepping the fork for an existing idiom finds what the\n> tree DOES, which is evidence about practice and not about correctness.\n\n*Not resolved here.  Recorded on both rows because both cite it, and any round that leans on it owes\nthe justification codex is asking for.*\n\n*** THE THIRD OPTION-SET MEMBER IS NAMED, AND THE TWO-OPTION FRAMING WAS REALLY A ONE-OPTION\nFRAMING.  codex, 2026-09-08; the two load-bearing claims VERIFIED here before recording. ***\n\n**VERDICT: C -- decline the out-of-set write, PRESERVE the previous register value, announce once.**\n\n### 1.  A AND B OVERLAP.  There was never a choice between them.\n\n*\"Your reconstructed A and B overlap: both store the written value and continue.  With the existing\nreads retained, B describes SHADOW STORAGE WITH READBACK, not a write-only register.  It supplies no\ndistinct state-transition policy.\"*  **So the panel that argued A-versus-B was arguing about one\noption under two names** -- the option-set-completeness failure in its purest form, and this row had\nit for two rounds.\n\n### 2.  *** WHAT `#447` ACTUALLY DOES IS DECLINE-AND-PRESERVE, AND I HAD IT BACKWARDS. ***\n\nThe precedent was cited as \"a register with no consumer, where the write is accepted\".  The\nimplementation says otherwise -- **the store is in the ELSE branch**:\n\n    if (idata & (SH_RCR1_CIE | SH_RCR1_AIE)) {\n            /* bounded diagnostic */\n    } else\n            d->rtc_rcr1 = idata;\n\n*\"Unsupported writes preserve the previous value.  They are NOT accepted into backing storage.\"*  And\nthe reason recorded in that comment is a READBACK concern -- echoing interrupt-enable flags would\nsuggest functionality the model does not provide -- so **absence of a consumer neither disqualifies\ndecline-and-preserve nor argues for accepting everything**: readback is guest-observable on its own.\n\n### 3.  *** `ioc_conf` HAS A FUNCTIONAL CONSUMER.  VERIFIED. ***\n\n`dev_lca.c:121` is `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1;`, feeding the PCI decode.  **The row's \"no functional\nconsumer\" premise is false for CONF**, whatever it is for the other five.\n\n> **AND IT TIES TWO HIGH ROWS TOGETHER.**  `lcaconfbus1kill` is precisely about that consumer: a\n> write of `0x1` makes `bus == 1` and arms the `bus != 0` host kill.  `0x1` is INSIDE this arm's\n> accepted set `{0, 1}`, so **C does not protect against it and must not be described as if it\n> does.**  The two rows are separate defects on one register and both need fixing.\n\n### 4.  THE RESET QUESTION IS DISSOLVED, NOT ANSWERED -- and the reason improves the fix.\n\n*\"The predicates govern WRITES, not all possible stored states.\"*  Initialization assigns zero with\nno guard; reads return the stored value with no guard; **and the write assigns BEFORE the guard is\nevaluated.**  So the model can return zero at reset while refusing a later write of zero:\n*\"that asymmetry is established; it is not a logical contradiction.\"*\n\n    register  writes the guard accepts        reset 0 passes?\n    CONF      {0, 1}                          yes\n    TB_ENA    {0, 0x80}                       yes\n    W_BASE0   {0, 0x300800000}                yes\n    W_MASK0   {0x700000}                      NO\n    W_BASE1   {0x240000000}                   NO\n    W_MASK1   {0x3ff00000}                    NO\n\n*** MY OWN ADDITION, from verifying the store-order claim at `:233` vs the guard at `:236`: BECAUSE\nTHE STORE HAPPENS FIRST, SIMPLY DELETING THE `exit(1)` LEAVES THE BAD VALUE STORED. ***  That is not\na weaker version of C -- it is a DIFFERENT OBSERVABLE STATE, readable by the guest through the\nexisting read arm.  **C requires moving each store behind its guard, exactly as RCR1 does.**\n\n### 5.  THE SIBLING EVIDENCE SUPPORTS BOTH POLICIES AND SELECTS NEITHER\n\nInside this same switch:\n\n    LCA_IOC_BASE, LCA_IOC_TBIA      discard writes; reads return 0; success\n    LCA_IOC_W_T_BASE0/1             store writes and return them on reads\n    default:                        diagnose and TERMINATE, no backing storage\n\n*\"Thus the sibling evidence supports BOTH discarding writes and retaining shadow state.  It does not\nselect a universal policy.\"*  **A useful negative result: the grep-the-fork rule found the member but\ncannot settle the policy here**, because the file is internally inconsistent.\n\n*`default:` is explicitly OUT of this round -- it has no previous value to preserve, so C is not even\nexpressible there.*\n\n### 6.  THE CENSUS SURVIVES ITS AUDIT\n\nEleven live explicit `exit(1)`: the six value arms at **248, 268, 283, 298, 322, 337**, plus\n**73, 85, 167, 196, 357**.  A bare `exit(` grep returns thirteen; two are comments (the explanatory\nmention and the disabled call whose live fallback is `dev = 0`).  *This is the row's third census and\nthe first to survive.\n\n*** CORRECTION, WITHIN THE HOUR AND BEFORE THIS PROPAGATED: \"THE THIRD OPTION-SET MEMBER\" IS WRONG.\nTHERE IS NO THIRD MEMBER.  THE OPTION SET WAS ALREADY RIGHT AND **MY BRIEF CORRUPTED IT**. ***\n\n`tools/pipeline/fable_queue.md` §7i states the fork exactly as the earlier panel left it:\n\n    A  delete fatal()+exit(1) from all six arms, KEEP the store that already ran\n    B  DECLINE -- move the store BELOW the guard, as #447 did for SH4's RCR1\n\n**The queue's B IS codex's C.**  Decline-and-preserve was already ON the table, correctly described,\nwith the `#447` precedent already attached to it.  My `DISPOSITION2_brief.md` reconstructed B as\n*\"treat the register as write-only backing state\"* -- which is NOT what B says -- and that\nreconstruction is what made A and B overlap.\n\n> **SO THE OPTION-SET FAILURE WAS IN THE BRIEF, NOT IN THE ROW.**  The row had two genuinely\n> distinct members; the brief collapsed them into one and then asked the panel for a third.  *A\n> brief that mis-states the options gets a panel arguing about the wrong set* -- which is the\n> option-set rule's own warning, arriving from the direction it does not usually name: not a MISSING\n> member, an ALTERED one.\n\n**codex flagged this itself and I read past it:** *\"If the earlier panel called decline-and-preserve\n'B,' its letter assignment differs from your reconstruction.  The quoted verdict alone cannot\nresolve that historical naming.  Its implemented precedent is unambiguous.\"*  **It hedged the exact\nerror, derived the right disposition anyway from the `#447` source, and I recorded its answer under a\nletter that does not exist in this row's own queue entry.**\n\n*** WHAT SURVIVES THE CORRECTION, and it is nearly all of it: ***\n\n* **The DISPOSITION is unchanged and now has two independent derivations** -- the earlier panel\n  reached decline-and-preserve as B, and codex reached it from `#447`'s implementation without\n  trusting my framing.\n* **The `#447` reading is new and load-bearing**: the store sits in the ELSE branch, so unsupported\n  writes PRESERVE the previous value.  *The brief had that backwards.*\n* **`ioc_conf`'s consumer at `dev_lca.c:121` is new**, and it ties this row to `lcaconfbus1kill`.\n* **The store-before-guard order at `:233`/`:236` is new**, and it is what makes A and B differ in\n  OBSERVABLE STATE rather than only in tidiness.\n* **The reset-coherence dissolution and the accepted-set table are new.**\n* **The census survived a third audit.**\n\n**What does NOT survive is the sentence \"the third option-set member is named\".**  Nothing was\nmissing.  *Recorded in place rather than deleted, because a correction that hides what it corrects\nis how this row got to three reversals in the first place.*\n\n*** A FACT THIS ROW'S QUESTION DOES NOT CARRY, ESTABLISHED 2026-09-08 FROM THREE INDEPENDENT FILES:\nOPTION-SET MEMBER 5 (FAULT) IS NOT AVAILABLE ON ALPHA. ***\n\nThis is an Alpha machine, and the adjudication of this row weighs dispositions from the standing\noption set.  **FAULT looks available and is not**, which is the option-set-completeness problem\ninverted -- not a missing member, a PHANTOM one.\n\n    memory_rw.c        the device-failure dispatcher has MIPS and M88K exception arms and\n                       NO ALPHA ARM                          (found via dev_lca.c, `lcaisalane`)\n    memory_alpha.c     no MM-fault dispatch exists at all    (found via `alphapteprot`)\n    cpu_alpha_palcode  ALPHA_KENTRY_MM and ALPHA_MMCSR_* are DEFINED and have NO CONSUMERS;\n                       kentry[] is written by wrent and initialised to a dummy PAL, never\n                       dispatched                            (found via `alphatbinegsel` Q3)\n\n**Three briefs reached this from three directions, none of them asked about it.**  And a fourth\nseat priced it: choosing FAULT obliges building delivery first -- a PAL frame,\n`kentry[ALPHA_KENTRY_MM]`, MMCSR/VA, and teaching `pc_to_pointers` about a redirected PC.  *\"That is\na new IMPLEMENT round, not a de-fatalisation.\"*\n\n> **SO ANY ANSWER TO THIS ROW THAT REACHES FOR FAULT IS REACHING FOR A ROUND THAT DOES NOT EXIST\n> YET.**  It remains a legitimate ANSWER -- but it is an answer with a prerequisite, and the\n> prerequisite is large.  Recorded here so the adjudication prices it rather than assuming it.\n\n*** THE NOTE ABOVE UNDERSTATED THE SCOPE, AND THE CORRECTION IS THE USEFUL PART: FAULT IS NOT\n\"UNAVAILABLE ON ALPHA\".  IT IS AVAILABLE ON **MIPS AND M88K ONLY**, FOR EVERY ARCHITECTURE IN THIS\nTREE.  Derived 2026-09-08 by opening the dispatcher. ***\n\n`src/cpus/memory_rw.c`, the device-failure path -- the whole of it:\n\n    if (res <= 0 && !no_exceptions) {\n            debug(\"[ %s device '%s' addr %08lx failed ]\");\n    #ifdef MEM_MIPS\n            mips_cpu_exception(cpu, cache == CACHE_INSTRUCTION? EXCEPTION_IBE : EXCEPTION_DBE, ...);\n    #endif\n    #ifdef MEM_M88K\n            m88k_exception(cpu, cache == CACHE_INSTRUCTION ? M88K_EXCEPTION_INSTRUCTION_ACCESS\n                                                           : M88K_EXCEPTION_DATA_ACCESS, 0);\n    #endif\n            return MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED;\n    }\n\n**TWO `#ifdef` ARMS.  There is no ARM arm, no Alpha arm, no PPC arm and no SH arm** -- every other\narchitecture reaches `return MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED` with no exception delivered at all.\n\n> **SO OPTION-SET MEMBER 5 IS A PHANTOM ON EVERY ARCHITECTURE BUT TWO**, and a round on an ARM, SH,\n> PPC or Alpha device that reaches for FAULT is reaching for a round that does not exist yet.\n\n*Found because I wrote the OPPOSITE into a brief.  Assessing `fbfiqsilent` -- an ARM footbridge row\n-- I told the seat that \"member 5 FAULT may genuinely be available here, `memory_rw`'s\ndevice-failure path has an ARM arm.\"  It replied that the claimed arm **is absent from this tree**,\nand opening the file confirms it.  **I was one brief away from spreading a false availability claim\nacross the ARM queue**, having just corrected three rows for the Alpha version of it.*\n\n*** THE 21066 MANUAL IS LOCAL NOW, AND IT PUTS A NUMBER ON THIS ROW FOR THE FIRST TIME -- BUT THE\nNUMBER IS MY DERIVATION AND IS OUT WITH A PANEL, NOT ESTABLISHED. ***\n\n**QUOTED, `_scratchpad/21066_66a_hrm.txt:9572-9578`, read locally:**\n\n> *\"The window mask register IOC_W_MASK provides a mask corresponding to bits <31:20> of an\n> incoming PCI address.  The size of each window is programmed to be from 1 MB to 4 GB ...  Table\n> 6-6 shows the supported values of IOC_W_MASK register bits <31:20>; **other values are\n> unsupported**.\"*\n\n**Table 6-6 (`:9581-9598`) has THIRTEEN rows** -- a doubling run from 1 MB (`0x000`) to 4 GB\n(`0xfff`), each a contiguous run of low ones.\n\n**MY DERIVATION, flagged as mine:** the register value is the field shifted left 20, so the fork's\ntwo accepted constants are\n\n    LCA_IOC_W_MASK0   accepts ONLY 0x700000     ->  field 0x007  ->    8 MB   legal, 1 of 13\n    LCA_IOC_W_MASK1   accepts ONLY 0x3ff00000   ->  field 0x3ff  ->    1 GB   legal, 1 of 13\n\nand the legal set is exactly `(f & (f + 1)) == 0`, verified over all thirteen.\n\n> **IF THAT IS RIGHT, the model terminates the host on TWELVE DOCUMENTED-LEGAL VALUES PER MASK\n> REGISTER -- 24 across the two -- and a guest programming any window size other than 8 MB / 1 GB\n> is doing something the hardware manual explicitly supports.**  That is a much sharper severity\n> statement than *\"rejects values outside a small accepted set\"*, because it names the denominator.\n\n*** IT IS NOT RECORDED AS ESTABLISHED, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. ***  The load-bearing step is my own\narithmetic, done in one sitting, on a table extracted by `pdftotext` -- and this project's rule is\nthat an extracted table can stagger names against values.  **Table 6-6 LOOKS self-calibrating (a\ndoubling bit-run beside a doubling size run, thirteen against thirteen), but the calibration rule\nsays one point is only valid for a UNIFORM stagger and you cannot tell which kind you have from one\npoint.**  So it went out to a panel with *\"check the premises, not just the derivations\"* and with\nthe derivation shown step by step:\n\n    regress/artifacts/lcabadval/MANUAL_brief.md   file-reading seats: codex, grok -- also asked to\n                                                  read S6.4 for the OTHER FOUR arms and to give a\n                                                  count WITH ITS DENOMINATOR per register\n    regress/artifacts/lcabadval/PACKET_brief.md   packet-fed seats: glm, deepseek, minimax, kimi --\n                                                  Table 6-6 inlined verbatim so they can check the\n                                                  arithmetic without file access\n\n**WHAT IS STILL OPEN, and the brief asks for each explicitly:** the manual enumerates legal values\nfor W_MASK and (as far as I read) NOT for W_BASE, so the two registers may need different\ndispositions; `0x300800000` and `0x240000000` are 34-bit values and I have not established how wide\nthese registers are or what bits above 31 mean; and the more dangerous error direction is a real\nquestion -- terminating on a legal value, versus accepting a value the model's own address decode\nthen does not honour.\n\n*** THE PANEL ANSWERED: SIX SEATS, MY COUNT CONFIRMED -- AND THE ONE SEAT THAT DISSENTED MADE THE\nDEFECT BIGGER, NOT SMALLER. ***\n\n    codex     CONFIRMS 12/13 per register, 24/26 register-value pairs, and Table 6-6 IS\n              self-calibrating -- it parsed all thirteen rows and checked the adjacent runs\n    glm       \"the S3 arithmetic is right -- I re-derived every step and found no defect\"\n    kimi      \"your derivation is right\": shift, both fields, the count, the invariant\n    minimax   \"the S3 derivation is correct\"\n    grok      answered, 14,818 b\n    deepseek  CORRECT ONLY IF bits <19:0> must be zero -- AND THAT PREMISE IS NOT ESTABLISHED\n              BY THE TEXT I QUOTED\n\n*** deepseek IS RIGHT, AND codex SETTLED IT FROM THE OTHER END. ***  My quote enumerates bits\n<31:20> and says nothing about <19:0>.  codex read the preface: **RES fields are type `X` --\nIGNORED ON WRITES, unpredictable on reads, NOT must-be-zero** (`21066_66a_hrm.txt:955`, `:985`).\n\n> **SO THE GUARD IS WORSE THAN \"ACCEPTS ONE OF THIRTEEN\".  IT TESTS THE WHOLE 64-BIT OPERAND\n> AGAINST A SINGLE LITERAL -- `if (idata != 0x700000ULL)` -- WHILE THE HARDWARE DEFINES A 12-BIT\n> FIELD AND IGNORES THE REST.**  A guest writing `0x7000ff`, functionally identical on real\n> hardware, terminates the emulator.  *deepseek said so in as many words: \"if lower bits are\n> ignored, the number of legal register values is much larger than 13 per mask, and the code is\n> even more restrictive.\"*\n\n**AND IT SUPPLIED THE TRAP A NAIVE FIX WOULD HIT:** the invariant must be applied to the SHIFTED\nFIELD.  `(idata & (idata + 1)) == 0` on the full value **REJECTS THE LEGAL `0x700000`**, because\n`0x700000 & 0x700001 == 0x700000`.  The guard is `f = idata >> 20; (f & (f + 1)) == 0`.\n\n*** FOUR MORE CORRECTIONS FROM codex, EACH NARROWING OR SHARPENING A CLAIM OF MINE. ***\n\n* **MY OWN TABLE DROPPED A ROW.**  `MANUAL_brief.md` reformatted Table 6-6 into three columns and\n  **lost `0000 0001 1111` = 32 MB** -- twelve rows under a sentence saying thirteen.  The PACKET\n  brief has all thirteen.  *A transcription error in the brief that asks others to check my\n  transcription.*\n* **ALIGNMENT AND NON-OVERLAP, which my quote stopped one paragraph short of** (`:9618`, S6.4.9):\n  bases must align naturally to window size and windows must not overlap.  **With the two accepted\n  bases held FIXED, window 0 admits only the four sizes through 8 MB and window 1 the eleven\n  through 1 GB** -- so in that configuration the guards reject 3/4 and 10/11.  All thirteen sizes\n  are supported; changing size can require changing the surrounding configuration.\n* **`IOC_CONF` IS A TWO-BIT FIELD, values 0..3, so `idata & ~1` rejects 2 of 4** defined settings --\n  I had quoted the guard without checking the field width.\n* **`IOC_TB_ENA` IS NOT A FUNCTIONAL DEFECT AT ALL: TEN has exactly two states and BOTH are\n  accepted -- 0/2.**  Its only defect is the ignored-bit one.  *Recorded because a round that\n  \"fixes six arms\" would be overclaiming.*\n\n*** THE SHARPEST SINGLE FINDING, AND IT IS NOT MINE: THE MANUAL REQUIRES INITIALISING BOTH BASE\nREGISTERS TO ZERO (S6.4.14, `:10788`), AND `LCA_IOC_W_BASE1` REJECTS EXACTLY THAT. ***  A guest\nfollowing the manual own documented initialisation sequence terminates the emulator.  That is not a\ncorner value -- it is the prescribed setup.\n\n**Also established:** the registers are **64-bit, quadword access only** (S6.4 excludes longword,\n`:10175`); and **all six arms STORE THE VALUE BEFORE TESTING IT** (`d->window_mask_0 = idata;`\nprecedes the guard), so device state is already updated when the process dies.\n\n**DISPOSITION IS NOT SETTLED HERE.**  deepseek proposes accept-with-backing-storage for the values\nthe manual or existing code already treats as legal, plus a `(once)` decline otherwise, and warns\nagainst expanding the W_BASE set without evidence since the manual enumerates none.  That is a\ndesign question for the round, and the round must not silently widen an accept set.\n\n*** THE FIX DESIGN IS IN, AND IT QUALIFIES THREE OF THE ASSESS PANEL'S FINDINGS -- ONE OF THEM A\nCONCLUSION I HAD ALREADY WRITTEN DOWN. ***\n\n**VERDICT (codex):** *preserve masked register state; validate `W_MASK` FIELDS; remove the\n`W_BASE` magic-value whitelists; leave TEN's two-state policy alone but correct `TB_ENA`'s\nreserved-bit guard.*  **\"This fixes register handling, not DMA translation\"** -- the scope\nsentence the round must carry.\n\n* *** `TB_ENA` IS NOT EXEMPT AFTER ALL, AND MY RECORD SAID IT WAS. ***  I recorded *\"IOC_TB_ENA is\n  NOT a functional defect at all -- TEN has exactly two states and both are accepted (0/2)\"*.  That\n  is right **only for normalised TEN values**: the arm's other bits are `RES`, type `X`, so the\n  existing whole-operand guard **still rejects writes the hardware ignores**.  Leaving the arm\n  untouched would contradict the very answer given to Q2.  *A per-field finding restated as a\n  per-arm exemption -- the dropped-qualifier class again.*\n* **ALL SIX REGISTERS ARE WRITE-ONLY, not RW.**  The manual makes reads of IOC write-only registers\n  UNPREDICTABLE, so **preserving readback is a useful existing emulator convention, not a hardware\n  requirement.**  A round that \"accepts with backing storage\" should say which of the two it is\n  doing.\n* **AND A PRECISION CORRECTION TO HOW I STATED THE RES RULE:** *\"`RES` alone does not mean ignored\n  -- the decisive evidence is each register table's type `X`, interpreted through the\n  conventions.\"*  My record leaned on \"RES fields are type X\" as one fact; it is two, and only the\n  second carries the ignored-on-write conclusion.\n* **`IOC_CONF` has four REPRESENTABLE field encodings**, and calling them four *supported PCI\n  operating modes* goes beyond the evidence -- the HRM also defers to the PCI specification's\n  configuration-cycle encoding restrictions.  So the \"2 of 4 rejected\" figure is a count of\n  representable encodings, not of legal modes.\n\n**Table 6-6's thirteen entries and the rest of the numerical premises are re-confirmed.**\n\n*** THE SHARP FINDING IS NOT THE OFFSET COUNT.  IT IS THAT TWO DOCUMENTED REGISTERS HAVE NO CASE,\nAND ONE OF THEM IS THE ERROR-STATUS REGISTER A DRIVER READS AFTER A FAILED PROBE.  2026-09-08. ***\n\nA codex ANALYSIS job (the construction-shaped brief is refused; see [[codexcyberflag]]) enumerated\nthe `lca_ioc' region and its stream ended before a verdict.  Its numbers were then VERIFIED here\nrather than taken:\n\n    memory_device_register(..., \"lca_ioc\", LCA_IOC_BASE, 0x20000000, ...)   512 MiB\n    switch (relative_addr + LCA_IOC_BASE)                    NO shift, NO mask before the switch\n    named cases: 10        0x20000000 - 10 = 536,870,902 byte offsets reach the shared `default:'\n\n**That count is arithmetically right and it is the WEAKER half of the finding.**  Most of those\noffsets are not registers at all, and the carrier's own rule says a domain is itself a premise: the\nAlpha alignment gate has not been analysed here, so the count of REACHABLE ACCESSES is smaller than\n536 million by an unmeasured factor.  It is recorded with its deriver and NOT as a severity claim.\n\n*** THE STRONGER HALF, from comparing the HEADER's register list against the switch's cases: ***\n\n    LCA_IOC_HAE        +0x000   HANDLED  (via `case LCA_IOC_BASE:' -- HAE and BASE are the same\n                                          address; see the correction below)\n    LCA_IOC_CONF       +0x020   HANDLED\n    LCA_IOC_STAT0      +0x040   *** NO CASE ***   Status 0\n    LCA_IOC_STAT1      +0x060   *** NO CASE ***   Status 1\n    LCA_IOC_TBIA       +0x080   HANDLED\n    LCA_IOC_TB_ENA     +0x0a0   HANDLED\n    LCA_IOC_W_BASE0/1  +0x100/0x120   HANDLED\n    LCA_IOC_W_MASK0/1  +0x140/0x160   HANDLED\n    LCA_IOC_W_T_BASE0/1 +0x180/0x1a0  HANDLED\n\n**TWO of the twelve documented registers have no case, and they are STAT0 and STAT1.**\n\n> *** AND STAT0 IS EXACTLY THE REGISTER THE 21066 MANUAL SAYS RECORDS A FAILED PCI ACCESS.\n> `_scratchpad/21066_66a_hrm.txt' S6.2.18 (PCI Master Abort): an unanswered read \"returns arbitrary\n> data with error status to the CPU, which causes an instruction exception\", plus an internal\n> interrupt and ERR/CODE/CMD/ADDR logging -- and that log is STAT0. ***\n>\n> **So the ordinary driver error-recovery sequence -- probe a PCI slot, take a master abort, then\n> read the status register to find out what happened -- reaches `fatal()' + `exit(1)'.**  That is\n> not an exotic value or an unusual address.  It is what a driver does when a probe fails.\n\n*** AND IT PUTS THIS ROW ON THE SAME GUEST CODE PATH AS `#515' / [[lcaconfbus1kill]], WHICH MATTERS\nFOR ORDERING RATHER THAN FOR SCOPE. ***  `#515` makes an absent-device config access return the\nempty-slot signature instead of terminating the host.  A driver that then consults STAT0 to check\nfor the error the manual says accompanies it lands here.  **The two rows are adjacent steps of one\ndriver sequence** -- which is an argument for scheduling this row next, and NOT an argument for\nfolding it into `#515` (different handler, different semantic class, different detector).\n\n*** A CORRECTION TO MY OWN CHECK, made before it could mislead: THE FIRST RUN REPORTED **THREE**\nUNHANDLED REGISTERS AND NAMED `LCA_IOC_HAE' AMONG THEM.  THAT IS WRONG. ***  `alpha_lcareg.h`\ndefines `LCA_IOC_HAE` as `LCA_IOC_BASE` -- the same address -- and the switch carries\n`case LCA_IOC_BASE:`, so HAE is handled under the other name.  **My script matched cases by NAME\nwhen the thing that decides handledness is the VALUE.**\n\n> That is this file's own deriver rule landing on me: *\"BEFORE WRITING A DERIVER, ASK WHAT MAKES A\n> MEMBER A MEMBER.  If the answer is a property the regex does not test, the deriver is measuring a\n> CORRELATE, and a correlate's error is invisible because the output is still a plausible number.\"*\n> A by-name census of a switch whose labels are aliases returns a plausible, wrong answer -- and it\n> inflated the finding by 50% in the direction that would have made the row look worse.\n\n**STILL OPEN and deliberately NOT answered here:** whether the two `default:` arms (read and write)\nare symmetric, what the write side does with `idata`, and what the manual says STAT0's reset value\nand semantics are.  The codex job was cut off before Q3-Q5 and its remaining questions stand.\n\n*** THE codex RE-FIRE LANDED WITH A VERDICT, AND IT REFUTED ONE OF MY OWN PREMISES. 2026-09-08. ***\n(The first firing could not resolve the manual -- a repo-relative path, when `_scratchpad/` sits one\nlevel ABOVE the git root -- and said so plainly instead of guessing.  A brief defect, not a seat\nfailure.  Re-fired with the absolute path.)\n\n**MY Q4 ASKED whether the READ and WRITE `default:` arms are symmetric.  codex: the question has no\nsubject.**\n\n    VERDICT: \"The claimed default-arm asymmetry is false: `lca_ioc' has ONE SHARED terminating\n             `default:'.\"\n\nCHECKED: `awk '/DEVICE_ACCESS\\(lca_ioc\\)/,/^}/' | grep -c 'default:'` = **1**.  I had assumed two\narms because the sibling handler `lca_pci_conf` splits by `writeflag`; this one does not.  *A brief\nthat asks about an asymmetry invites an answer about which side is broader, and the honest answer\nwas that there is one side.*\n\n*** THE COUNT, WITH THE ALIGNMENT QUALIFICATION I HAD NOT ASKED FOR AND WHICH THE CARRIER'S OWN\nDOMAIN RULE DEMANDS. ***\n\n    registration     0x180000000, length 0x20000000 = 536,870,912 bytes, ending 0x19fffffff\n    recognised       10 relative offsets: 0x000 (BASE, also HAE), 0x020, 0x080, 0x0a0,\n                     0x100, 0x120, 0x140, 0x160, 0x180, 0x1a0\n    reach default    536,870,902 byte offsets      (= 0x20000000 - 10)\n    QUADWORD-ALIGNED  67,108,854                   <- the honest figure for ordinary accesses\n\n**codex volunteered the alignment split rather than letting the big number stand**, noting the\nhandler adds the base *without shifting, masking, or checking alignment*, and that the permitted\nfile set could not establish what the dispatcher actually delivers.  It also cites the manual (S6.4)\nrequiring quadword IOC accesses -- so the 67 million figure is the one an ordinary driver can reach,\nand unaligned delivery is not needed to make the point.\n\n*** TEN ACTIVE `exit(1)` CALLS IN THIS ONE FILE, enumerated by codex and consistent with what this\nsession has separately measured: ***\n\n    2   the interrupt callbacks -- UNCONDITIONAL on entry, no predicate at all   -> [[lcairqstubkill]]\n    1   the `lca_isa' guard `ofs > len'\n    6   `lca_ioc' value guards\n    1   the shared `lca_ioc' default\n    ---\n    (plus the one `#515' / [[lcaconfbus1kill]] has just REMOVED from `lca_pci_conf')\n\n*** THE `lca_isa' GUARD IS REACHABLE, BUT ONLY BY A BYTE OR WORD ACCESS -- and codex's phrasing was\ncorrectly qualified where a careless reading of it would not be. ***  It said the manual's\n*\"ordinary longword and quadword accesses cannot satisfy `ofs > len'\"*.  Enumerated here rather than\ninferred, since `ofs = relative_addr & 3` is 0..3:\n\n    len >= 4 (longword, quadword)   ofs > len is UNSATISFIABLE          -- codex is right\n    len == 1 (byte)                 ofs = 2 or 3  ->  REACHABLE\n    len == 2 (word)                 ofs = 3       ->  REACHABLE\n\n**So it is not a dead guard; it is a guard no CONFORMING access reaches.**  Three (ofs, len) pairs\nreach it, all of them sub-longword.  *Worth stating precisely because \"the manual says accesses are\nquadword\" is an argument about what a good driver does, not about what the model will accept -- and\nthe model accepts a byte access and then terminates the host.*\n\n**SCOPE, so this row does not silently swallow its neighbours:** the interrupt callbacks are\n[[lcairqstubkill]] and are separately witnessed; the `lca_isa` guard is a DIFFERENT handler with a\ndifferent reachability story and should be its own row rather than folded in here.  This row remains\nthe six `lca_ioc` value guards plus the shared default."}
{"id": "lcaisalane", "sev": "high", "state": "in work", "title": "dev_lca.c DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_isa): THREE defects in eleven lines -- an off-by-one guard that lets a bus-hitting read return all zeros, a discarded downstream failure result, and an uninitialised byte consumed on that failure", "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07.  The lane-semantics half was raised by the codex seat (regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_scoping_20260907.txt) with an\nenumerated table; the third defect I found by reading the function while checking that table.\n\n    ofs = relative_addr & 3;                       <- guest address bits 5-6\n    if (ofs > len) { fatal(...); exit(1); }        <- `>' where `>=' was meant\n    ...\n    uint8_t byte;                                  <- never initialised\n    cpu->memory_rw(..., &byte, 1, MEM_READ, ...);  <- result DISCARDED\n    for (i = 0; i < len; i++)\n            data[i] = i == ofs ? byte : 0x00;\n\n**1. OFF-BY-ONE.**  The guard is `>' rather than `>=', so ofs == len passes.  codex's table, re-derived:\n\n    len=1  ofs {0,1} accepted      len=4  ofs {0,1,2,3} accepted\n    len=2  ofs {0,1,2} accepted    len=8  ofs {0,1,2,3} accepted\n\nFor (len,ofs) = (1,1) and (2,2) the loop never reaches i == ofs, so **the access performs a real downstream\nread and returns ALL ZEROS**.  The guest is told the device answered zero when that lane was never asked.\n\n**2. THE READ PATH DISCARDS THE FAILURE RESULT** that the WRITE path returns -- codex's observation, and the\nasymmetry is visible with the two branches side by side.\n\n**3. `byte' IS UNINITIALISED and is consumed if that discarded read failed.**  This is the FOURTH instance\nof the uninitialised-consumption family recorded on 2026-09-07, alongside [[sgirebufuninit]],\n[[sgigbeconvover]] and #95's incomplete class closure.  **#95 zeroes device READ buffers, so a\ndevice-served read is covered; a read that FAILS before reaching a handler is not** -- the same gap\nsgirebufuninit turns on.\n\nMED rather than HIGH: the wrong-lane read is a correctness defect with no host death and nothing disclosed\nbut a stale stack byte.  The exit(1) in the same function is [[lcabadval]]'s range site, graded there.\n\nNO WITNESS.  Reachability of the (1,1)/(2,2) pairs is established by reading; nothing shows a guest driving\nthem.\n\n*** codex ASSESS 2026-09-08.  I DELIBERATELY DID NOT TELL IT WHAT THE THREE DEFECTS WERE, and\nwarned it not to manufacture three.  It found SIX items and said the filed count is wrong. ***\n\n*\"Four behavioral issues, one conditional initialization gap, and one minor format mismatch.  **The\nfiled 'three' overstates the initialization claim.**\"*\n\n## *** THE TRAP: THE OBVIOUS ONE-CHARACTER FIX MAKES IT WORSE ***\n\n    dev_lca.c:193    if (ofs > len) {          <- equality PASSES\n\nOn equality the write selects byte zero via `ofs % len` and the read never places the downstream\nbyte anywhere -- **but the downstream read HAS OCCURRED and may have side effects.**  The guard\nshould be `ofs >= len`.\n\n**BUT the rejected-lane arm is `exit(1)` at :196, so changing only `>` to `>=` EXPANDS the\ntermination.**  *That is the same shape as `alphavmkill`, where deleting a kill would have published\na bogus 4 TB mapping: the boundary repair and the disposition repair are one design or neither.*\n\n**AND IT BOUNDED THE CLAIM RATHER THAN INFLATING IT:** *\"This is NOT a demonstrated buffer\noverrun\"* -- positive-length writes use modulo and the read loop stays within `len`; the modulo is\npart of the boundary behaviour, not an independent OOB.\n\n## THE IDIOM CENSUS -- five files, grepped rather than argued\n\n* **the same function's WRITE arm already returns the downstream result** -- the strongest precedent\n  for repairing its read arm;\n* both siblings (`lca_pci_conf`, `lca_ioc`) initialise `odata = 0`, and the config handler has a\n  non-terminating `dev = 0` fallback;\n* `eagle` and `footbridge_isa` initialise read results, and footbridge warns on unsupported writes\n  without exiting -- *but footbridge is not an equivalent sparse-lane mapper*;\n* `DEV_RAM_MIRROR` is the closest FORWARDING precedent: passes `PHYSICAL`, returns the nested result;\n* `wdc` -- ISA-connected -- already declines and latches for unsupported widths.\n* **no second implementation of LCA's sparse-lane packing exists in the fork.**\n\n## SIX ITEMS, each with disposition, bounded severity, and its observable\n\n    1 lane boundary        member 3   MED source-only   downstream access occurrence + payload\n                                                        (readback zero ALONE cannot distinguish)\n    2 process exit         member 3   MED pending witness -- and ALREADY RECORDED under lcabadval\n    3 lost read failure    member 7   LOW/documented    handler return status, writeback suppression\n    4 initialisation gap   member 7   LOW/documented    definedness analysis; normal readback is NOT\n                                                        an established channel\n    5 physical forwarding  member 7   MED conditional   which backing is accessed; bootstrap masks it\n    6 diagnostic format    member 7   LOW portability   no runtime text difference established\n\n**Member 5 FAULT is unavailable**, confirmed for the third time this session from a different file:\n`memory_rw.c`'s device-failure dispatcher has MIPS and M88K arms and **no Alpha arm**, and\n`ALPHA_KENTRY_MM` is defined and unused.\n\n## *** THE DUPLICATE I ASKED IT TO LOOK FOR -- AND THERE IS ONE ***\n\n*\"The original `lcabadval` scope explicitly EXCLUDES range-guard analysis, while its later note\nrecords this exact termination site.  **Extract or reassign that site explicitly rather than counting\nit twice.**\"*  `lcaconfbus1kill` and `lcairqstubkill` remain separate; their severity and witnesses\ndo not transfer here.  **This is an open obligation on both rows and is not discharged by recording\nit here.**\n\n## SCOPE: SEVERAL ROUNDS\n\nBoundary + termination are **one** design (same guard site).  Read completion is another; physical\nforwarding another.  *\"Sharing a function -- or a prospective test executable -- does not establish\none semantic class and one oracle.\"*\n\n## NO WITNESS EXISTS AND NONE IS CHEAP\n\n*\"I found no in-tree regression exercising the sparse `lca_isa` handler.\"*  The config probes cover\nother handlers, and `lca_irqstub_probe.py` **accesses the downstream ISA mapping directly, bypassing\nthis shim.**  So every item here is a documented source candidate until someone drives the shim.\n\n**FIX DESIGN IN (codex, 2026-09-10): next LCA round after #518 closes.  Witness first (len-1 access at ofs 2/3); implement per the design; rows per its Q3 table.**\n\n**WITNESSED 2026-09-10 (Opus): 13/16 on HEAD.  SEVERITY RAISED med -> high on the measured lane mapping: ofs == port & 3, so a byte read of the console UART's LSR (0x3fd) returns ZERO with a false success and a byte access to LCR/MSR/scratch ends the process -- every 8250 driver polls LSR.  Fix design ready (codex, 2026-09-10): NEXT LCA round.**"}
{"id": "alphavmkill", "sev": "med", "state": "open", "title": "memory_alpha.c: a guest virtual address of 0xfffffffd80000000 ends the host process -- fatal() + exit(1) on a specific vaddr, inside the not-yet-implemented VM path", "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07, found while checking [[alphapfnoffset]]'s reachability rather than by any seat.\n\n    if (vaddr == 0xfffffffd80000000ULL) {\n            fatal(\"Alpha kernel virtual memory seems to want to be used. Not yet implemented.\\n\");\n            exit(1);\n    }\n\n**A guest-chosen virtual address ends the emulator process.**  It sits inside the `ptbr == 0' bootstrap arm,\nso it is reached BEFORE virtual memory is configured -- i.e. early in a boot, on an address the kernel\nchooses, not one a user program supplies.\n\nIt is the same de-fatalisation class this project has worked through repeatedly in devices, arriving in a CPU\nmemory path where nobody has been looking.  **The disposition is NOT obvious, which is why this is filed\nrather than fixed:** declining leaves the guest with a wrong translation and it will fault or hang somewhere\nless legible, so this may be one of the rare sites where a loud stop is genuinely better than continuing --\nand if so the round's product is a RECORD saying so, plus a latch, not a removal.\n\nMED: reachable by construction, but only on a machine whose Alpha VM support is already declared incomplete\nseven TODOs over.\n\n*** CLUSTER SCOPING, codex, 2026-09-08 (ev: regress/artifacts/alphatbinegsel/codex_verify_20260908.txt).\nSIX SEPARATE ROUNDS -- no merger has sufficient evidence. ***\n\n    one fix design : replace the terminal path with the architecturally appropriate outcome\n    one oracle     : that outcome is observed for the affected address class\n\n**The ordering ruling matters more than the grouping:** codex put **`alphaswpctxreg` FIRST**, on the\nground that it is the only one with verified behaviour AND a decisive witness (a returned register\nvalue), and it labelled that *\"a confidence judgment, not a claim that it has the greatest potential\nseverity\"* -- which is the rung-versus-severity distinction the ladder already insists on, arrived at\nindependently.\n\n*** AND IT REFUTED A GROUPING HYPOTHESIS I HAD FLOATED: \"the claim that `alphapteprot` prevents all\nguest paging is not established by this review.\" ***  I had suggested the page-walk row might be a\nprerequisite for testing the others; that is not supported, and scheduling on it would have been\nscheduling on an assumption.  `alphafaultclaim` and `alphavmkill` may be reconsidered together only\nafter proving they share a file, one fault-handling repair and one fault-delivery oracle --\n*\"correcting a comment and preventing termination do not establish that grouping.\"*\n\n*** grok ASSESS 2026-09-08 -- the sixth and last Alpha row.  THE CLUSTER IS NOW FULLY ASSESSED. ***\n\n**THE CLAIM IS TRUE BUT NARROWER THAN FILED: it holds for kernel mode with PTBR == 0 on that exact\nVA.**  There are TWO hits on `0xfffffffd80000000` in `memory_alpha.c` and **only one terminates**;\nthe other is a bare `fatal(\"AYONA3\n\")` -- a debug string left in the tree.  `fatal()` only prints;\nthe termination is the `exit(1)` immediately after, with nothing recovering in between.\n\n## THE CENSUS I ASKED FOR, and it changes the framing\n\n| walker | unmodelled / stub region | ordinary translation miss |\n|---|---|---|\n| Alpha | this one VA: `fatal`+`exit(1)`; other PTBR==0 VAs **ACCEPT** identity | `fatal` + **`return 0`**, no guest exception (`#194` removed the abort here) |\n| MIPS | -- | `mips_cpu_exception`, `return 0` |\n| ARM | unimplemented descriptor type: `fatal`+`exit(1)` | `arm_exception`, `return 0` |\n| PPC | 64-bit translation: `fatal`+`exit(1)` | `ppc_exception`, `return 0` |\n| M88K | internal inconsistency: `exit(1)` | `m88k_exception`, `return 0` |\n| SH | unimplemented kernel VA: `fatal`+`exit(1)` (**honours FLAG_NOEXCEPTIONS**) | `sh_exception`, `return 0` |\n\n**The kill is an outlier in BOTH in-file directions** -- it is neither the PTBR==0 siblings' ACCEPT\nnor the walk's own `return 0`, which this project already chose over host death in `#194`.\n\n## *** ACCEPT IS REFUTED ON A PREMISE, AND THIS IS THE SHARPEST PART OF THE ANSWER ***\n\nDeleting the `exit(1)` looks like member 4, because `*return_paddr` is already computed above it.\n**But that value is the comment's own \"UGLY hack\": a K0SEG mask applied to a K1SEG address**, giving\na physical address near 4 TB against a default 128 MB of RAM.  Data then takes the unmapped-physical\npath -- **zeros returned, writes dropped, and the access reported SUCCESSFUL.**\n\n> *\"That is the silent bogus map the landmine exists to refuse.\"*\n\n**So a bare de-fatalisation here would be WORSE than the kill it removes**, which is the opposite of\nthis queue's usual shape and is exactly the kind of thing the option-set rule exists to surface.\n\n## DISPOSITION: member 3, DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE, `return 0`\n\n* **5 FAULT is architecturally right and NOT AVAILABLE.**  Choosing it obliges building delivery\n  first: a PAL frame, `kentry[ALPHA_KENTRY_MM]`, MMCSR/VA, and teaching `pc_to_pointers` about a\n  redirected PC.  **That is a new IMPLEMENT round, not a de-fatalisation.**  *My brief's caution\n  that FAULT may be unavailable on Alpha is confirmed, with the bill itemised.*\n* **7 IMPLEMENT does not apply** -- its tell is \"no predicate, whole path stubbed\"; this is a\n  one-address predicate, and K1SEG translation IS the walk once PTBR is set.\n* **2 silent DECLINE drops the only signal the author planted.**\n\n## SCOPE: STILL SIX SEPARATE ROUNDS\n\nI asked whether the shared missing MM-dispatch now meets codex's bar for merging this with\n`alphafaultclaim`.  **It does not:** *\"missing MM-dispatch is a CARRIER, not a shared repair.\"*  A\ncommon blocker is not one fix design and one oracle.\n\n## SEVERITY: **med**, explicitly BELOW `alphatbinegsel`\n\nRaised from unset to `med` on grok's argument: this needs a guest already executing privileged code\nwith PTBR == 0 at one specific address, whereas `alphatbinegsel` is a documented primitive silently\nunder-flushing during ordinary operation.  *The ranking is the seat's and I am adopting it rather\nthan substituting my own.*"}
{"id": "lcaiocstat0", "sev": "med", "state": "open", "title": "dev_lca.c DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_ioc) has no arm for four DOCUMENTED IOC registers -- STAT0 +0x040, STAT1 +0x060, SFT_RST +0x0c0, PAR_DIS +0x0e0 (HRM :10461, :10496) -- and S6.4.14 items 1-2 REQUIRE SROM to write SFT_RST and STAT0.  Before #518 those writes ended the process in default:; after #518 they survive as a once-announced declined write and a later read answers zero, not the written bits.  A strict improvement and still not the register: option-set member 7, IMPLEMENT (SFT_RST drives PCI rst_l; STAT0/STAT1 are the error log chapter 6 writes).  alpha_lcareg.h defines STAT0/STAT1 with field masks and has no SFT_RST.  Rung-3 witness constructible from lca_badval_probe.py's session shape.  *** REVISED 2026-09-10 by the codex design reading: STAT1 is RO with no W1C field; STAT0 is RO status plus W1C ERR/LOST only (mask 0x30); SFT_RST is a persistent WO reset control (RST=1 after reset, released by a written zero); PAR_DIS is WO.  Member 7 for STAT0+STAT1 (coupled), member 3 with a persistent bit for SFT_RST/PAR_DIS until PCI reset and parity exist; a uniform member-6 echo would be WRONG.  bus_pci has no reset entry point. ***"}

